hadiths[99]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1","[AI-translation] On the authority of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: «Actions are but by intentions, and every person shall have only what they intended. Whoever's migration was to Allah and His Messenger, his migration is to Allah and His Messenger. And whoever's migration was for some worldly gain to attain, or a woman to marry, his migration is to that which he migrated for.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim)."
"2","[AI-translation] On the authority of Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whoever introduces into this matter of ours something that is not of it» — and in a (variant) narration: «Whoever does a deed that is not in accordance with our matter — it is rejected.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
These two great hadiths encompass the whole of the religion, its principles and branches, its outward and inward aspects. The hadith of Umar is the scale for inward actions, and the hadith of Aisha is the scale for outward actions. They contain sincerity toward the One worshipped and adherence to the Messenger — the two conditions for every word and deed, outward and inward. Whoever purifies his deeds for Allah while following the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein, his deed is accepted; and whoever lacks both conditions or one of them, his deed is rejected, falling under the words of Allah Almighty: «And We shall turn to whatever deeds they did, and make them as scattered dust» (Qur'an Al-Furqan 25:23). And whoever combines both qualities falls under His Almighty words: «And who is better in religion than one who submits his face to Allah while doing good» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:125), and: «Rather, whoever submits his face to Allah while doing good shall have his reward with his Lord; no fear shall come upon them, nor shall they grieve» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:112). As for intention: it is the resolve to do the deed in order to draw near to Allah, seeking His pleasure and reward. Included in this is the intention for the deed itself and the intention toward the One for whom the deed is done. As for the intention for the deed: no purification in any of its forms is valid, nor prayer, zakah, fasting, Hajj, nor any act of worship, except by intending it — specifying that particular act of worship in one's intention. When an act of worship comprises kinds and types, such as prayer — including the obligatory, the specified voluntary, and the absolute voluntary — for the absolute voluntary it suffices to intend prayer in general. As for a specified prayer, whether obligatory or a specified voluntary one such as the witr or the regular sunnah prayers, one must, along with intending prayer, intend that specific prayer. The same applies to the rest of the acts of worship. One must also distinguish (mere) habit from worship. For example, washing (ghusl) may be done for cleanliness or to cool down, or it may be done for major ritual impurity, or the washing of the deceased, or for Friday (prayer), and the like, so one must intend thereby the removal of ritual impurity or that recommended washing. Likewise, a person may pay out money, for example, as zakah, or as expiation, or as a vow, or as recommended charity, or as a gift — the decisive factor in all of that is the intention. Of the same kind are the stratagems in transactions: when a person carries out a transaction whose outward form and appearance is valid, but by it he intends to arrive at a usurious transaction, or intends to evade an obligation, or to reach something forbidden — the consideration is his intention and purpose, not the outward wording, for actions are but by intentions. That is (done) by joining to one of the counter-values something not (really) intended, or by joining to the contract another contract not (really) intended — as Shaykh al-Islam said. Likewise Allah has made it a condition in (the husband's) taking back (his divorced wife) and in bequests that the servant should not intend harm therein. Included in this are all the means used to reach their ends, for means take the rulings of their ends, whether righteous or corrupt — and Allah knows the one who makes mischief from the one who makes good. As for the intention toward the One for whom the deed is done: it is sincerity to Allah in all that the servant does and refrains from, and in all that he says and does. He Almighty said: «And they were commanded only to worship Allah, sincere to Him in religion» (Qur'an Al-Bayyinah 98:5). And He said: «Surely, sincere religion belongs to Allah» (Qur'an Az-Zumar 39:3). That is because the servant must hold a comprehensive general intention covering all his affairs, aimed at the Face of Allah, drawing near to Him, seeking His reward, reckoning his recompense, and fearing His punishment. Then he maintains this intention in each individual of his deeds and words, and in all his states, keen therein to realize sincerity and perfect it, and to repel all that contradicts it — showing off, seeking repute, aiming at praise among people, hoping for their veneration. Rather, if anything of that occurs, the servant should not make it his aim and ultimate goal; his original aim should be the Face of Allah and seeking His reward, without regard to people and without hope for their benefit or praise. If something of that occurs without the servant's intending it, it does not harm him at all — indeed it may be part of the advance glad tidings of the believer. His (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying «Actions are but by intentions» means: they do not come about and do not exist except with intention, and their pivot is intention. Then he said: «And every person shall have only what they intended» — i.e., they come to be according to the servant's intention: their soundness or corruption, perfection or deficiency. Whoever intends a good deed and aims thereby at the sublime goals — that which draws near to Allah — his is the complete, most ample reward and recompense. Whoever's intention and aim are deficient, his reward is deficient; whoever's intention is directed to other than this glorious goal misses the good and gets the base, deficient goals he intended. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck an example by which all matters are measured, saying: «Whoever's migration was to Allah and His Messenger, his migration is to Allah and His Messenger» — i.e., he attains what he intended, and his reward falls upon Allah — «and whoever's migration was for some worldly gain to attain, or a woman to marry, his migration is to that which he migrated for.» He singled out the woman one marries after having covered all worldly matters, to show that all of that is base aims and unprofitable goals. Likewise when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the man who fights out of bravery, or out of partisanship, or so that his standing be seen in the battle line — which of that is in Allah's path? — he said: «Whoever fights so that the word of Allah is supreme is in Allah's path.» And Allah Almighty spoke of the variation of spending according to intentions: «The parable of those who spend their wealth seeking Allah's pleasure and out of their own certainty is as the parable of a garden on a height» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:265). And He said: «And those who spend their wealth to show off to people and believe neither in Allah nor in the Last Day» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:38). And so with all deeds. Deeds differ in merit and their reward is magnified according to the faith and sincerity in the doer's heart — so much so that the holder of a truthful intention, especially when joined with what he is able of deed, catches up with the doer. He Almighty said: «Whoever leaves his house emigrating to Allah and His Messenger, then death overtakes him — his reward has fallen upon Allah» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:100). In the Sahih, raised to the Prophet: «When the servant falls ill or travels, there is written for him what he used to do, sound and resident»; «In Madinah there are people: you travelled no path nor crossed a valley but they were with you — i.e., in their intentions, hearts, and reward — excuse detained them.» When the servant aims at good and then is not able to do it, his resolve and intention are written for him as a complete good deed. Kindness to people with wealth, word, and deed is good, with reward and recompense from Allah — but its reward is magnified by intention. He Almighty said: «No good is there in much of their private counsel except for one who enjoins charity, or good, or reconciliation between people» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:114) — i.e., that is good; then He said: «And whoever does that seeking Allah's pleasure, We shall grant him a mighty reward» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:114) — making the mighty reward follow upon doing that seeking His pleasure. In Al-Bukhari, raised to the Prophet: «Whoever takes people's wealth intending to repay it, Allah repays on his behalf; and whoever takes it intending to destroy it, Allah destroys him.» Consider how He made the righteous intention a strong cause for provision and for Allah's repaying on one's behalf, and made the evil intention a cause of ruin and destruction. Likewise intention runs through permissible things and worldly matters: whoever intends by his earning and his worldly and customary activities to seek help thereby in upholding Allah's right and discharging obligations and recommended acts, and maintains this righteous intention in his eating, drinking, sleep, rest, and earnings — his habits turn into worship, Allah blesses the servant in his deeds, and opens for him doors of good and provision that he never reckons and never cross his mind. Whoever misses this righteous intention through ignorance or negligence has none to blame but himself. In the Sahih, from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: «You never do a deed seeking thereby the Face of Allah but you are rewarded for it — even what you place in your wife's mouth.» Thus it is known: this hadith gathers all matters of good. It behooves the believer who seeks his soul's salvation and benefit to understand the meaning of this hadith and to keep acting by it before his eyes in all his states and times. As for the hadith of Aisha: his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying «Whoever introduces into this matter of ours what is not of it, it is rejected» — or «Whoever does a deed that is not in accordance with our matter, it is rejected» — indicates by its expressed meaning and by its implied meaning. As for its expressed meaning: it indicates that every innovation introduced into the religion that has no basis in the Book or the Sunnah — whether of the doctrinal verbal innovations, such as tajahhum (Jahmism), rafd (rejectionism), i'tizal (Mu'tazilism), and others, or of the practical innovations, such as worshipping Allah with acts of worship that Allah and His Messenger have not legislated — all of that is rejected against its authors, and its people are blameworthy according to their innovations and their distance from the religion. Whoever reports other than what Allah and His Messenger reported, or worships (Allah) with something Allah and His Messenger neither permitted nor legislated, is an innovator; whoever forbids permissible things or worships with unlegislated forms is an innovator. As for the implied meaning of this hadith: whoever does a deed that accords with the command of Allah and His Messenger — which is worshipping Allah with sound doctrines and righteous deeds, obligatory and recommended — his deed is accepted and his striving thanked. From this hadith it is inferred that every act of worship performed in a prohibited manner is corrupt, because the Lawgiver's command does not cover it, and that prohibition entails corruption; and that every transaction the Lawgiver has prohibited is void and given no regard."
"3","[AI-translation] On the authority of Tamim ad-Dari (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The religion is sincere counsel; the religion is sincere counsel; the religion is sincere counsel. They said: To whom, O Messenger of Allah? He said: To Allah, to His Book, to His Messenger, and to the leaders of the Muslims and their common folk.» Reported by Muslim.
The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated this phrase out of concern for the matter, and to guide the community to know with certain knowledge that the whole religion — its outward and inward — is summed up in sincere counsel, which is the complete fulfillment of these five rights. Sincere counsel to Allah is: affirming the oneness of Allah and His singularity in the attributes of perfection in a way in which no partner shares with Him in any respect; carrying out servitude to Him outwardly and inwardly; turning to Him at all times in worship and in seeking (from Him) with hope and fear, along with constant repentance and seeking forgiveness — for the servant must inevitably fall short in some of Allah's obligations or venture into some forbidden things, and by constant repentance and continual seeking of forgiveness his deficiency is mended and his word and deed perfected. As for sincere counsel to the Book of Allah: it is by memorizing it, reflecting on it, learning its words and meanings, and striving to act by it oneself and with others. As for sincere counsel to the Messenger: it is belief in him and love of him, preferring him over one's self, wealth, and children, following him in the principles and branches of the religion, giving precedence to his word over the word of anyone else, striving to be guided by his guidance, and aiding his religion. As for sincere counsel to the leaders of the Muslims — who are their rulers, from the supreme imam to governors and judges to everyone holding general or specific authority — it is by believing in their authority, hearing and obeying them, urging people to that, exerting what one can in guiding them and alerting them to everything that benefits them and benefits the people, and to discharging their duty. As for sincere counsel to the common Muslims: it is to love for them what one loves for oneself and dislike for them what one dislikes for oneself, and to strive in that as far as possible — for whoever loves a thing strives for it and exerts himself to realize and complete it. Thus the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained sincere counsel by these five matters, which encompass fulfilling the rights of Allah, the rights of His Book, the rights of His Messenger, and the rights of all Muslims in their varying conditions and ranks. This comprises the whole religion, leaving nothing that is not included in this comprehensive, all-encompassing speech. And Allah knows best."
"4","[AI-translation] Narrated Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: «A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Guide me to a deed which, if I do it, I shall enter Paradise. He said: You worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, establish the prescribed prayer, pay the obligatory zakah, and fast Ramadan. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I shall add nothing to this nor take away from it. When he turned away, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased to look at a man of the people of Paradise, let him look at this man.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
Many hadiths have been reported concerning this great principle indicated by the hadith, and their import is all in agreement or close: that whoever discharges what Allah has made obligatory upon him — both the obligations shared (by all) and those obligations attached to specific causes that become obligatory upon whoever meets their conditions — whoever discharges the obligations and avoids the forbidden things deserves entry into Paradise and salvation from the Fire. Whoever is characterized by this description deserves the name of Islam and faith, and becomes one of the God-fearing, the successful, and those who travel the straight path. Similar and close to this is the hadith: «Say: I believe in Allah, then be steadfast.»"
"5","[AI-translation] On the authority of Sufyan ibn Abdullah ath-Thaqafi, who said: I said: «O Messenger of Allah, tell me a saying about Islam after which I shall ask no one but you. He said: Say: I believe in Allah, then be steadfast.» Reported by Muslim.
This man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a comprehensive, beneficial statement that gathers all good and brings its holder to success. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to believe in Allah — which includes what must be believed: the doctrines of faith and its principles, and what follows from that, of the deeds of the heart, and submission and surrender to Allah inwardly and outwardly — then to remain constant in that and steadfast upon it until death. It is like His Almighty words: «Indeed, those who say: Our Lord is Allah, then remain steadfast — the angels descend upon them: Do not fear and do not grieve, but receive glad tidings of Paradise which you were promised» (Qur'an Fussilat 41:30). He made safety from all evils and the attainment of Paradise and all beloved things follow from faith and steadfastness. Many texts of the Book and the Sunnah indicate that faith includes what is in the hearts of sound doctrines, and the deeds of the heart — such as desire for good, dread of evil, willing good, and hating evil — and the deeds of the limbs. And that is not complete except by firmness upon it."
"6","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The Muslim is one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe, and the emigrant is one who abandons what Allah has forbidden.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim); At-Tirmidhi and An-Nasa'i added: «And the believer is one whom people trust with their blood and their wealth.» Al-Bayhaqi added: «And the mujahid is one who strives against his soul in obedience to Allah.»
He mentioned in this hadith the perfection of these glorious names upon which Allah and His Messenger made the happiness of this world and the Hereafter depend: Islam, faith, emigration, and jihad. And he stated their definitions in comprehensive, all-encompassing speech: that the Muslim is one from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe. That is because true Islam is submission to Allah, perfecting servitude to Him, and upholding His rights and the rights of the Muslims. Islam is not complete until one loves for the Muslims what one loves for oneself, and that is not realized except by their being safe from the evil of his tongue and the evil of his hand. For this is the basis of this obligation he owes the Muslims. Whoever's tongue or hand the Muslims are not safe from — how can he be upholding the obligation he owes his Muslim brothers? Their safety from his verbal and physical harm is the mark of the perfection of his Islam. And he defined the believer as the one whom people trust with their blood and their wealth: for when faith circulates in the heart and fills it, it necessitates for its holder upholding the rights of faith, among the most important of which are guarding trusts, truthfulness in dealings, and scrupulousness against wronging people in their blood and their wealth. Whoever is so, people recognize this in him, trust him with their blood and their wealth, and place confidence in him, because of what they know in him of guarding trusts; for guarding trust is among the most particular obligations of faith, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «There is no faith for one who has no trustworthiness.» And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) defined the emigration which is an individual obligation upon every Muslim as emigrating from sins and acts of disobedience. This obligation does not fall from any accountable person in any of his states; for Allah forbade His servants violating prohibitions and venturing into disobedience. The specific emigration — moving from a land of disbelief or innovation to a land of Islam and the Sunnah — is part of this emigration, and it is not obligatory upon everyone; it becomes obligatory only with the existence of its known causes. And he defined the mujahid as one who strives against his soul in obedience to Allah: for the soul inclines to laziness toward good deeds, commands evil, is quickly affected at calamities, and needs patience and striving in binding it to obedience to Allah, keeping it firm upon it, striving against it away from disobedience to Allah, restraining it from it, and striving with it to patience at calamities. These are the acts of obedience: complying with commands, avoiding prohibitions, and patience with decrees. The true mujahid is one who strives against it in these matters that it may uphold its duty and function. Among the noblest and most glorious of this kind is striving against it in fighting enemies, and striving against them by word and deed; for jihad in Allah's path is the peak of the hump of the religion. Whoever upholds what this hadith indicates has upheld the whole religion: whoever's tongue and hand the Muslims are safe from, whom people trust with their blood and their wealth, who abandons what Allah forbade, and who strives against his soul in obedience to Allah — there remains nothing of religious and worldly good, outward and inward, but he has done it, and nothing of evil but he has abandoned it. And Allah alone is the granter of success."
"7","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «There are four (traits); whoever has them in him is a sheer hypocrite, and whoever has one of them in him has in him a trait of hypocrisy until he abandons it: when he is trusted he betrays, when he speaks he lies, when he covenants he acts treacherously, and when he disputes he behaves wickedly.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
Hypocrisy is the basis of evil: it is to display good while concealing evil. This definition includes the greater hypocrisy of belief, in which its holder displays Islam while concealing disbelief; this kind takes one out of the religion entirely, and its holder is in the lowest depth of the Fire. Allah has described these hypocrites with all the attributes of evil: disbelief and lack of faith, mocking the religion and its people, ridiculing them, and inclining wholly toward the enemies of the religion, sharing with them in enmity toward the religion of Islam. They exist in every age, especially in this age in which materialism, atheism, and permissiveness have overrun. What is meant here is the second category of hypocrisy mentioned in this hadith: this practical hypocrisy — though it does not take one out of the religion entirely — is the corridor to disbelief. Whoever has these four traits combined in him has combined evil in himself, and the descriptions of the hypocrites are purely realized in him. For truthfulness, discharging trusts, fulfilling covenants, and scrupulousness regarding the rights of others are the sum of good and among the most distinctive traits of the believers. Whoever lacks one of them has demolished one of the obligations of Islam and faith — so what of all of them together? Lying in speech includes speaking about Allah and speaking about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — whoever deliberately lies about him, let him take his seat in the Fire — «And who is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allah» (Qur'an As-Saff 61:7); it includes speaking of events reported, general and particular. Whoever's habit is this has shared with the hypocrites in their most distinctive trait, which is lying, of which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Beware of lying, for lying guides to wickedness, and wickedness guides to the Fire; a man keeps lying and pursuing lying until he is recorded with Allah as a liar.» And whoever, when trusted with wealth, rights, and secrets, betrays them and does not uphold his trust — where is his faith? And where is the reality of his Islam? Likewise whoever breaks the covenants between him and Allah and the covenants between him and creatures is described by a vile trait of the hypocrites' traits. Likewise whoever does not scruple over people's wealth and rights, seizes opportunities for them, and disputes over them falsely to establish a falsehood or repel a right. These traits hardly combine in a person while he has with him of faith what suffices or avails; for they contradict faith in the severest contradiction. And know that among the principles of Ahl as-Sunnah wa al-Jama'ah is that there may combine in a servant traits of good and traits of evil, traits of faith and traits of disbelief or hypocrisy; and he deserves of reward and punishment according to what stands in him of what necessitates that. Many texts of the Book and the Sunnah have indicated this principle, so all the texts must be acted upon and all of them believed. And we must disown the doctrine of the Kharijites, who reject what the texts brought: of the remaining of faith and the remaining of religion, even if a person commits of disobedience what he commits, so long as he does nothing of the nullifiers that take their doer out of faith. For the Kharijites reject all that and deem whoever commits any of the major sins or any of the traits of disbelief or traits of hypocrisy as out of the religion, abiding eternally in the Fire. And this is a false doctrine by the Book, the Sunnah, and the consensus of the predecessors of the community."
"8","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Satan comes to one of you and says: Who created such-and-such? Who created such-and-such? — until he says: Who created Allah? When it reaches that, let him seek refuge with Allah and stop.» And in a wording: «Let him say: I believe in Allah and His messengers.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
And in a wording: «People will keep questioning one another until they say: Who created Allah?» This hadith contains (notice) that Satan must inevitably cast this false objection — either as pure whispering, or upon the tongues of the devils among men and their atheists. And it has occurred as he foretold, for both have occurred: Satan still drives this false question into the hearts of those without insight, and the people of atheism still cast this doubt, which is the falsest of doubts, speaking about causes and about the matter of the universe with well-known foolish talk. In this great hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided to repelling this question with three things: stopping, seeking refuge from Satan, and faith. As for stopping — the first matter: Allah Almighty has set for thoughts and minds a limit at which they stop and which they do not pass; it is impossible, were they to try to pass it, that they could — for it is impossible, and attempting the impossible is false and foolish. Of the most impossible of impossibilities is an infinite regress of causes and agents: created beings have a beginning and an end, though they may form long chains in many of their affairs until they end at Allah, who brought them into being and brought into being what is in them of qualities, substances, and elements: «And to your Lord is the final end» (Qur'an An-Najm 53:42). When minds reach Allah Almighty they halt and stop, for He is the First, before whom there is nothing, and the Last, after whom there is nothing. His firstness has no beginning, whatever times and states may be supposed, and He is the one who brought into being times, states, and minds, which are but one of man's faculties. So how should the mind attempt to cling to raising the false question? The duty incumbent upon it in this situation is to halt and stop. The second matter: seeking refuge with Allah from Satan, for this is of his whisperings and his casting into hearts, to shake people's faith in their Lord. When the servant finds that, he should seek refuge with Allah from him; whoever seeks refuge with Allah truthfully and firmly, Allah grants him refuge, drives Satan away from him, and his false whisperings vanish. The third matter: repelling it with its opposite — faith in Allah and His messengers — for Allah and His messengers have told that He Almighty is the First, before whom there is nothing, and that He Almighty is singular in oneness and in creating and bringing into being, of existents past and to come. This true, sincere, certain faith repels all that contradicts it of doubts opposed to it, for truth repels falsehood, and doubts do not stand against certainty. These three things mentioned by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nullify these doubts which the atheists never cease to utter, casting them in varied phrases. He commanded stopping, which nullifies the false infinite regress; seeking refuge from Satan, who is the caster of this doubt; and sound faith, which repels everything opposed to it of falsehood — and praise be to Allah. (By stopping): evil is cut off directly; (by seeking refuge): the cause inviting to evil is cut off; (and by faith): refuge and holding fast to the true certain belief which repels every opponent. These three things together are all the causes that repel every doubt opposing faith. So they should be cared for in everything that confronts faith by way of doubt and confusion: the servant repels it directly with the proofs showing its falsity and establishing its opposite — which is the truth, after which there is nothing but error — and by seeking refuge with Allah from Satan, who drives into hearts the trials of doubts and the trials of desires, to shake their faith and make them fall into kinds of disobedience. So through patience and certainty the servant attains safety from the trials of desires and from the trials of doubts. And Allah is the granter of success, the preserver."
"9","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Everything is by decree, even incapacity and shrewdness.» Reported by Muslim.
This hadith contains a great principle among the six principles of faith: belief in the decree, its good and evil, sweet and bitter, general and particular, past and to come — that the servant should acknowledge that Allah's knowledge encompasses everything, that He knew the deeds of servants, good and evil, and knew all their affairs and states, and wrote that in the Preserved Tablet, as He Almighty said: «Do you not know that Allah knows what is in the heaven and the earth? Indeed that is in a Book; indeed that is easy for Allah» (Qur'an Al-Hajj 22:70). Then Allah carries out these decrees in their times according to what His wisdom and will require, which embrace all that was and shall be, embracing creation and command. And along with that — along with His creating the servants, their deeds, and their qualities — He has given them a power and will by which their deeds occur according to their choice; He has not compelled them. He is the one who created their power and will; the Creator of the complete cause is the Creator of the effect. Their deeds and words occur by their power and will, which Allah created in them, as He created the rest of their outward and inward faculties. But He Almighty facilitates each for that for which he was created. Whoever directs his face and purpose to his Lord: He makes faith beloved to him and beautifies it in his heart, makes disbelief, wickedness, and disobedience hateful to him, and makes him of the rightly guided — so Allah's blessings upon him are complete in every way. And whoever directs his face to other than Allah, taking his enemy Satan as patron: He does not facilitate these matters for him; rather Allah leaves him to what he chose, forsakes him, and entrusts him to himself — so he strays and errs, and he has no plea against his Lord, for Allah gave him all the means by which he could attain guidance, but he chose error over guidance, so let him blame none but himself. He Almighty said: «One party He guided, and one party deserved error: they took the devils as patrons instead of Allah» (Qur'an Al-A'raf 7:30). And He said: «By it Allah guides whoever follows His pleasure to the paths of peace, and brings them out of darkness into light by His permission, and guides them to a straight path» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 5:16). This decree extends over all the servant's states, deeds, and qualities — even incapacity and shrewdness. These two opposite qualities: by the first — incapacity — are attained disappointment and loss, and by the second — shrewdness — earnestness in obeying the Most Merciful. What is meant here is the blameworthy incapacity: lack of resolve, which is laziness — not incapacity as lack of ability. This is the meaning of the other hadith: «Act, for each is facilitated to that for which he was created.» As for the people of felicity: they are facilitated to the deeds of felicity, by their shrewdness, their being granted success, and Allah's kindness to them. And the shrewd and the incapable are those mentioned in his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: «The shrewd one is he who calls himself to account and works for what is after death, and the incapable one is he who makes himself follow its desires and entertains wishful hopes about Allah.»"
"10","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whoever calls to guidance shall have of the reward the like of the rewards of those who follow him, without that decreasing anything from their rewards. And whoever calls to error shall bear of the sin the like of the sins of those who follow him, without that decreasing anything from their sins.» Reported by Muslim.
This hadith — and others like it — contains: encouragement to call to guidance and good and the merit of the caller; warning against calling to error and deviance and the enormity of the caller's crime and punishment. Guidance is beneficial knowledge and righteous deed. Whoever teaches knowledge or directs learners to follow a path by which they gain knowledge is a caller to guidance. Whoever calls to a righteous deed connected with the right of Allah or the general and particular rights of people is a caller to guidance. Whoever offers religious or worldly counsel used as a means to (good in) religion is a caller to guidance. Whoever is rightly guided in his knowledge or deed and others follow his example is a caller to guidance. Whoever precedes others in a charitable deed or a project of general benefit falls under this text. And the opposite of all that is the caller to error. The callers to guidance are the leaders of the God-fearing and the best of the believers; the callers to error are the leaders who call to the Fire. Whoever helps another in righteousness and God-consciousness is of the callers to guidance; whoever aids another in sin and aggression is of the callers to error."
"11","[AI-translation] On the authority of Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whomever Allah wills good for, He gives him understanding of the religion.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith is among the greatest (texts on) the merits of knowledge. It contains: beneficial knowledge is a sign of the servant's felicity, and that Allah has willed good for him. Understanding of the religion includes understanding of the principles of faith, the laws of Islam and its rulings, and the realities of excellence. For the religion comprises all three, as in the hadith of Gabriel when he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about faith, Islam, and excellence, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him with their definitions: he explained faith by its six pillars, explained Islam by its five pillars, and explained excellence by: «That you worship Allah as though you see Him; for if you do not see Him, He sees you.» Included in that is understanding of doctrines, knowing the school of the early predecessors therein, realizing it outwardly and inwardly, knowing the schools of the dissenters, and showing their opposition to the Book and the Sunnah. Included in that is the science of jurisprudence: its principles and branches — the rulings of worship, transactions, crimes, and others. Included in that is understanding the realities of faith and knowing the journey and wayfaring to Allah in conformity with what the Book and the Sunnah indicate. Likewise included in this is learning all the means that aid understanding of the religion, such as the sciences of Arabic in all their kinds. Whomever Allah wills good for, He gives him understanding of these matters and enables him for them. The implied meaning of the hadith shows that whoever turns away from these sciences entirely, Allah has not willed good for him, by depriving him of the means by which good things are attained and felicity earned."
"12","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The strong believer is better and more beloved to Allah than the weak believer, and in each there is good. Be keen on what benefits you, seek help from Allah, and do not give in to incapacity. If something befalls you, do not say: If only I had done such-and-such, it would have been such-and-such; but say: Allah decreed, and what He willed He did; for ""if"" opens the work of Satan.» Reported by Muslim.
This hadith comprises great principles and comprehensive words. Among them: affirmation of love as an attribute of Allah, and that it attaches to the things He loves and to those who uphold them; it indicates that it is connected to His will and wish, and that it also admits of degrees. So His love for the strong believer is greater than His love for the weak believer. The hadith indicates that faith encompasses the beliefs of the heart, words, and deeds, as is the doctrine of Ahl as-Sunnah wa al-Jama'ah; for faith has seventy-odd branches, the highest of which is the saying: (There is no god but Allah), and the lowest of which is removing harm from the road, and modesty is a branch of it. These branches, which go back to inward and outward deeds, are all part of faith. Whoever truly fulfills them, perfects himself with beneficial knowledge and righteous deeds, and perfects others by enjoining one another to truth and enjoining one another to patience: he is the strong believer who has attained the highest ranks of faith; and whoever does not reach this rank is the weak believer. This is among the proofs used by the righteous predecessors that faith increases and decreases, according to one's sciences and knowledge of faith and according to one's deeds. This principle is indicated by the Book and the Sunnah in many places. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew a distinction between the believers, strong and weak, he feared lest it be imagined as disparagement of the less excellent, so he said: «and in each there is good.» In this precaution there is a precious benefit, namely that whoever draws distinctions between persons, kinds, or deeds should mention the aspect of the preference and the respect in which it holds, and should guard by mentioning the merit shared between the superior and the inferior, lest disparagement attach to the inferior. Likewise on the other side, when the ranks of evil and of evildoers are mentioned, and the disparity between them, one should then mention the common measure they share of the causes of good or evil. This is frequent in the Book and the Sunnah. In this hadith: the believers differ in goodness, in Allah's love, and in upholding His religion, and in that they are in degrees: «And for all there are degrees for what they did» (Qur'an Al-Ahqaf 46:19). They are gathered in three divisions: the forerunners in good deeds, who upheld the obligations and recommended acts, abandoned the forbidden, the disliked, and superfluous permissible things, perfected the deeds they undertook, and were characterized by all attributes of perfection; then the moderate, who confined themselves to upholding the obligations and abandoning the prohibited; then the wrongdoers against themselves, who mixed a righteous deed with another that was evil. His (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: «Be keen on what benefits you and seek help from Allah» is comprehensive, beneficial speech, containing the happiness of this world and the Hereafter. Beneficial matters are of two kinds: religious matters and worldly matters. The servant needs worldly matters as he needs religious ones. The pivot of his happiness and success is keenness and striving in the beneficial matters among both, along with seeking help from Allah Almighty; whenever the servant is keen on beneficial matters, strives in them, follows their means and paths, and seeks his Lord's help in attaining and perfecting them, that is his perfection and the mark of his success. Whenever one of these three matters escapes him, he misses good in proportion to it. Whoever is not keen on beneficial matters, but is lazy, attains nothing. Laziness is the root of disappointment and failure. The lazy person attains no good, gains no noble rank, and attains neither religion nor worldly life. Whenever he is keen, but on other than beneficial matters — either on harmful matters or on matters that forfeit perfection — the fruit of his keenness is disappointment, loss of good, and the occurrence of evil and harm; how many are keen on pursuing unbeneficial ways and states and gain from their keenness nothing but toil, hardship, and misery. Then when the servant follows beneficial paths, is keen on them, and strives in them, they are not completed for him except by sincerely turning to Allah and seeking His help to attain and perfect them, and by not relying on himself, his own power and strength; rather his complete reliance, inwardly and outwardly, is upon his Lord. By that, difficulties become light for him, circumstances become easy, and good results and fruits are completed for him in matters of religion and matters of worldly life. Yet in these states he needs — indeed is in the utmost dire need of — knowing the matters over which one should be keen and earnest in seeking. Beneficial matters in religion go back to two things: beneficial knowledge and righteous deeds. As for beneficial knowledge: it is the knowledge that purifies hearts and souls and yields the happiness of the two abodes. It is what the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought of hadith, exegesis, and jurisprudence, and what assists toward that of the Arabic sciences, according to the state of the time and the place in which the person is; specifying that differs with differing circumstances. The approximate state: the student of knowledge should strive to memorize a concise primer from among the concise primers of the field in which he is engaged. If memorizing it word for word is unfeasible or difficult for him, let him repeat it often, reflecting on its meanings, until its meanings are firmly rooted in his heart. Then the remaining books of that field become like explanation, clarification, and elaboration of that foundation which he has known and grasped; for when a person memorizes the foundations and gains a complete mastery in knowing them, all the books of the field become easy for him, small and large. Whoever neglects the foundations is barred from arrival. Whoever is keen on what we have mentioned, and seeks Allah's help, Allah helps him and blesses his knowledge and the path he has taken. Whoever follows in seeking knowledge other than this beneficial method: time slips away from him and he attains nothing but toil, as is known by experience, and reality testifies to it; and if Allah makes easy for him a teacher who does well in the method of teaching and the paths of making understood, the cause leading to knowledge is completed for him. As for the second matter — righteous deeds, what they are — it is what combines sincerity to Allah and adherence to the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is drawing near to Allah by believing what is due to Allah of the attributes of perfection, and what He deserves from His servants of servitude, declaring Him far above what does not befit His majesty, affirming Him and affirming His Messenger in every report they have given of what has passed and what is to come, concerning the messengers, the books, the angels, the states of the Hereafter, Paradise and Hell, reward and punishment, and other than that. Then he strives to perform what Allah has imposed on His servants of the rights of Allah and the rights of His creatures, and perfects that with voluntary and supererogatory acts, especially the emphasized ones in their times, seeking Allah's help to perform them, to realize and perfect them, and to perform them in the manner of sincerity untainted by any aim of the selfish aims. Likewise he draws near to Allah by abandoning the forbidden, especially those to which souls call and incline. So he draws near to his Lord by abandoning them for Allah, as he draws near to Him by performing the commanded acts; whenever the servant is granted success in following this path in deeds, and seeks Allah's help for that, he prospers and succeeds. His perfection is according to what he upholds of these matters, and his deficiency according to what he misses of them. As for beneficial matters in worldly life: the servant must seek provision. He should follow the most beneficial worldly means suited to his state. That differs with differing people. By his earning and striving he should intend fulfilling the duty toward himself, the duty toward those he maintains and whose support he undertakes, and intend sufficiency and independence of creatures by his seeking. Likewise by his striving and earning he should intend attaining what the financial acts of worship depend on: zakah and charity, and the special and general charitable expenditures that depend on wealth; and he should aim at wholesome earnings, avoiding vile, forbidden earnings. Whenever the servant's seeking and striving in worldly life is for these noble aims, and he follows the most beneficial path he sees as suited to his state, his movements and striving become an act of devotion by which he draws near to Allah. Part of the perfection of that: the servant should not rely on his own power, strength, intelligence, knowledge, and skill in knowing and managing means; rather he should seek his Lord's help, relying on Him, hoping from Him that He make easy for him the easiest, most successful matters, nearest to attaining his aim, and ask his Lord to bless his provision. The first blessing of provision: that it be founded on God-consciousness and righteous intention. Part of the blessing of provision: that the servant be granted success in placing it in its obligatory and recommended places. Part of the blessing of provision: that the servant not forget graciousness in dealings, as He Almighty said: «And do not forget graciousness among you» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:237) — by easing for the well-off, granting respite to the insolvent, and showing favor in buying and selling, with what is easy of little or much; by that the servant attains much good. If it is said: which earnings are most appropriate and best? It is said: the people of knowledge have differed over that; among them are those who preferred farming and plowing, among them those who preferred buying and selling, and among them those who preferred practicing crafts and trades and the like. Each adduced his proof, but this hadith is the decisor of the dispute, namely that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Be keen on what benefits you, and seek help from Allah.» What is beneficial of that is known to differ with differing states and persons; among them are those for whom plowing and farming are best in his case, and among them those for whom buying and selling and practicing the craft he does well are best in his case; the best of that and of other things is the most beneficial. May Allah's prayers and peace be upon him who was given the comprehensive and beneficial words. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged contentment with Allah's decree and décision, after exerting effort and expending one's utmost in keenness on the beneficial. If what he dislikes befalls the servant, let him not attribute that to abandoning some of the means whose benefit he supposes if he had done them; rather let him rest upon Allah's decree and décision, so that his faith increases, his heart becomes still, and his soul rests; for ""if"" in this situation opens the work of Satan, by deficiency of his faith in the decree, objection against it, and opening the doors of worry and grief that weaken the heart. This state to which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided is the greatest of paths to rest of heart, and most conducive to attaining contentment and the good life: keenness on beneficial matters, striving to attain them, seeking Allah's help for them, thanking Allah for what He made easy of them, and being pleased concerning what escaped and was not attained of them. Know that the use of ""if"" differs with differing intent behind it: if it is used in this situation in which the missed cannot be retrieved, it opens upon the servant the work of Satan, as has preceded; likewise if it is used in wishing for evil and acts of disobedience, it is blameworthy, and its utterer is sinful even if he does not directly commit the act of disobedience, for he wished for its occurrence. As for when it is used in wishing for good, or in explaining beneficial knowledge, it is praiseworthy, because means take the rulings of their ends. This principle which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned — the command to be keen on beneficial matters, whose necessary accompaniment is avoiding harmful matters, along with seeking Allah's help — its use and command covers the particular matters specific to the servant and his attachments, and covers the universal matters pertaining to the community as a whole. They must all be keen on beneficial matters, namely the universal interests, and preparing against their enemies with every possible means suited to the time, of moral and material strength, and exerting the utmost of their capacity in that, seeking Allah's help to realize and perfect it and to repel all that opposes it. Explaining this sentence at length would be long, and its details are known. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined in this hadith between faith in the divine decree and predestination and acting by beneficial means; these two principles are indicated by the Book and the Sunnah in many places, and the religion is not complete except by them; indeed none of the intended matters at all are complete except by them, because his saying «Be keen on what benefits you» is a command to every religious and worldly means; indeed a command to earnestness and striving therein and keenness on it, in intention and aspiration, in deed and planning. And his saying: «and seek help from Allah» is faith in the divine decree and predestination, and a command to reliance on Allah, which is complete reliance on His power and strength Almighty in bringing benefits and repelling harms, along with complete confidence in Allah for the success of that. The follower of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) must rely on Allah in matters of his religion and worldly life, and uphold every beneficial means according to his capacity, knowledge, and understanding; and Allah is the one whose help is sought."
"13","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The believer to the believer is like a building, each part of which strengthens the other» — and he interlaced his fingers. Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This is a great hadith, containing the report from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the believers that they are upon this description, and including his urging to observe this principle, and that they be brothers, merciful, loving, and compassionate to one another; each of them loving for the other what he loves for himself and striving in that; and that they must observe the universal interests that gather all their interests, and that they be upon this description. For a building composed of foundations and encompassing walls, and walls enclosing particular dwellings, and what they include of roofs, doors, benefits, and utilities — no kind of that stands alone until each joins with the other. Likewise the Muslims must be so: observing the upholding of their religion and its laws, and what sustains and strengthens that and removes its hindrances and impediments. The individual obligations are upheld by every accountable person; it is not open to any capable accountable person to abandon them or to neglect them. As for the communal obligations: in each such obligation some of the Muslims are set to uphold it, so that sufficiency is attained by them and the intended aim is completed by them. He Almighty said concerning jihad: «And it is not for the believers to go forth all together; so why should there not go forth from each group among them a party, that they may gain understanding in religion and warn their people» (Qur'an At-Tawbah 9:122). And He Almighty said: «And let there be among you a community calling to good, enjoining the right and forbidding the wrong» (Qur'an Aal Imran 3:104). And He Almighty commanded mutual help in righteousness and God-consciousness. The Muslims' aim and objective is one: upholding the interests of their religion and worldly life, without which the religion is not complete; and each group strives to realize its task according to what suits it and suits the time and state. That is not completed for them except by holding consultations and examining the universal interests, by whatever means they may be attained, and how the paths to pursuing them are, and each group's assisting the other in its opinion, word, and deed, and in repelling oppositions and impediments from it. Among them is a group that learns and a group that teaches; among them is a group that goes out to jihad after learning the arts of war; among them is a group that garrisons and guards the frontiers and the enemy's routes; and among them is a group that occupies itself with the industries producing arms suited to each age as it comes; and among them is a group that occupies itself with tillage, cultivation, trade, varied earnings, and pursuing economic means; and among them is a group that occupies itself with studying politics and matters of war and peace, and what should be done with enemies of what returns to the interest of Islam and the Muslims: preferring the higher interests over the lower, repelling the higher harms by descending to the lower, weighing matters, and knowing the reality of interests and harms and their ranks. In short, they all strive to realize the interests of their religion and worldly life, helping and supporting one another, deeming the goal one though the paths differ, and the aim one though the means to it are many. How beneficial is acting upon this great hadith in which this noble Prophet guided his community to be like a building, each part of which strengthens the other, and like a single body: when one limb complains, the rest of the body responds with fever and sleeplessness. For this reason the Lawgiver urged all that strengthens this matter, all that necessitates love among the believers, and all by which cooperation in benefits is completed; and he forbade division and enmity and splitting the word, in many texts — until he counted this a mighty principle among the principles of the religion, which must be observed, considered, preferred over others, and pursued by every possible means. So we ask Allah Almighty to realize for the Muslims this principle, join their hearts, and make them one hand against whoever opposes and shows enmity to them; indeed He is Generous."
"14","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whenever a beggar or a seeker of a need came to him, would say: «Intercede, and you shall be rewarded; and Allah shall decree upon the tongue of His Messenger what He wills.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains a great principle and an immense benefit: that the servant should strive in matters of good, whether their aims and results bear fruit, or some of them occur, or nothing of them is completed. Such is intercession for those with needs before kings and notables, and those upon whom their needs depend; for many people refrain from striving therein when they do not know that their intercession will be accepted, and so miss for themselves much good from Allah, and a kind act toward their Muslim brother. For this the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his Companions to assist those with needs by interceding for them with him, so that they hasten reward with Allah, by his saying: «Intercede, you shall be rewarded.» For good intercession is beloved to Allah and pleasing to Him. He Almighty said: «Whoever intercedes a good intercession shall have a share of it» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:85). Along with hastening the present reward, he also hastens kindness and doing good to his brother, and thereby has a favor with him. Also, perhaps his intercession may be a cause for attaining his aim from the one interceded with, or part of it, as is the reality. Striving in matters of good and kindness that may or may not occur is an immediate good, accustoming souls to assisting in good, and preparing for undertaking the intercessions whose acceptance is certain or presumed. It contains among its benefits: striving in all that removes despair; for asking and striving are a mark of hope and aspiration to attain what is sought, and the opposite is the opposite. In the hadith is a proof of encouragement to direct people to doing good, and that the one interceded with is not obliged to accept the intercession, except when he intercedes to deliver due rights; for a due right must be discharged and delivered to its owner even if no one interceded for it, and that is emphasized along with intercession. It also contains the mercy of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in attaining good for his community by every path. This is one instance among thousands upon thousands indicating the perfection of his mercy and compassion (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for all good and benefits, general and particular, the community has not attained except at his hand, by his mediation, teaching, and guidance, just as he guided them to repelling evils and harms, general and particular, by every path. He conveyed, discharged the trust, and sincerely counseled the community — may Allah's prayers, peace, and blessings be upon him and upon his family and Companions. His saying: «And Allah decrees upon the tongue of His Prophet what He wills» — His Almighty decree is of two kinds: a universal ordained decree, encompassing good and evil, obedience and disobedience; indeed encompassing all that was and all that shall be, and all past and later events; and more specific than it, the religious, ordained decree, specific to what Allah loves and is pleased with. What He decrees upon the tongue of His Prophet is of the second division; for he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is a servant and messenger who fulfilled the station of servitude and perfected the ranks of messengership; all his words, deeds, guidance, and character are servitude to Allah connected to Allah Almighty's beloved things. In his case (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there was nothing merely permissible with no reward or recompense in it, let alone what is not commanded. This is the state of the servant-messenger, which rank he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) chose when given the choice between being a king-messenger or a servant-messenger."
"15","[AI-translation] On the authority of Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her): that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Give people their (proper) stations.» Reported by Abu Dawud.
What a wise hadith, containing urging to his community to observe wisdom. For wisdom is putting things in their places and bringing them down to their stations; and Allah Almighty is wise in His creation and ordaining, and wise in His law, command, and prohibition. He has commanded His servants to wisdom and to observing it in everything; and the commands of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his guidance all turn upon wisdom. Among them: this comprehensive hadith, in which he commanded that we give people their stations. That is in all dealings, all address, learning, and teaching. Among that: people are two divisions: a division with a special right, such as parents, children, relatives, neighbors, companions, scholars, and benefactors according to their general and special beneficence. For this division, giving them their stations is fulfilling their rights known by law and custom, of dutifulness, joining ties, kindness, honoring, loyalty, consolation, and all their rights; these are distinguished from others by these special rights. And a division with no distinction of a special right; they have only the right of Islam and the right of humanity. Their shared right: that harm and injury be withheld from them by word or deed, and that one love for the Muslims what one loves for oneself of good and dislike for them what one dislikes for oneself of evil; indeed harm must be withheld from all mankind, and one should convey to them what one can of kindness. What enters into this: associating with creatures according to their stations; the elder has honoring and respect, the young one is treated with the mercy and gentleness suited to his state, the peer is treated as he must be treated; the mother has a right special to her, the wife another right; one treats him in whom one confides and places trust, and is expansive with him, in a way one does not treat him in whom one does not confide nor place trust. One speaks with kings and holders of leadership with gentle speech suited to their ranks; for this He Almighty said to Musa and Harun: «Go to Pharaoh; indeed he has transgressed — then speak to him a gentle word, that perhaps he may remember or fear» (Qur'an Ta-Ha 20:43-44). Scholars are treated with honoring, reverence, learning, humility before them, showing need and want of their beneficial knowledge, and much supplication for them, especially at the time of their teaching and their special and general fatwas. Among that: commanding the young to good and forbidding them evil with gentleness and encouragement, expending what is suited of worldly things to stimulate and direct them to good, and avoiding verbal and physical harshness. For this he said: «Command your children to pray at seven years, and strike them for it at ten.» Likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dealt with those whose hearts were to be reconciled — with much worldly giving — in a way that attains reconciliation and entails benefits, and did not do that with one known for sincere faith, giving people their stations. Likewise addressing the wife and small children with address suited to them, in which is cheerfulness toward them and bringing joy into them. Likewise among giving people their stations: placing religious, worldly, and mixed offices in the hands of the competent, distinguished, who surpass others in undertaking that office. It is known that the rule of kingship: the duty therein especially — and in other offices generally — is consulting the people of authority in appointing who is fit for it, gathering strength, courage, forbearance, knowledge of inner and outer politics, and one with sufficient power to execute justice, deliver rights to their owners, deter wrongdoers and criminals, and other than that of what enters into rule. Likewise the office of judgeship: there is chosen for it the most knowledgeable of the law and of reality, the best in his religion, intellect, and praiseworthy qualities. Likewise the office of leading in mosques in Friday and congregational prayer: there is chosen for it the most knowledgeable of the rulings of worship, the most God-fearing, then the next best and so on. Likewise the office of leading armies: there are chosen for it people of strength, courage, judgment, sincere counsel, and knowledge of the arts of war and its instruments, and what follows of what this important, most dangerous office depends on, to other than that of the major and minor offices. They enter into His Almighty saying: «Indeed Allah commands you to render trusts to their owners» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:58). These offices are among the greatest trusts. They must be rendered to their owners, and the competent in them must be employed therein; every office has its particular competent. This enters into this noble hadith. Likewise enters into that the treatment of sinners and criminals: whoever the Lawgiver has set for his crime a punishment of a prescribed penalty and the like, what the Lawgiver specified is binding, for it is the very comprehensive general interest; and whoever has no specified punishment is disciplined according to his state and standing. Among them are those for whom rebuke and speech suited to his deed suffice, and among them those whom nothing deters but severe punishment. Likewise in charity and gifts: the gift to the itinerant beggar who goes around people, for whom a date or two, a morsel or two suffice, is not like the gift to the chaste poor person struck by poverty after wealth. And in the tradition: (Show mercy to the honored of a people brought low). Likewise one distinguished by traces, precedence, valor, and benefit to the Muslims is set apart from one who is not so. These matters and their likes enter into this comprehensive speech upon which law and reason agree. Whatever the Muslims see as good is good with Allah."
"16","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Sarmah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whoever harms, Allah shall harm him; and whoever causes hardship, Allah shall cause hardship upon him.» Reported by At-Tirmidhi and Ibn Majah.
This hadith indicates two of the principles of the law. One: that recompense is of the same kind as the deed, in good and evil; this is of Allah's wisdom for which He is praised. Just as whoever does what Allah loves, Allah loves him; and whoever does what He hates, Allah hates him; and whoever eases for a Muslim, Allah eases for him in this world and the Hereafter; and whoever relieves a believer of a distress among the distresses of this world, Allah relieves him of a distress among the distresses of the Day of Resurrection; and Allah is in the need of the servant so long as the servant is in the need of his brother — likewise whoever harms a Muslim, Allah harms him; whoever schemes against him, Allah schemes against him; whoever causes him hardship, Allah causes him hardship; to other than that of the examples entering into this principle. The second principle: prohibiting harm and mutual harm, and that «there is no harm nor reciprocal harm.» This encompasses all kinds of harm. Harm goes back to one of two matters: either missing a benefit or incurring an injury in some way. Unrightful harm is not lawful to convey and do to people; rather one must withhold his harm and injury from them in all ways. Entering into that are: cheating and fraud in transactions, concealing defects therein, scheming, deception, najsh, meeting riders bringing goods, a Muslim selling upon his brother's sale and buying upon his purchase. Likewise leases, all transactions, and proposing upon his brother's proposal, and proposing for offices in which there are qualified people holding them. All this is of the forbidden harming. And every transaction of this kind: Allah does not bless it, for whoever harms a Muslim, Allah harms him; and whomever Allah harms, good departs from him and evil heads toward him, because of what his hands earned. Entering into that is: a partner harming his partner, and a neighbor his neighbor, by word or deed — to the point that it is not lawful for him to introduce in his property what harms his neighbor, let alone directly harming him. Entering into that is: a debtor harming his creditor, and pursuing transactions that harm his creditor — to the point that it is not lawful for him to give charity while leaving what is due of debt except with his creditor's permission, or to pledge his assets to one of his creditors to the exclusion of the rest, or to endow, or to free what harms his creditor, or to spend more than necessary without his permission. Likewise harming in bequests, as He Almighty said: «after a bequest bequeathed or a debt, without harming» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:12) — by singling out one of his heirs with more than his share, or diminishing an heir, or bequeathing to other than his heir with intent to harm the heirs. Likewise it is not lawful for a husband to harm his wife in many ways: either by constraining her unjustly that she ransom herself from him, or by taking her back with intent to harm, or by inclining to one of his two wives in a way that harms the other and leaves her as if suspended. Of that is: bias in judgments, testimonies, division, and other things, toward one of two persons to benefit the other. All this enters into harming, and its doer deserves punishment and that Allah harm him for it. Worse than that is: backbiting people before rulers and commanders, to incite them to punish him, take his wealth, or withhold from him a right that is his; for whoever does this deed is a transgressor, so let him expect prompt and deferred punishment. Of this is: «The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade bringing a sick camel to a healthy one,» because of the harm in that. Likewise he forbade lepers and their like from mixing with people; and this and its like enters into His Almighty's saying: «And those who harm the believing men and believing women undeservedly have borne slander and manifest sin» (Qur'an Al-Ahzab 33:58). And he forbade frightening the Muslim, even in jest. Of this is mocking creatures, ridiculing them, backbiting their honor, and stirring enmity among them. All of it enters into harming and causing hardship, which necessitates punishment. And just as the hadith indicates by its express wording that whoever harms and causes hardship, Allah harms him and causes him hardship, its understood meaning indicates that whoever removes harm and hardship from a Muslim, Allah brings him good and repels harm and hardships from him — recompense in kind — whether attached to himself or to another."
"17","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Dharr al-Ghifari (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Fear Allah wherever you are; follow a bad deed with a good one, it shall erase it; and mix with people with good character.» Reported by Imam Ahmad and At-Tirmidhi.
This is a great hadith in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined the right of Allah and the rights of servants. The right of Allah upon His servants: that they fear Him as He deserves to be feared, fearing His wrath and punishment by avoiding the forbidden and performing the obligatory. This counsel is Allah's counsel to the first and the last, and the counsel of every messenger to his people, saying: (Worship Allah and fear Him). Allah has mentioned the traits of God-consciousness in His Almighty saying: «Righteousness is not that you turn your faces toward the east and the west, but righteousness is one who believes in Allah, the Last Day, the angels, the Book, and the prophets, and gives wealth in spite of love of it to relatives, orphans, the needy, the traveler, beggars, and for necks, and establishes prayer, and pays zakah, and those who fulfill their covenant when they covenant, and the patient in poverty, illness, and at the time of battle; those are they who were truthful, and those are the God-fearing» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:177); and in His saying: «And hasten to forgiveness from your Lord and a garden whose width is the heavens and the earth, prepared for the God-fearing» (Qur'an Aal Imran 3:133); then He mentioned the traits of God-consciousness, saying: «Those who spend in ease and hardship, and who restrain anger, and pardon people; and Allah loves the doers of good» (Qur'an Aal Imran 3:134). He described the God-fearing with faith in its principles, doctrines, and outward and inward deeds, with performing bodily acts of worship and financial acts of worship, patience in poverty, illness, and at the time of battle, pardoning people, bearing their injury, doing good to them, and their hastening, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves, to seek forgiveness and repent. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded and counseled constant God-consciousness wherever the servant is, in every time, every place, and every state of his states, because he is in dire need of God-consciousness, not dispensing with it in any state of his states. Then, since the servant must fall short in the rights and duties of God-consciousness, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded what repels and erases that: following a bad deed with a good one. ""The good deed"" is a comprehensive name for all that draws near to Allah Almighty; the greatest of the good deeds repelling bad deeds are sincere repentance, seeking forgiveness, and turning to Allah by His remembrance, love, fear, hope, and aspiration to Him and His bounty at all times. Among that are the financial and bodily expiations specified by the Lawgiver. Among the good deeds that repel bad deeds: pardoning people, doing good to creatures among humans and others, relieving distresses, easing for the insolvent, and removing harm and hardship from all worlds. He Almighty said: «Indeed good deeds remove bad deeds» (Qur'an Hud 11:114). And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The five prayers, Friday to Friday, and Ramadan to Ramadan are expiations for what is between them, so long as the major sins are avoided.» How many in the texts are forgivenesses arranged upon many acts of obedience. Among what Allah expiates sins by: calamities; for no worry, grief, or injury befalls the believer, even a thorn he is pricked with, except Allah expiates his sins by it. They are either loss of something beloved or occurrence of something hated, bodily, heartfelt, or financial, inner or outer — but calamities are without the servant's doing. For this he commanded him to what is of his doing: following a bad deed with a good one. Then, having mentioned the right of Allah — the counsel to God-consciousness, comprehensive of the doctrines of religion and its inward and outward deeds — he said: «and mix with people with good character.» The first of good character: withholding your injury from them in every way, pardoning their faults and their injury to you, then treating them with good in word and good in deed. Most particular of good character: breadth of forbearance toward people, patience with them, not being vexed by them, cheerfulness of face, gentleness of speech, and beautiful words that comfort the companion, bring him joy, and remove his loneliness and the hardship of his reserve. Jesting may at times be good if there is a benefit in it, but one should not do it much; jesting in speech is like salt in food: if absent or exceeding the limit, it is blameworthy. Among good character: treating everyone as suits him and fits his state — young and old, intelligent and foolish, learned and ignorant. Whoever fears Allah, realizes His God-consciousness, and mixes with people of differing ranks with good character has attained all good; for he has upheld the right of Allah and the rights of servants, and because he was of the doers of good in worshipping Allah, the doers of good to Allah's servants."
"18","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Wrongdoing is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains warning against wrongdoing and urging to its opposite, which is justice. The whole law is justice, commanding justice and forbidding wrongdoing. He Almighty said: «Say: My Lord has commanded equity» (Qur'an Al-A'raf 7:29); «Indeed Allah commands justice» (Qur'an An-Nahl 16:90); «Those who believed and did not clothe their faith with wrongdoing — those shall have security, and they are guided» (Qur'an Al-An'am 6:82). For faith — its principles and branches, inward and outward — is all justice, and its opposite is wrongdoing. The justest justice and its root: acknowledging and devoting monotheism sincerely to Allah, believing in His beautiful names and attributes, and devoting religion and worship sincerely to Him; and the greatest, severest wrongdoing is associating partners with Allah, as He Almighty said: «Indeed associating partners is a great wrong» (Qur'an Luqman 31:13). That is because justice is putting the thing in its place and upholding due rights, and wrongdoing is its opposite. The greatest, most binding rights: Allah's right upon His servants, that they know Him, worship Him, and associate nothing with Him; then upholding the principles of faith and the laws of Islam — establishing prayer, paying zakah, fasting Ramadan, pilgrimage to the Sacred House, striving in Allah's path by word and deed, and enjoining one another to truth and enjoining one another to patience. Among wrongdoing: neglecting anything of that, just as among justice: upholding the rights of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — believing in him, loving him, preferring that love over love of all creatures, obeying him, honoring and venerating him, and giving precedence to his command and word over the command and word of another. Among immense wrongdoing: the servant's neglecting anything of the rights of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who is nearer to the believers than their own selves, more merciful and compassionate to them than anyone of creatures, and through whose hands no good has reached anyone. Among justice: dutifulness to parents, joining kinship ties, and discharging the rights of companions and those one deals with. Among wrongdoing: neglecting that. Among justice: each spouse's fulfilling the right of the other; and whoever of them neglects that is a wrongdoer. Wronging people is of many kinds, gathered by his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in his sermon in the Farewell Pilgrimage: «Indeed your blood, your wealth, and your honor are inviolable to you, like the inviolability of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours.» All wrongdoing of its kinds is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection; its people are punished according to their wrongdoing, and the wronged are recompensed from the good deeds of the wrongdoers; if they have no good deeds, or they are exhausted, some of their bad deeds are taken and cast upon the wrongdoers. All justice is light on the Day of Resurrection: «On the Day you see the believing men and believing women, their light running before them and on their right: Glad tidings for you today — gardens beneath which rivers flow» (Qur'an Al-Hadid 57:12). Allah Almighty forbade wrongdoing for Himself and made it forbidden among His servants. Allah Almighty is upon a straight path in His words, deeds, and recompense, and He is justice. He has set for His servants the straight path, which goes back to justice; whoever turns aside from justice turns to wrongdoing and tyranny leading to Hell. Wrongdoing is of three kinds: a kind Allah does not forgive — associating partners with Allah: «Indeed Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:48); a kind of which Allah leaves nothing — the wrongdoing of servants against one another; for part of the perfection of His justice is that He settles accounts among creatures according to their wrongs; and a kind under Allah's will — if He wills He punishes for it, and if He wills He pardons its people — the sins between servants and their Lord, short of association."
"19","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Look to one below you, and do not look to one above you; it is more fitting that you not belittle Allah's blessing upon you.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
What a beneficial counsel, a healing, sufficing word. This indicates urging to thank Allah by acknowledging His blessings, speaking of them, and seeking help by them in obeying the Bestower, and performing all means assisting to thanks. For thanks to Allah is the head of worship, the root of good, and the most binding upon servants; for there is no blessing among servants, outward or inward, special or general, except from Allah. He is who brings good and good deeds and repels evil and bad deeds. He deserves that servants expend to Him of thanks what their powers reach; and the servant must strive by every means conveying and assisting him to thanks. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided to this wondrous remedy and strong cause for thanking Allah's blessings: that the servant observe at all times one below him in intellect, lineage, wealth, and kinds of blessings. Whenever he maintains this observation, it compels him to much thanks of his Lord and praise of Him; for he ever sees many creatures below him by degrees in these descriptions, many of whom wish to reach near what he has been given of health, wealth, provision, character and form. So he praises Allah for that with much praise, saying: Praise be to Allah who blessed me and preferred me over many of those He created with preference. He looks at many creatures deprived of their intellects, and praises his Lord for perfection of intellect; he witnesses a great world with no stored sustenance nor dwellings to shelter in, while he is secure in his dwelling, ample in his provision. He sees many creatures afflicted with kinds of diseases and sorts of illnesses, while he is healthy of that, clothed in health. He witnesses many creatures afflicted with a more terrible trial than that: deviation in religion and falling into the filths of disobedience, while Allah has preserved him from them or from much of them. He reflects on many people overcome by worry, possessed by grief, whisperings, and tightness of chest, then looks at his health from this disease and Allah's favor upon him with rest of heart — so that perhaps, though poor, by this blessing — the blessing of contentment and rest of heart — he surpasses many of the rich. Then whoever is afflicted with something of these matters finds a great world greater and more afflicted than him, so he praises Allah for the existence of health and for lightening the trial; for there is no hated thing but there exists a more hated thing greater than it. Whoever is granted success in being guided by this guidance to which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided, his thanks ever remain in strength and growth, and Allah's blessings upon him ever follow in succession. Whoever reverses the matter, raising his gaze and looking to one above him in health, wealth, provision, and their concomitants, must belittle Allah's blessing and lose His thanks. Whenever thanks is lost, blessings depart from him, punishments hasten to him, and he is tried with abiding gloom, lasting grief, resentment at the good he is in, and lack of contentment with Allah as Lord and disposer. That is harm in religion and worldly life and clear loss. Know that whoever reflects on the abundance of Allah's blessings, perceives Allah's favors outward and inward, and that there is no means to them but Allah's sheer bounty and kindness, and that one kind of Allah's blessings the servant cannot count and enumerate, let alone all kinds, let alone thanking them — he is compelled to complete acknowledgment of blessings, much praise of Allah, and is ashamed before his Lord to seek help by any of His blessings for what He does not love and is not pleased with; and it necessitated for him shame before his Lord, which is among the best branches of faith. So he is ashamed before his Lord to see him where He forbade him, or to miss him where He commanded him. And since thanks is the pivot and mark of good, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of it to Mu'adh ibn Jabal: «I love you, so do not omit to say at the end of every prescribed prayer: O Allah, help me to remember You, thank You, and worship You well.» And he used to say: «O Allah, make me thankful to You, remembering of You. O Allah, make me magnify thanks to You, multiply remembrance of You, follow Your counsel, and keep Your command.» And the greatest of the thankful acknowledged incapacity to thank Allah's blessings, saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «I cannot count praise of You; You are as You praised Yourself.» And Allah knows best."
"20","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Allah does not accept the prayer of one of you — when he has lost ablution — until he performs ablution.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
The hadith indicates by its express wording: whoever has not performed ablution after ritual impurity, his prayer is not accepted — that is, not valid nor discharging; and by its understood meaning: whoever has performed ablution, his prayer is accepted — that is, along with the rest of what is due and stipulated for prayer. For the Lawgiver often attaches many rulings to particular matters that alone do not suffice for the ruling to follow, until the remaining conditions join and impediments are absent. This legal principle is agreed upon among the people of knowledge; for worship containing many matters — such as prayer — it is not stipulated that its rulings be gathered in the Lawgiver's speech in one place; rather all that has been reported therein of rulings are gathered, and the sum of its rulings is taken from multiple texts. This is among the greatest causes for the jurists' establishing the sciences of jurisprudence and rulings, arranging and chaptering them, and joining kinds and types to one another, to make it near for others. They have therein the white hand — may Allah reward them for Islam and the Muslims with the best reward. This principle should be considered in every place: rulings are not complete except by the gathering of their conditions and requisites and the absence of their impediments. Ritual impurity includes all nullifiers of ablution: entering into it are what exits from the two passages, sleep that nullifies ablution, much exiting impurity from the rest of the body if impure, eating camel meat, touching a woman with desire, and touching the private part with the hand — with disagreement in some of them. Whoever has any of these nullifiers occur from him, his prayer is not valid until he performs the legal ablution: washing the limbs Allah specified in Surat al-Ma'idah, with order and continuity; or purifying with dust in place of water when use of water is unfeasible — either for its absence or from fearing harm by its use. In this is a proof that if one prayed forgetfully or ignorantly of his impurity, he must repeat, by the generality of the hadith — and this is agreed upon. Though he is rewarded for his performance of the form of prayer and what it contains of worship, he must repeat to clear his liability. This is unlike one who purified himself but forgot impurity on his body or garment; he need not repeat, according to the sound view. For purification is of the category of performing commanded matters, without whose performance liability is not cleared. As for avoiding impurity, it is of the category of avoiding the prohibited, which, when done while one is excused, entails no repetition."
"21","[AI-translation] On the authority of Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Ten are of the fitrah: trimming the mustache, leaving the beard, the tooth-stick, sniffing water, clipping the nails, washing the finger-joints, plucking the armpit, shaving the pubic hair, cleansing with water — meaning istinja.» The narrator said: And I forgot the tenth, unless it be rinsing the mouth. Reported by Muslim.
""Fitrah"" is the constitution upon which Allah created His servants, making them constituted upon it: upon love of good and preferring it, hatred of evil and repelling it; He constituted them as upright, ready to accept good, sincerity to Allah, and drawing near to Him. Allah Almighty made the laws of fitrah two kinds. One: purifying the heart and soul — belief in Allah and its concomitants: fear of Him, hope in Him, love of Him, and turning to Him. He Almighty said: «So set your face to the religion, upright — Allah's fitrah upon which He constituted people; there is no altering Allah's creation; that is the upright religion, but most people do not know — turning to Him, fearing Him, establishing prayer, and not being of the associators» (Qur'an Ar-Rum 30:30-31). These purify the soul, cleanse and nurture the heart, remove vile defects from it, and adorn it with beautiful character; they all go back to the principles of faith and the deeds of hearts. The second kind: what goes back to purifying the outward and its cleanliness, repelling dirt and filth from it — these ten. They are among the beauties of the Islamic religion; for they are all cleansing of the limbs and perfecting them, that their health be complete and they be ready for all that is wanted of them. As for rinsing the mouth and sniffing water: they are legislated in purification from minor and major impurity by consensus, and they are obligatory therein, of purifying and cleaning the mouth and nose — for the mouth and nose are coursed over by much dirt, vapors, and the like, and one is in need of that and its removal. Likewise the tooth-stick purifies the mouth. It is ""a purifier of the mouth, pleasing to the Lord."" For this reason it is legislated at all times, and is emphasized at ablution, prayer, waking from sleep, change of mouth, yellowness of teeth, and the like. As for trimming the mustache or paring it until the lip shows: because of the cleanliness in that, and guarding against what exits from the nose; for when the mustache hair hangs over the lip, it touches what one takes of food and drink, besides disfiguring the appearance by its abundance — though he who pays no heed deems it fine. This is unlike the beard: for Allah made it a dignity for a man and a beauty for him. For this reason his beauty remains in old age by the presence of beard hair. Consider him who disobeys the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and shaves it: how his face remains disfigured, its beauties gone — especially in old age — becoming like an old woman: when she reaches this age her beauties are gone, even if in her youth she was among the most beautiful of women. This is sensed, but habit and blind imitation necessitate deeming the ugly fine and the fine ugly. As for clipping the nails, plucking the armpit, washing the finger-joints — the folds of the body in which dirt gathers — they have of cleaning and removing harmful things what cannot be denied; likewise shaving the pubic hair. As for istinja — removing what exits from the two passages with water or stone — it is necessary and a condition of purity. So you know that all these things perfect man's outward, purify and clean him, repel from him harmful and repugnant things; and cleanliness is of faith. The point is that fitrah encompasses the whole Sharia, its inward and outward: for it purifies the inward of vile character and adorns it with beautiful character, which goes back to the doctrines of faith and monotheism, sincerity to Allah and turning to Him; and it removes from the outward impurities, filth, and their causes, and purifies it with sensory purity and moral purity. For this reason he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Purity is half of faith.» And He Almighty said: «Indeed Allah loves the oft-repentant and loves the purified» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:222). So the whole Sharia is purity, growth, development, and perfecting; urging to lofty matters and forbidding the base thereof. And Allah knows best."
"22","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Water is pure; nothing makes it impure.» Reported by Ahmad, At-Tirmidhi, Abu Dawud, and An-Nasa'i.
This authentic hadith indicates a comprehensive principle: that water — all waters springing from the earth that are pure, and descending from the sky, remaining upon their constitution, or altered by their settling place or passage, or by pure things cast into them even if altered much — is pure, used in purification and other than it. Excluded from this comprehensive speech is only water whose color, taste, or smell is altered by impurity, as in some wordings of this hadith. Scholars have agreed on the impurity of water altered by impurity; Imam Ahmad and others argued by His Almighty saying: «Forbidden to you are carrion, blood, and the flesh of swine» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 5:3) to the end of the verse. That is: whenever the qualities of these forbidden things appear in water, it becomes impure, foul. This hadith and others indicate that water altered by pure things is purifying; that water a woman has been alone with does not prevent from it at all; and the purifying nature of water into which the hand of one rising from night sleep is dipped — though one rising from sleep is forbidden to dip it until he washes it thrice. As for prohibiting the water, the hadith does not indicate it. What is meant: this hadith indicates that water is of two divisions: impure — what one of its qualities is altered by impurity, little or much; and purifying — what is not so. Affirming a third kind — neither purifying nor impure, but pure not purifying — has no legal proof, so it remains upon the foundation of purifying nature. This generality is supported by His Almighty saying: «And if you find no water, then seek wholesome earth» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 5:6). This is general for all water, for it is an indefinite in the context of negation, encompassing all water, excepting impure water by consensus. This hadith also indicates: the foundation in waters is purity. Likewise in other than them: whenever doubt occurs in something of them — whether the cause of defilement is found in it or not — the foundation is purity."
"23","[AI-translation] From Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding the cat: «It is not impure; it is among those, male and female, that go around among you.» Reported by Malik, Ahmad, and the authors of the four Sunan.
This hadith contains two principles. The first of them: hardship brings ease — and this is a major principle among the principles of the Sharia. Among its applications is that these things which are difficult to guard against are pure; it is not obligatory to wash whatever (the cat) has come into contact with by its mouth, its paw, or its foot, for he gave as the reason for that his saying: «It is among those that go around among you, male and female.» Just as He permitted cleaning with stones (istijmar) of the place where filth exits from the two passages, and wiping whatever impurity has touched of the two sandals and the two leather socks, and the lower part of the garment; and He pardoned a small amount of impure street mud; and the blood remaining in meat and veins after the outpoured blood (has been drained) was permitted; and what the dog's mouth has touched of game was permitted; and similar things brought together by a single cause, which is hardship. The second: the cat and whatever is smaller than it in build, such as the mouse and the like, is pure during life; whatever it comes into contact with of food, drink, clothing, and other things does not become impure. For this reason our companions said: animals are of five categories with respect to purity and impurity. The first of them: impure while alive and after death, in its essence, its parts, and its excretions — such as dogs and all predatory beasts, swine, and the like. The second: what is pure during life but impure after death — such as the cat and whatever is smaller than it in build; neither slaughter nor anything else renders it lawful (to eat). The third: what is pure during life and after death, but whose meat is not lawful to eat — such as insects that have no flowing blood. The fourth: what is pure during life and after (ritual) slaughter — such as animals whose meat is lawful to eat, like grazing livestock and the like. The fifth: what is pure during life and after death — whether slaughtered or not — and is lawful — such as all sea animals and locusts. Many scholars have drawn evidence from his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «It is among those that go around among you, male and female» for the purity of children and the purity of their mouths, even after impurity has touched them; and likewise the purity of the saliva, sweat, and hair of the donkey and the mule. And where is the hardship of the cat compared with the hardship of the donkey and the mule? It is supported by the fact that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to ride them — he and his Companions — and they did not guard themselves against what we have mentioned; and this is the correct view. As for his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the meat of donkeys on the day of Khaybar: «It is filth (rijs)» — meaning its meat is filthy, impure, and forbidden to eat — as for their saliva, sweat, and hair, he did not prohibit them, nor did he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guard himself against them. As for dogs: he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded washing whatever they lick seven times, one of them with earth."
"24","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The five prayers, Friday to Friday, and Ramadan to Ramadan are expiations for what is between them, so long as the major sins are avoided.» Reported by Muslim.
This hadith indicates the greatness of Allah's bounty and generosity in His preferring these three great acts of worship, and that they hold a lofty station with Allah, and their fruits are countless. Among their fruits is that Allah made them a completion of the servant's religion and Islam, and a means of growth for faith, watering its tree. For Allah planted the tree of faith in the hearts of the believers according to their faith, and decreed — out of His kindness and bounty — obligatory and recommended acts that water this tree, make it grow, and repel blights from it until it becomes complete and yields its fruit at all times by permission of its Lord; and He made them remove blights from it. The harm of sins is great, and their diminishing of faith is known. These three obligations — prayer, Friday, and fasting — are expiations for sins when the servant avoids the major sins; Allah forgives through them the minor sins and misdeeds. They are among the greatest of what enters into His saying, exalted be He: «Indeed, good deeds remove evil deeds» (Qur'an Hud 11:114). Just as Allah, out of His kindness, made avoiding major sins a cause for the expiation of minor sins; He, exalted be He, said: «If you avoid the major sins that you are forbidden, We will expiate your evil deeds and admit you to a noble abode» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:31). As for major sins, they require repentance. It is learned from this hadith that every text in which the expiation of evil deeds by some righteous deeds is mentioned, what is meant thereby is minor sins; for if these great acts of worship do not expiate major sins, then how could lesser ones? The hadith is explicit that sins are of two kinds: major and minor. People have spoken much about the difference between minor and major sins, and the best that has been said is: the major sin is that for which a prescribed punishment (hadd) in this world is stipulated, or a threat in the Hereafter, or its doer is cursed, or (divine) anger and the like is stipulated for it; and minor sins are what is besides that. Or it is said: major sins are what is prohibited as ends in themselves, and minor sins are what is prohibited as means. Means: such as the forbidden glance together with seclusion with a non-mahram woman, while the major sin is the act of adultery itself; and like the usury of surplus (riba al-fadl) together with the usury of delay (riba al-nasi'ah), and the like. And Allah knows best."
"25","[AI-translation] From Malik ibn al-Huwayrith (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Pray as you have seen me pray; when the prayer is due, let one of you call the adhan for you, and let the eldest of you lead you in prayer.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains three sentences, the greatest of them the first. The first sentence: his saying, «When the prayer is due, let one of you call the adhan for you.» In it is the legislation of the adhan and its obligation, because of the command; and that it is after the entry of the time. Excepted from that is the Fajr prayer, for he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Bilal calls the adhan at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls, for he does not call until it is said to him: morning has come, morning has come.» The adhan is a communal obligation (fard kifayah), not an individual obligation, for a command from the Lawgiver: if every accountable individual is addressed by it and its performance is sought from him, then it is an individual obligation; but if only its performance is sought, regardless of the individuals, then it is a communal obligation. Here he said: «Let one of you call the adhan for you.» The wordings of the adhan are well known. The mu'adhdhin should be loud-voiced, trustworthy, knowledgeable of the time and careful in determining it, for that best attains the purpose; and whoever (generally) achieves the announcement suffices. The hadith indicates the obligation of the adhan in residence and in travel. The iqamah is part of the completion of the adhan, for the adhan is the announcement of the entry of the time of prayer, and the iqamah is the announcement of standing for it. Many texts have come regarding its virtue and the abundance of its reward, and the recommendation of answering the mu'adhdhin: the one answering says the same as the mu'adhdhin says, except when he says, ""Come to prayer, come to success,"" when he says the phrase of seeking Allah's help for what he has been called to of prayer and success — which is all good: ""There is no power nor strength except through Allah."" Then he sends blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and says: ""O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and the established prayer, grant Muhammad the wasilah and the excellence, and raise him to the praised station that You promised him."" Then he supplicates for himself, for it is among the occasions of answered supplication which the supplicant should seek. The second sentence: «Let the eldest of you lead you.» In it is the obligation of congregational prayer, and that its minimum is an imam and a follower; and that the most entitled to imamate is the one who best fulfills the purpose of imamate, as is established in the Sahih: «The people are led by the one among them most versed in the Book of Allah; if they are equal in recitation, then the most knowledgeable of the Sunnah; if they are equal in the Sunnah, then the earliest in emigration or in Islam.» If they are close to one another — as in this hadith — then the elder of the two is preferred; for preferring the elder is legislated in every matter in which order is sought, when the younger has no additional merit, in accordance with his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «Let the elder, the elder (begin).» When the prayer is arranged with an imam and followers, the imam is appointed only to be followed: when he says the takbir, those behind him say it; when he bows, prostrates, and rises, those after him follow him; and keeping pace with him in the actions of the prayer is prohibited. As for racing the imam and going ahead of him in bowing or prostration, lowering or rising: that is forbidden and invalidates the prayer. The followers are commanded to follow their imam, and are prohibited from keeping pace (with him), racing him, or lagging far behind. If they are two or more, the best is that they form rows behind him; it is permissible (for them to stand) on his right or on both his sides. A single man stands in the row at the imam's right; a woman (stands) behind the man or the men, standing alone — unless there are other women with her, in which case they are like men regarding the obligation of forming rows. If a single man stands behind the imam or behind the row without an excuse, his prayer is invalid. The imam must achieve the purpose of imamate: audibility in the takbirs of movement and in the tasmī', and audibility in the audible recitation; and he must be considerate of the followers in moving ahead and holding back, and lighten (the prayer) while completing it. The third sentence — which is the first in this hadith — his saying: «Pray as you have seen me pray.» This is teaching from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by word and deed, as he did in the Hajj, where he would perform the rites and say to the people: «Take your rites from me.» This sentence covers everything he used to do, say, and command in the prayer. That is: the servant completes all the conditions of the prayer (its manner), then stands for his prayer facing the qiblah, intending the specific prayer in his heart, and says, ""Allah is Most Great."" Then he says the opening supplication, and seeks refuge (with Allah) with what is established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the forms of opening supplications and seeking refuge; and he recites, ""In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."" Then he recites the Fatihah, and a long surah in the Fajr prayer, a short one in the Maghrib prayer, and in between in the remaining prayers. Then he bows, saying the takbir and raising his hands level with his shoulders in his bowing and in his rising from it in every rak'ah, and at the opening takbir, and when he rises from the first tashahhud — according to the correct view — in the four- and three-rak'ah prayers. And he says, ""Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great,"" once as obligatory; the minimum of perfection is three times, or more; and likewise the tasbih of prostration, saying, ""Glory be to my Lord, the Most High."" Then he raises his head, saying — whether imam or praying alone — ""Allah hears whoever praises Him; our Lord, to You belongs praise, abundant, good, blessed praise."" And likewise the follower, except that he does not say, ""Allah hears whoever praises Him."" Then he says the takbir and prostrates upon seven limbs: the two feet, the two knees, the two palms, and the forehead, along with the nose; pressing them firmly upon the ground and keeping them apart, and not spreading his forearms as the dog spreads (them). Then he rises, saying the takbir, and sits in iftarash — sitting upon his left foot, erecting his right foot, directing its toes toward the qiblah. All the sitting of the prayer is iftarash, except in the final tashahhud, where he should sit in tawarruk: sitting upon the ground and bringing his left foot out on his right side. And he says between the two prostrations: ""My Lord, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, provide for me, and mend me."" Then he performs the second prostration like the first; and he does likewise in every rak'ah. He must be tranquil in every lowering and rising, bowing and prostration, standing and sitting. Then he recites the tashahhud, saying: ""Greetings belong to Allah, and prayers and good things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger."" This is the first tashahhud. Then he rises, if it is a four- or three-rak'ah prayer, and prays the rest of it with the Fatihah alone. If he is in the tashahhud that is followed by the salam, he says: ""O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Abraham; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Abraham; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of Hell, from the punishment of the grave, from the trials of life and death, and from the trial of the False Messiah."" And he supplicates with whatever he wishes, then gives the salam, and remembers Allah with what has been reported. Everything reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding the prayer — his actions, sayings, teaching, and guidance — is included in his saying: «Pray as you have seen me pray.» And it is commanded: a command of obligation or of recommendation, according to the evidence. Whatever among its parts is not waived through forgetfulness, ignorance, or deliberate omission is called a pillar (rukn) — such as the opening takbir, the recitation of the Fatihah, the final tashahhud, and the salam; and like standing, bowing, prostration, and uprightness after them. Whatever is waived through forgetfulness and is remedied by the prostration of forgetfulness is called obligatory (wajib) — such as the first tashahhud and sitting for it, the takbirs other than the opening takbir, saying ""Allah hears whoever praises Him"" for the imam and the one praying alone, saying ""Our Lord, to You belongs praise"" for every worshipper, saying ""Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great"" once in bowing, and ""Glory be to my Lord, the Most High"" once in prostration, and saying ""My Lord, forgive me"" between the two prostrations. What is besides that is among its perfecting and recommended elements — especially the soul and core of the prayer: presence of heart in it, reflection upon what one says of recitation, remembrance, and supplication, and what one does of standing and sitting, bowing and prostration; humility before Allah and devoutness (khushu') in it toward Allah. Among what enters into that is avoiding what the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited in the prayer, such as laughing, talking, and much consecutive movement without necessity. For the prayer is not complete except with the presence of its conditions, pillars, and obligations, and the absence of its invalidators, which go back to two matters: either neglecting something required, or doing something prohibited in it, such as talking and the like."
"26","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «I have been given five (things) which none of the prophets before me was given: I have been aided with terror (cast into the hearts of the enemy) over a month's journey; the whole earth has been made for me a mosque and a means of purification, so whichever man of my ummah is overtaken by prayer, let him pray; spoils of war have been made lawful for me though they were not lawful for anyone before me; I have been given intercession; and (whereas) a prophet would be sent to his people specifically, I have been sent to mankind generally.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
Our Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was favored with many excellences by which he surpassed all the prophets. Every praiseworthy trait going back to beneficial knowledge, sound understanding, and righteous deeds — our Prophet has its highest, best, and most perfect. For this reason, when Allah mentioned the eminent noble prophets, He said to His Prophet: «Those are they whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance» (Qur'an Al-An'am 6:90). Their guidance is what they possessed of outward and inward excellences. He fulfilled what he was commanded, and surpassed all creation. For this reason Allah singled out our Prophet with special qualities in which none of the prophets shares; among them are these five, which have returned upon his ummah with all good, blessing, and benefit. The first of them is that he was aided with terror over a month's journey — and this is a divine aid and a host from heaven by which Allah aids His Messenger and his ummah who follow his guidance. Whenever his enemy is at a distance of a month (of travel) or less from him, he is in terror of him. When Allah wills to aid someone, He casts terror into the hearts of his enemies; He, exalted be He, said: «We shall cast terror into the hearts of those who disbelieve for associating with Allah that for which He sent down no authority» (Qur'an Aal Imran 3:151). And He casts into the hearts of the believers such strength, firmness, serenity, and tranquility as is the greatest of the causes of victory. Allah, exalted be He, promised His Prophet and his ummah great victory, and to aid them with causes to which He guided them — such as unity and harmony, patience, and preparation against the enemy with every attainable (means) of strength — among other wise counsels; and He aided them with this victory, and He — exalted be He — has done so, as is known of the state of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those who followed him among the Rightly-Guided Caliphs and the righteous kings: there was accomplished for them, in the shortest time, such victory and mighty honor as was accomplished for none besides them. The second: his saying, «The whole earth has been made for me a mosque and a means of purification.» He affirmed that with his saying: «Wherever prayer overtakes anyone of my ummah, there is his mosque and his means of purification.» All the regions of the earth are a mosque in which one prays, without exception, except what the Lawgiver has specified as prohibited. It is established that prayer is prohibited in the graveyard and the bathroom, and in camel resting places; and likewise in a usurped place and an impure place, because of the requirement of purity for the worshipper's body, garment, and spot. Likewise, whoever lacks water or is harmed by using it may turn to tayammum with anything that rises upon the surface of the earth — whether dust that has particles or otherwise — as is explicit in this hadith, together with His saying, exalted be He: «Seek clean earth and wipe your faces and your hands with it» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 5:6). For sa'id (earth) is everything that rises upon the surface of the earth in all its parts. It indicates that tayammum upon the face and the hands takes the place of purification with water, and that one may do with it of prayer, tawaf, touching the mushaf, and other things what one does with purification by water. The Lawgiver substituted dust for water when its use is impossible; this indicates that if one purifies with dust and his ablution is not broken, his tayammum is not nullified by the exit of the time nor by its entry; and that if one intends tayammum for a voluntary (prayer), the obligatory becomes permissible with it, as with purification by water; and that its ruling is the ruling of water in all rulings in the case of inability. The third: his saying, «Spoils of war have been made lawful for me, though they were not lawful for anyone before me.» That is because of his nobility with his Lord, and the nobility and virtue of his ummah, and the perfection of their sincerity; so He made them lawful for them, without diminishing anything of the reward of their jihad. There has come to this ummah through them such expansion of provisions, abundance of good things, and aid in matters of religion and worldly life as cannot be counted. For this reason he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «My provision has been placed under the shade of my spear.» As for the nations before us, their jihad is little in comparison with this ummah, and they are below this ummah in strength of faith and sincerity; so out of His mercy toward them He withheld spoils from them, lest it impair their sincerity. And Allah knows best. The fourth: his saying, «I have been given intercession.» It is the greatest intercession, from which the senior messengers excuse themselves, and for which their seal, Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), steps forward; Allah grants him intercession for creation, and he attains the Praised Station in which the first and the last, and the inhabitants of the heavens and the earth, praise him. His ummah obtains the fullest share and the most complete portion of this intercession, and he intercedes for them a special intercession, and Allah, exalted be He, grants him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Every prophet had a supplication which he hastened; I have saved my supplication as intercession for my ummah, and it will reach — if Allah wills — whoever dies without associating anything with Allah.» And he said: «The happiest of people with my intercession is he who says: there is no god but Allah, sincerely from his heart.» The fifth: his saying, «(Whereas) a prophet» — that is, the genus of prophets — «would be sent to his people specifically, but I have been sent to mankind generally.» That is because of the perfection, universality, and comprehensiveness of his law, and its encompassing absolute welfare; it is suited to every time and place, and true welfare is only attained through it. It has laid down for mankind great foundations: whenever they observe them, their worldly life is set right for them just as their religion is set right for them."
"27","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: «My close friend (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me three things: fasting three days of every month, the two rak'ahs of Duha, and that I pray witr before I sleep.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
His counsel (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his address to one member of his ummah is an address to the whole ummah, unless evidence indicates specificity. These three counsels are among the most emphasized of the voluntary prayers and fasts. As for fasting three days of every month: it is equivalent to fasting the year, for a good deed is (rewarded) tenfold, and fasting three days of every month is equivalent to fasting the whole month. The Sharia is built upon ease and facility, and the aspect of bounty in it predominates. This deed is easy for whomever Allah makes it easy; it is not burdensome for a person, nor does it prevent him from attending to any of his concerns — yet it carries this immense virtue, for a deed is better the more obedient to the Lord and the more beneficial to the servant it is than what is not so. There is established encouragement to specify six (days) of Shawwal, fasting the day of 'Arafah, the ninth and tenth of Muharram, and Mondays and Thursdays. As for the Duha prayer: many authentic hadiths have come in succession regarding its virtue. The scholars have differed over the desirability of performing it regularly or of omitting it on some days; the correct view is that performing it regularly is desirable, in accordance with this hadith and others — except for one who has a habit of night prayer: if he leaves it sometimes, there is no harm. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported: «Each morning, three hundred and sixty charities are due upon every human being: every tasbihah is a charity, every tahmidah is a charity, every takbirah is a charity, enjoining good is a charity, forbidding evil is a charity — and two rak'ahs which one prays of Duha suffice for that.» The scholars said: the minimum of the Duha prayer is two rak'ahs, and its maximum is eight; its time (the Duha prayer) is from the sun's rising a spear's length until just before the zenith. As for witr (its ruling and the number of its rak'ahs): it is an emphasized sunnah; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encouraged it and performed it regularly in residence and in travel. Its minimum is one rak'ah; if he wishes, three, five, seven, nine, or eleven rak'ahs. He may perform them consecutively with a single salam, or greet (with salam) after every two rak'ahs. The time of witr is from the later 'Isha prayer until the rising of dawn; the best is the end of the night for one who hopes to rise at its end, otherwise he prays witr at its beginning, as in this hadith."
"28","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Religion is ease. No one makes religion hard upon himself but it overwhelms him. So be upright, aim for what is right, rejoice, and seek help in the morning, the evening, and part of the night.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim). In a (variant) narration: «Moderation, moderation, and you will reach.»
How great is this hadith, and how comprehensive of good and beneficial counsels and compendious foundations! He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laid down at its beginning this great foundation, saying: «Religion is ease» — that is, facilitated and eased in its beliefs, its morals, and its deeds; in its commands and its prohibitions. Its beliefs, which go back to faith in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, the Last Day, and the decree — its good and its evil — are the sound beliefs in which hearts find tranquility, and which convey those who follow them to the noblest goal and the best objective. Its morals and deeds are the most perfect morals and the most upright deeds; through them come the soundness of religion, worldly life, and the Hereafter, and with their loss all soundness is lost. They are all facilitated and eased; every accountable person finds himself capable of them — they are not burdensome for him nor do they overtax him. Its beliefs are sound and simple, accepted by sound minds and upright natures; its obligations are the easiest of things. As for the five prayers: they recur every day and night five times at times suited to them. The Gentle, All-Knowing completed their ease by enjoining congregation and gathering for them, for gathering in acts of worship is among the encouragements and facilitations of them; and He arranged for them such religious good, soundness of faith, and reward from Allah — immediate and deferred — as must make the believer find them sweet and praise Allah for enjoining them upon the servants, since they have no sufficiency without them. As for zakah: it is not obligatory upon a poor person who has no zakatable minimum; it is obligatory only upon the wealthy — as a completion of their religion and Islam, a growth for their wealth and morals, a repelling of blights from them and their wealth, a purification for them from evil deeds, a consolation for their needy, and an upholding of their universal interests. Yet it is a very small portion in comparison with what Allah has given them of wealth and provision. As for fasting: what is obligatory is one month of every year, in which all the Muslims gather, abandoning in it their elemental desires — of food, drink, and marital relations — during the day; and Allah compensates them for that out of His bounty and kindness with the completion of their religion and faith, increase in their perfection, His immense reward, His extensive beneficence, and other of the abundant good He has arranged for fasting. It is a cause for the attainment of piety, which goes back to performing all good deeds and abandoning evil deeds. As for Hajj: Allah has enjoined it only upon the capable, and once in a lifetime; in it are such abundant religious and worldly benefits as cannot be counted. We have detailed the interests and benefits of Hajj in another place. He, exalted be He, said: «That they may witness benefits for themselves» (Qur'an Al-Hajj 22:28) — that is, religious and worldly. Then after that, the rest of the laws of Islam, which are of the utmost ease, going back to rendering the right of Allah and the right of His servants; they are in themselves facilitated. He, exalted be He, said: «Allah intends ease for you and does not intend hardship for you» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:185). Moreover, when an impediment — illness, travel, or otherwise — befalls the servant, He has arranged for that such dispensations, and the dropping of some obligations or their descriptions and forms, as is known. Then, when the servant looks at the duties assigned to the servants in the day and night — varied, of obligation and voluntary, prayer and fasting and charity and other things — and wishes to follow in them the most perfect of creation and their leader, Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he finds that not burdensome for him, nor preventing him from his worldly interests; rather, he is able along with it to render all rights: the right of Allah, the right of the self, the right of family and companions, and the right of everyone who has a right over a person — gently and easily. As for one who is strict with himself, not content with what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was content with, nor with what he taught the ummah and guided them to — but exaggerates and plunges deep into acts of worship: religion overwhelms him, and his end is inability and breaking off. For this reason he said: «No one contends with religion in severity but it defeats him.» Whoever resists this religion with severity and exaggeration, without moderation, is defeated by religion; he is left exhausted and turns back. For this reason he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded moderation and encouraged it, saying: «Moderation, moderation, and you will reach.» Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counseled uprightness (tasdid) and aiming for what is right (muqarabah), and strengthening souls with glad tidings of good and absence of despair. Uprightness is that a person speak sound speech, do sound deeds, and tread the right path — correctness in his sayings and deeds in every way. If he does not attain uprightness in every way, let him fear Allah as much as he can and aim near the goal; whoever does not attain all correctness, let him content himself with approximation; whoever is unable to do all of a deed, let him do of it what he can. From this is taken a beneficial principle, to which His saying, exalted be He, also points: «So fear Allah as much as you can» (Qur'an At-Taghabun 64:16); and his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «When I command you with a matter, do of it what you can.» The questions built upon this principle are countless. In another hadith: «Make things easy and do not make them hard; give glad tidings and do not repel.» Then he concluded the hadith with a counsel light upon souls yet of the utmost benefit, saying: «Seek help in the morning, the evening, and part of the night.» These three times — just as they are the sole means of covering short and long distances in physical journeys, with the traveler's comfort, his mount's comfort, and his arrival in comfort and ease — are likewise the sole means of covering the journey of the Hereafter, treading the straight path, and journeying to Allah in a beautiful manner. Whenever the worker takes himself in hand and occupies himself with good and righteous deeds suited to his time — the beginning of his day, its end, and part of his night, especially the end of the night — he obtains the fullest share and most abundant portion of good and of the enduring righteous deeds; he attains happiness, triumph, and success; success is completed for him in comfort and tranquility, along with the attainment of his worldly aims and personal objectives. This is among the greatest proofs of Allah's mercy toward His servants through this religion — which is the substance of eternal happiness — in that He established it for His servants, clarified it upon the tongues of His messengers, made it facilitated and easy, aided toward it from every direction, was kind to the workers, and protected them from interruptions and obstacles. It is thus known that several principles are taken from this great hadith. The first principle: the comprehensive ease of the Sharia in general. The second principle: hardship brings ease when it occurs. The third principle: ""When I command you with a matter, do of it what you can."" The fourth principle: energizing the workers and giving them glad tidings of the good and the reward arranged for deeds. The fifth principle: the comprehensive counsel on the manner of journeying and traveling to Allah, which suffices for everything while nothing suffices for it. So may the blessings and peace of Allah be upon him who was given the most concise, comprehensive, and beneficial of speech."
"29","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The right of the Muslim upon the Muslim is six.» It was said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: «When you meet him, greet him; when he invites you, answer him; when he seeks your counsel, counsel him; when he sneezes and praises Allah, respond to him (with tashmit); when he falls ill, visit him; and when he dies, follow him.» Reported by Muslim.
These six rights — whoever fulfills them toward the Muslims, his fulfillment of others is even more expected; and he achieves the performance of these duties and rights in which there is much good and great reward from Allah. The first: «When you meet him, greet him.» For the greeting is a cause of love, which necessitates faith, which necessitates entering Paradise, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I not guide you to something which, if you do it, you will love one another? Spread the greeting among you.» The greeting is among the beauties of Islam, for each of the two who meet supplicates for the other for safety from evils, and for mercy and blessing that bring every good. There follows of cheerfulness and suitable words of greeting what necessitates harmony and love and removes estrangement and breaking off. The greeting is a right of the Muslim, and upon the one greeted is responding to the greeting with its like or better; and the best of people are those who initiate them with the greeting. The second: «When he invites you, answer him» (the right of the Muslim upon the Muslim) — that is, when he invites you to an invitation of food or drink, comfort the heart of your brother who has favored you and honored you with the invitation, and answer him in that, unless you have an excuse. The third, his saying: «When he seeks your counsel, counsel him» — that is, when he consults you about one of the deeds, whether to do it or not, counsel him with what you would love for yourself. If the deed is beneficial in every way, urge him to do it; if it is harmful, warn him against it; if it combines benefit and harm, explain that to him and weigh the interests against the harms. Likewise, if he consults you about dealing with someone among the people, or marrying him or marrying from him, exert for him your pure counsel, and form for him the opinion you would form for yourself. Beware of deceiving him in any of that, for whoever deceives the Muslims is not of them, and has abandoned the duty of counsel. This counsel is obligatory absolutely, but it is emphasized when he seeks your counsel and asks of you the beneficial opinion; for this reason he restricted it to this emphasized case. The explanation of the hadith «Religion is sincere counsel» has already been given, which suffices for repeating the discussion. The fourth, his saying: «When he sneezes and praises Allah, respond to him» (the right of the Muslim upon the Muslim). That is because sneezing is a blessing from Allah, through the exit of this confined air in the parts of the human body — Allah has facilitated for it an outlet through which it exits, and the sneezer is relieved. So it is legislated for him to praise Allah for this blessing, and legislated for his brother to say to him, ""May Allah have mercy on you""; and he is commanded to answer him with his saying, ""May Allah guide you and set right your affairs."" Whoever does not praise Allah does not deserve tashmit, and let him blame none but himself; for it is he who has forfeited for himself the two blessings: the blessing of praising Allah, and the blessing of his brother's supplication for him consequent upon the praise. The fifth, his saying: «When he falls ill, visit him.» Visiting the sick is among the rights of the Muslim, especially one who has a confirmed right over you, such as a relative, a companion, and the like. It is among the best of righteous deeds. Whoever visits his sick Muslim brother keeps wading in mercy; when he sits with him, mercy covers him. Whoever visits him at the beginning of the day, the angels bless him until evening; whoever visits him at the end of the day, the angels bless him until morning. The visitor should supplicate for him for healing, give him hope, cheer his heart with glad tidings of recovery, remind him of repentance and turning to Allah and of the beneficial bequest, and not prolong sitting with him — but (stay) to the measure of the visit — unless the sick person prefers his frequent visits and prolonged sitting with him; for every occasion has its due speech. The sixth, his saying: «When he dies, follow him.» Whoever follows a funeral until he prays over it has a qirat of reward; if he follows it until it is buried, he has two qirats. Following the funeral contains a right of Allah, a right of the deceased, and a right of his living relatives."
"30","[AI-translation] From Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «When the servant falls ill or travels, what he used to do while healthy and resident is recorded for him.» Reported by al-Bukhari.
This is among the greatest favors of Allah upon His believing servants: that their continuous, habitual deeds, when illness or travel cuts them off from them, are all recorded for them in full; for Allah knows of them that, but for that impediment, they would have done them. So He Almighty gives them, by their intentions, the like of the rewards of the workers — along with the special reward of illness, and along with what comes with it of upholding the function of patience, or what is more perfect than that of contentment and thanks, and of humility before Allah and brokenness to Him; and along with what the traveler does of deeds he perhaps would not do in residence: teaching, counsel, or guiding to a religious or worldly interest — especially in virtuous journeys such as jihad, Hajj, Umrah, and the like. Entering into this hadith is that whoever performs worship in a deficient manner while unable to perform it in the most complete manner, Allah completes for him by his intention what he would have done had he been able; for inability regarding the complements of worship is a kind of illness. And Allah knows best. Whoever intends a good deed but occupies himself with another deed better than it, while he cannot combine the two matters, is even more deserving that there be recorded for him that deed which a better deed kept him from — indeed, even if he occupied himself with its like. And Allah's bounty is immense."
"31","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Hasten with the funeral; if it is righteous, it is good that you advance it to; and if it is other than that, it is evil that you lay down from your necks.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains matters of principles and of branches. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «Hasten with the funeral» includes hastening in washing it, shrouding it, carrying it, and burying it, and all matters of preparation. For this reason these matters are among the communal obligations. Excepted from this hastening is when delay involves a preponderant benefit, such as when one dies suddenly: it becomes necessary to delay him until his death is verified, lest he have been struck by a seizure. It is also fitting to delay him for a large gathering, or for the arrival of one who has a right over him, such as a relative and the like. He gave as the reason for that the benefit of the deceased — advancing him to what is good for him of bliss — or the benefit of the living, in swiftness in removing evil. If this is commanded in matters of his preparation, then hastening to clear his liability of debts and rights owed by him is even more appropriate, for he is needier for that. In it is encouragement to care for the affair of your Muslim brother, alive and dead, and to hasten to what is good for him in his religion and his worldly life; just as in it is encouragement to keep away from the causes of evil and to keep distant from wrongdoers, even in the situation in which a person is tried with dealing with them. In this hadith is affirmation of the bliss and punishment of the Barzakh; the hadiths from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to that effect are successive (mutawatir); and the beginning of that is his placement in his grave when his burial is complete. For this reason it is legislated in this situation to stand at his grave, supplicate for him, seek forgiveness, and ask Allah for firmness for him. In this is also a reminder of the causes of the bliss and punishment of the Barzakh: the causes of bliss are righteousness, in accordance with his saying, «If it is righteous.» Righteousness is a comprehensive word containing belief in Allah and His Messenger, and obedience to Allah and His Messenger: belief in the reports, compliance with the commands, and avoidance of the prohibitions. The punishment is caused by failure in righteousness: either doubt in religion, or boldness toward prohibitions, or abandoning any of the duties and obligations. All the detailed causes in hadiths and reports go back to that. For this reason He Almighty said: «None shall enter it but the most wretched — who denied and turned away» (Qur'an Al-Layl 15-16): he denied the reports and turned away from the commands."
"32","[AI-translation] From Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «There is no charity (sadaqah) due on less than five awsuq of dates; there is no charity due on less than five uqiyyahs of silver (wariq); and there is no charity due on less than five dhawd (of camels).» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith comprises the determination of the minimum amounts (nisabs) of the predominant zakatable kinds of wealth in which zakah is obligatory: grains, fruits, livestock of the three grazing kinds, cash, and what branches from them of trade goods. As for the zakah of grains and fruits: the text of this hadith is that their minimum is five awsuq; what is less than that has no zakah in it. The wasq is sixty sa' by the sa' of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the five awsuq are three hundred sa'. Whoever's grains of his crop or yield of his fruit reaches this amount or more, its zakah is due from him: half a tenth for what is irrigated with cost, and a tenth for what is irrigated without cost. As for the zakah of livestock: there is nothing due on fewer than five camels. When they reach five, a sheep is due for them; then for every five, a sheep, up to twenty-five — in which a bint makhad, which has completed a year, is due. In thirty-six, a bint labun, which is two years old; in forty-six, a hiqqah, which is three years old; in sixty-one, a jadha'ah, which is four years old; in seventy-six, two bint labuns; in ninety-one, two hiqqahs. When they exceed one hundred and twenty, then for every forty a bint labun, and for every fifty a hiqqah. As for the minimum of cattle: thirty — in which a tabi' or tabi'ah, one year old; in forty, a musinnah, two years old; then for every thirty a tabi', and for every forty a musinnah. As for the minimum of sheep and goats: its least is forty — in which a sheep; in one hundred and twenty-one, two sheep; in two hundred and one, three sheep; then for every hundred, a sheep. What is between the (stipulated) obligations is called ""waqs"" — in livestock specifically; nothing is due on it, but it is pardoned. As for the remaining animals — horses, mules, donkeys, and others — there is no zakah on them, unless they are prepared for sale and purchase. As for the minimum of cash of silver: its least is five uqiyyahs; the uqiyyah is forty dirhams. Whenever one has two hundred dirhams, a quarter-tenth is due on it. Likewise what branches from the two currencies of trade goods — everything prepared for sale and purchase for gain and profit: it is valued at the turn of the year at the value of currency, and a quarter-tenth is paid on it. A complete year is required for all of them, except grains and fruits, whose zakah is paid at the time of harvest and cutting. He, exalted be He, said: «Give its due on the day of its harvest» (Qur'an Al-An'am 6:141). These are the kinds of wealth in which zakah is obligatory. As for its disbursement: it is for the eight categories mentioned in His saying, exalted be He: «Charities are only for the poor, the needy, those employed upon it, those whose hearts are to be reconciled, for (freeing) necks, the debtors, in the cause of Allah, and the wayfarer — an ordinance from Allah; and Allah is Knowing, Wise» (Qur'an At-Tawbah 9:60)."
"33","[AI-translation] From Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whoever seeks chastity, Allah will make him chaste; whoever seeks sufficiency, Allah will enrich him; whoever strives to be patient, Allah will make him patient. No one has been given a gift better and vaster than patience.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith comprises four comprehensive, beneficial sentences. The first of them: his saying, «Whoever seeks chastity, Allah will make him chaste.» The second: his saying, «Whoever seeks sufficiency, Allah will enrich him.» These two sentences are inseparable, for the perfection of the servant lies in his sincerity toward Allah — in desire, fear, and attachment to Him to the exclusion of created beings. He must strive to realize this perfection and work at every means that conveys him to it, until he becomes a servant of Allah in truth, free from servitude to created beings. That is by struggling against his soul in two matters: turning it away from attachment to created beings by seeking chastity regarding what is in their hands — not seeking it by his speech nor by the language of his state. For this reason he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to 'Umar: «What comes to you of this wealth without your aspiring to it or asking, take it; otherwise, do not let your soul follow it.» Cutting off aspiration in the heart and asking by the tongue — in chastity and elevation above the favors of created beings and attachment of the heart to them — is a strong cause for attaining chastity. Its completion is that one struggle against one's soul in the second matter: contentment with Allah and trust in His sufficiency, for whoever relies upon Allah, He suffices him. This is the aim, and the first is a means to it. Whoever seeks chastity regarding what is in people's hands and what he may obtain from them, that necessitates strengthening his attachment to Allah, his hope and aspiration for the bounty and kindness of Allah; and he thinks well of his Lord and trusts Him. Allah, exalted be He, is as His servant thinks of Him: if he thinks good, it is his; if he thinks otherwise, it is his. Each of the two matters extends the other and strengthens it: the stronger his attachment to Allah grows, the weaker his attachment to created beings grows, and vice versa. Among the supplications of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is: «O Allah, I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity, and sufficiency.» He combined all good in this supplication. Guidance is beneficial knowledge; piety is righteous deeds and abandoning all prohibitions — this is the soundness of religion. Its completion is through the soundness of the heart and its tranquility through chastity toward created beings and sufficiency with Allah. Whoever is rich with Allah is truly rich, though his resources be few; for wealth is not abundance of goods — wealth is wealth of heart. With chastity and sufficiency are completed for the servant the good life, worldly bliss, and contentment with what Allah has given him. The third is his saying: «Whoever strives to be patient, Allah will make him patient.» Then he mentioned in the fourth sentence that patience — when Allah gives it to the servant — is the best, vastest, and greatest of gifts, as an aid in matters. He, exalted be He, said: «Seek help through patience and prayer» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:45) — that is, in all your affairs. Patience, like the rest of morals, needs struggling against the soul and training it; for this reason he said: «Whoever strives to be patient» — that is, struggles against his soul for patience — «Allah will make him patient» and aid him. Patience is the greatest of gifts only because it relates to all the servant's affairs and perfections; every state of his states needs patience. He needs patience in obeying Allah, until he performs it and discharges it; patience against disobeying Allah, until he abandons it for Allah; patience with the painful decrees of Allah, without resenting them; and even patience with the blessings of Allah and the beloved things of the soul — not letting the soul exult and rejoice with blameworthy joy, but occupying himself with gratitude to Allah. In all his states he needs patience, and through patience he attains success. For this reason Allah mentioned the people of Paradise, saying: «And the angels will enter upon them from every gate: ""Peace be upon you for that which you patiently endured; how excellent is the final home!""» (Qur'an Ar-Ra'd 23-24). Likewise His saying: «Those will be rewarded with the high chamber for their patience» (Qur'an Al-Furqan 25:75). They attained Paradise with its bliss and reached the stations through patience. But the servant asks Allah for well-being from trials whose outcome he does not know; then, when it comes upon him, his duty is patience. Well-being is what is sought fundamentally in matters of trial and testing; patience is commanded when its causes and attachments are present; and Allah is the Helper. Allah has promised the patient in His Book and upon the tongue of His Messenger lofty, glorious matters. He promised them aid in all their affairs; that He is with them with care, guidance, and direction; that He loves them, firms their hearts and their feet, casts upon them serenity and tranquility, eases acts of obedience for them, protects them from violations, bestows upon them blessings, mercy, and guidance at times of calamity; that Allah raises them to the highest stations in this world and the Hereafter; He promised them victory, that He will ease them toward ease and spare them hardship; He promised them happiness, success, and triumph; that He will pay them their reward without reckoning; that He will replace for them in this world more than what He took from them of beloved things, and better — compensating them for the occurrence of hated things with an immediate recompense counterbalancing many times over what befell them of misfortune and calamity. In its beginning it is difficult and severe; in its ending it is easy, of praiseworthy outcomes — as it was said: Patience is like its name, bitter in taste ... but its consequences are sweeter than honey."
"34","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Charity does not decrease wealth, Allah does not increase a servant through pardon except in honour, and no one humbles himself for Allah except that Allah raises him."" Reported by Muslim.
This hadith contains the virtue of charity, pardon, and humility, and an explanation of their immediate and ultimate fruits, and that everything that one might imagine — of charity decreasing wealth, of pardon contradicting honour, and of humility contradicting elevation — is a mistaken illusion and a false assumption. For charity does not decrease wealth, for even if it is supposed to decrease from one angle, it has increased from other angles: charity blesses wealth, protects it from calamities, develops it, and opens for the charitable person doors of provision and means of increase that are not opened for others. So can that decrease compare with some of these magnificent fruits? Charity for Allah, given in its proper place, absolutely does not exhaust wealth nor decrease it, by the text of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and by observed and well-known experiences — all this apart from what its owner has with Allah of abundant reward, good, and elevation. As for pardoning the offences of wrongdoers by their words and deeds: no humiliation should be imagined from it; rather this is honour itself, for honour is elevation with Allah and with His creation, together with the ability to overpower opponents and enemies. And it is well known what the pardoner obtains of good and praise among people, the turning of an enemy into a friend, people siding with the pardoner and supporting him by word and deed against his opponent, and Allah's dealing with him according to the nature of his deed, for whoever pardons Allah's servants, Allah pardons him. Likewise the one who is humble for Allah and for His servants: Allah raises him in degrees, for Allah mentioned elevation in His saying: ""Allah raises those of you who believe and those given knowledge in degrees"" (Qur'an Al-Mujadila 58:11). Among the finest fruits of knowledge and faith is humility, for it is complete submission to the truth and subservience to the command of Allah and His Messenger, in compliance with commands and avoidance of prohibitions, together with humility toward Allah's servants, lowering the wing to them, and showing consideration to the young and the old, the noble and the lowly; its opposite is arrogance, which is rejecting the truth and despising people. These three mentioned in this hadith are the forerunners of the qualities of the doers of good: this one does good with his wealth and relieves the needs of the needy, this one does good by pardoning the offences of wrongdoers, and this one does good to them by his forbearance, his humility, and his good character with all people. These have embraced people with their character and beneficence, and Allah raised them so that they attained the noblest station among servants, besides what Allah stores for them of reward. And in his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""and no one humbles himself for Allah"" is an alert to good intention and sincerity to Allah in his humility, for many people may display humility to the rich in order to obtain something of their worldly goods, or to leaders in order to attain through them what they seek, and one may display humility for show and repute. All these are corrupt aims; nothing benefits the servant except humility for Allah, as a means of drawing near to Him, seeking His reward, and doing good to creation, for the perfection of beneficence and its spirit is sincerity to Allah."
"35","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every deed of the son of Adam is multiplied: the good deed by ten times its like up to seven hundredfold. Allah the Exalted said: except fasting, for it is Mine, and I reward for it; he leaves his desire and his food for My sake. The fasting person has two joys: a joy at his breaking of fast, and a joy at meeting his Lord. The khuluf of the mouth of the fasting person is better with Allah than the scent of musk. Fasting is a shield, and when it is the fasting day of one of you, let him not utter foul speech nor clamour; if someone insults him or fights him, let him say: I am a fasting man."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
How great this hadith is, for it mentioned deeds in general, then fasting in particular, and mentioned its virtue and characteristics, its immediate and ultimate reward, an explanation of its wisdom, its intended purpose, and the excellent manners befitting it — all contained in this hadith, so it clarified this comprehensive principle. It clarified that all righteous deeds — of words and acts, outward or inward, whether connected to the right of Allah or the rights of servants — are multiplied from ten to seven hundredfold, to many more multiples. This is among the greatest proofs of the vastness of Allah's bounty and His kindness to His believing servants, since He made their single misdeeds and violations requited with a single recompense, and Allah's forgiveness is beyond that. As for the good deed, the least multiplication is that one counts as ten, and it may increase beyond that for reasons, among them: the strength of the doer's faith and the perfection of his sincerity, for the stronger faith and sincerity grow, the more the reward of the deed is multiplied. Among them: that the deed has great significance, like spending in jihad and knowledge, and public religious projects, and like a deed whose merit is great through its excellence, its strength, and its overcoming of obstacles, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in the story of the people of the cave, and the story of the prostitute who gave water to the dog, for Allah thanked her and forgave her, and like a deed that yields other deeds, so others follow his example or another shares in it, and like averting grave necessities and achieving great acts of kindness, and like multiplication due to the virtue of time or place, or of the doer with Allah. All these multiplications cover every deed. In this hadith fasting was excepted, and it was ascribed to Him, and He Himself rewards for it out of His sheer bounty and generosity, without matching the deed with the mentioned multiplication shared by other deeds. This is something that cannot be expressed — rather He rewards them with what no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and has not occurred to a human heart. And in the hadith is as it were an alert to the wisdom of this particularization: that the fasting person, having left the beloved things of the soul upon whose love it is fashioned, preferring them over all else, though they are among necessary matters, yet the fasting person preferred over them the love of his Lord, and left them for Allah in a state in which none but Allah observes him, so his love for Allah became foremost and overpowering over every love of the soul, and seeking His pleasure and reward put ahead of attaining selfish aims — for this reason Allah singled it out for Himself, and made the fasting person's reward with Him. So what do you think of a wage and recompense guaranteed by the Most Merciful, the Generous, the Bestower, whose gifts encompass all beings, and Who singled out His allies among them with the fullest share and the most complete portion, and decreed for them means and graces by which they attain what He has, in ways that do not cross the mind nor revolve in imagination? So what do you think Allah will do with these sincere fasting people? Here the pen stops, and the fasting person's heart roams in joy and delight at a deed Allah singled out for Himself and made its reward from His sheer bounty and pure kindness — and that is Allah's bounty He gives to whom He wills, and Allah is Possessor of great bounty. The hadith indicates that complete fasting is that in which the servant leaves two things: the physical nullifiers, of food, drink, intercourse and their concomitants, and the practical diminishers: so he does not utter foul speech nor clamour, nor do a forbidden deed, nor speak forbidden speech; rather he avoids all sins, and all disputes and quarrels that produce rancour. For this reason he said: ""let him not utter rafath"" i.e.: let him not speak ugly speech, ""nor clamour"" with speech that produces trials and disputes, as he said in the other hadith: ""Whoever does not leave false speech and acting upon it, Allah has no need for him to leave his food and drink."" Whoever realizes both matters — leaving the nullifiers and leaving the prohibitions — the reward of fasting people is completed for him, and whoever does not do that, let him blame none but himself. Then he guided the fasting person, when someone confronts him wanting to dispute and revile him, to say to him with his tongue: ""I am fasting."" The benefit of that: intending as it were to say: know that I am not unable to answer you for what you say, but I am fasting; I respect my fast and observe its perfection, and the command of Allah and His Messenger, and know that fasting calls me to leave requital and urges me to patience; so what I have done is better and higher than what you have done to me, O disputant. And in it: care for all deeds, of fasting and otherwise, and observing their completion, distancing from all that diminishes them, and recalling the requirements of the deed and what it imposes on the doer when causes that violate the deed occur. And his saying: ""Fasting is a shield"" i.e.: a protection by which the servant guards against sins in this world and trains in good, and a protection from punishment. This is among the greatest wisdoms of the Lawgiver, of the benefits of fasting, and that is because of His saying: ""O you who believe, fasting is prescribed for you as it was prescribed for those before you, that you may be God-conscious"" (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:183). The fact that fasting is a shield and a cause for attaining God-consciousness is the sum of the wisdoms detailed concerning the wisdom of fasting and its benefits, for it prevents forbidden things or lessens them, and urges many acts of obedience. And his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""The fasting person has two joys: a joy at his breaking of fast, and a joy at meeting his Lord."" These are two rewards: immediate and delayed. The immediate: witnessed — when the fasting person breaks his fast he rejoices at Allah's blessing upon him in completing the fast, and rejoices at attaining the desires withheld during the day. And the delayed: joy at meeting his Lord through His pleasure and honour. This hastened joy is a sample of that deferred joy, and Allah will combine both for the fasting person. And in it: an indication that when the fasting person nears his breaking of fast and this joy occurs to him, it matches what passed during his day of the hardship of leaving desires, so it is a form of encouragement and rousing of resolve toward good. And his saying: ""The khuluf of the mouth of the fasting person is better with Allah than the scent of musk."" Al-khuluf is the trace in the mouth of the odour of the stomach when empty of food and vapours rise; though it is disliked by souls, do not grieve, O fasting person, for it is better with Allah than the scent of musk, for it results from His worship and drawing near to Him, and everything resulting from acts of worship, of hardships and unpleasant things, is beloved to Allah, and what Allah loves is, with the believer, put ahead of everything."
"36","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah said: Whoever shows enmity to a friend of Mine, I have declared war on him. My servant does not draw near to Me with anything more beloved to Me than what I have obligated upon him. My servant continues to draw near to Me with supererogatory acts until I love him; when I love him, I am his hearing with which he hears, his sight with which he sees, his hand with which he strikes, and his foot with which he walks. If he asks Me, I will surely give him, and if he seeks refuge in Me, I will surely grant him refuge. And I have not hesitated in anything I do as I hesitate in taking the soul of the believer: he dislikes death, and I dislike distressing him, but he must needs meet it."" Reported by al-Bukhari.
This is a momentous hadith, the noblest hadith on the descriptions of the allies of Allah, their virtue and their stations. He reported that enmity toward His allies is enmity toward Him and war against Him. Whoever sets himself up for enmity toward the Lord and war against the Master of the Kingdom is forsaken, and whoever is guarded by Allah's defence is victorious — and that is because of the perfect conformity of Allah's allies to Allah in the things He loves, so He loved them, undertook their sufficiency, and sufficed them in what concerned them. Then He mentioned the complete description of the allies: that Allah's allies are those who first draw near to Allah by performing the obligatory and the supererogatory: prayer, fasting, zakat, hajj, enjoining good and forbidding evil, jihad, and upholding His rights and the rights of His servants that are obligatory. Then they moved from this degree to drawing near to Him with supererogatory acts, for of every kind of obligatory worship there is legislated of its kind supererogatory acts, with great virtues that complete the obligatory acts and complete their reward. So Allah's allies upheld the obligatory and the supererogatory, and He took them under His guardianship, loved them, made easy for them every path leading to His pleasure, and granted them success and right guidance in all their movements: if they hear, they hear by Allah; if they see, it is for Allah; if they strike or walk, it is in obedience to Allah. Along with His guiding them in their movements, He made them ones whose supplication is answered: if they ask Him, He gives them the interests of their religion and worldly life, and if they seek refuge in Him from evils, He grants them refuge. Along with that, He was gentle with them in all their states; and had He not decreed death upon His servants, His allies would have been spared it, for they dislike it due to its hardship and gravity, and Allah dislikes distressing them — but since the decree is effective, they must needs meet it. So He clarified in this hadith the description of the allies, their diverse virtues, Allah's love for them — which is the greatest thing competitors compete for — and that He is with them, aiding, supporting, guiding them, and answering their supplications. This hadith indicates the affirmation of Allah's love and its varying degrees for His allies according to their stations. The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) description of Allah's allies by performing the obligatory acts and multiplying supererogatory acts matches Allah's description of them by faith and God-consciousness in His saying: ""Indeed, the allies of Allah — no fear is upon them, nor do they grieve — those who believed and were God-conscious"" (Qur'an Yunus 10:62-63). Everyone who is a believer, God-conscious, is an ally of Allah, for faith covers beliefs, acts of hearts and limbs, and God-consciousness is leaving all forbidden things. It indicates a great principle: that the obligatory acts take precedence over the supererogatory, are more beloved to Allah, and greater in wage and reward, because of His saying: ""My servant does not draw near to Me with anything more beloved to Me than what I have obligated upon him,"" and that when they compete, the obligatory must be given precedence over the supererogatory."
"37","[AI-translation] From Hakim ibn Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The two parties to a sale have the choice so long as they have not parted. If they are truthful and disclose, their sale is blessed for them; if they lie and conceal, the blessing of their sale is effaced."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith is a foundation for explaining beneficial transactions and harmful transactions, and that the divider between the two kinds is truthfulness and disclosure. Whoever is truthful in his transaction and discloses everything on which the transaction depends, of intended attributes and of defects and deficiencies — this is a transaction beneficial in the present through compliance with the command of Allah and His Messenger, safety from sin, and the descent of blessing in his transaction, and in the future through obtaining reward and safety from punishment. Whoever lies and conceals defects and the attributes in contracts — along with his sin, his transaction is effaced of blessing. Whenever blessing is removed from a transaction, its owner loses his worldly life and his Hereafter. From this principle is derived the prohibition of concealment, hiding defects, the prohibition of cheating, and shortchanging in weights, measures, cubits, and the like, for these are of lying and concealment. Likewise the prohibition of najsh and deception in transactions, and of meeting caravans before they reach the market to sell to them or buy from them. Entering into it is: lying about the amount of price and priced goods, about the description of the contracted object, and other than that. The criterion of that is that everything you would dislike your Muslim brother or another to deal with you in without informing you of it is of the chapter of lying, concealment, and cheating. Entering into this are: sale in its kinds, leases, partnerships, and all exchanges, their terms and their documents. In all of them truthfulness and disclosure are incumbent upon the servant, and lying and concealment are not lawful for him. In this hadith is affirmation of the option of the session in sale: that each of the two contracting parties has the choice between concluding or annulling, so long as they are in the place of contracting. When they part, the sale is established and binding, and neither of them has the choice after that except for a cause necessitating annulment — such as a stipulated option, a defect found that was hidden from him, cheating, or inability to know price or priced goods. The wisdom in affirming the option of the session is that sale occurs very often, and a person often regrets his selling or buying; so the Lawgiver granted him the choice, that he may deliberate and consider his state: whether to conclude or annul. And Allah knows best."
"38","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the pebble sale and the sale involving gharar."" Reported by Muslim.
This is comprehensive speech covering every gharar, and what is meant by gharar is risk and ignorance. That falls under gambling, for gambling enters into contests and wagers — except wagers on horse, camel, and arrow racing — and likewise enters into matters of transactions. Every sale involving risk as to whether the sold item will be obtained or not — like selling a runaway, stray, or usurped thing from other than its usurper, or from one unable to take it, and like selling what is in people's liabilities — especially procrastinators and insolvents — falls under gharar. Likewise every sale involving apparent ignorance in which the intended object varies falls under the sale involving gharar, like his selling what is in his house of goods, or what is in his shop, or what is in this place, while he does not know it, or the pebble sale, which is an example of examples of gharar — as when one says: throw this pebble, and whatever goods it falls upon is yours for such-and-such, or throw it on the ground and whatever distance it reaches is yours for such-and-such — or the sale by throwing, or by touching, or the sale of what is in the bellies of livestock, and the like: all of that is clear gharar. Of the wisdom of the Lawgiver is the prohibition of this kind, because of the risks in it and the enmities it produces, in which one of the two may be cheated by the other with a gross, harmful cheating. For this reason the scholars stipulated for a sale: knowledge of the sold item and knowledge of the price. They also stipulated that the contractor be one whose disposal is valid — adult, sane, prudent — for a contract with a minor or an imprudent person must involve harmful cheating, and that is of gharar. Likewise they stipulated knowledge of the term when the price or part of it, or the sold item in salam, is deferred, for ignorance of the term makes the contract gharar. And as the gharar sale they agree upon enters the prohibition, then with greater reason enters deception, and one of the two concealing from the other something of the matters of the transaction: of the object contracted for or upon, or something of its attributes. All cheating falls under deception. The instances of cheating and its details cannot be delimited, and they are known among people. In sum, the gharar sale goes back to the sale of the non-existent, like the sale of the unborn offspring of an unborn camel, or of years ahead, or the sale of what one is unable to deliver, like the runaway and the like, or the sale of the absolutely unknown in its essence, its kind, or its attributes."
"39","[AI-translation] From Amr ibn Awf al-Muzani (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: ""Settlement is permissible among Muslims, except a settlement that forbids the lawful or permits the unlawful. And Muslims are bound by their stipulations, except a stipulation that forbids the lawful or permits the unlawful."" Reported by the authors of the Sunan except al-Nasa'i.
He combined in this noble hadith the kinds of settlement and stipulations — sound and corrupt — in speech covering countless kinds and instances of knowledge, with a clear boundary. He reported that the basis concerning settlement is that it is permissible, there is no harm in it, except if it forbids the lawful or permits the unlawful. This is encompassing speech, covering all divisions of settlement. Settlement is good, because of the settlement of dispute, soundness of hearts, and clearance of liabilities in it. There enters: settlement in wealth upon acknowledgment — where one acknowledges to him a debt, an object, or a right, and he settles with him for part of it or for something else. And settlement upon denial — where one claims against him a right of a debt or an object, and he denies; then they agree on settlement for this with an object or a debt, a benefit or a release, or otherwise: all of that is permissible. Likewise settlement concerning unknown rights — as when between two persons there is a long transaction in which the establishment of a right against one of them or both is unclear, or its amount is unclear, and they settle upon what they agree, seeking justice. Its completion is that each of them releases the other, or when between two persons there is partnership in inheritance, endowment, bequest, or other wealth — of debts or objects — then they settle concerning that with what they see as nearest to justice and right. Likewise there enters: settlement between the spouses concerning a marital right — of maintenance, clothing, housing, or otherwise, past or present — even if the situation requires one of them to overlook part of his right, to obtain the rest, or to preserve the marriage, or to remove discord, or for other aims: all of that is good, as He the Exalted said concerning them: ""There is no blame upon them if they settle between them a settlement, and settlement is best"" (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:128). Likewise settlement concerning retaliation for souls or limbs for wealth they agree upon, or compensation for blood-money of souls, limbs, and wounds, or the judge settling between litigants with what the situation requires, seeking the interest of both. All this falls under his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""Settlement is permissible among Muslims."" If the settlement includes forbidding the lawful or permitting the unlawful, it is corrupt by the text of this hadith, like settlement upon enslaving free persons, or permitting forbidden private parts, or a settlement involving injustice. For this reason Allah qualified it by His saying: ""So settle between them with justice, and act equitably. Indeed, Allah loves the equitable"" (Qur'an Al-Hujurat 49:9). Or a coerced settlement, like one compelled, or like a woman whose husband wrongfully constrains her so that she ransoms herself from him, or settlement over another's right without his permission, and the like — this kind is a forbidden, invalid settlement. As for stipulations: he reported in this hadith that Muslims are bound by their stipulations, except a stipulation that permits the unlawful or forbids the lawful. This is a great principle, for stipulations are what one of the two contractors stipulates upon the other, in which he has a share and an interest; that is permissible, and binding when the other agrees to it and acknowledges it. That is like when the buyer stipulates in the sold item an intended attribute, such as stipulating that the slave is a scribe or excels in such-and-such work, or the animal is easy-paced or milch, or the hunting animal catches prey, or the slave-girl is virgin or beautiful or has such-and-such intended attribute. And like the buyer stipulating that the price or part of it be deferred to a named term, or one selling a thing while the seller stipulates benefiting from it for a known period — as Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari sold his camel to the Prophet and stipulated its back to Madinah. And like stipulating residence in the house or shop for a known period, or use of the vessel for a known period, and the like. Likewise stipulations of pledge, guarantee, and surety are of the sound, binding stipulations. And like the stipulations the partners stipulate in mudaraba, inan partnership, wujuh, abdan, musaqat, or muzaraa: all are sound, except stipulations that permit the unlawful, and its converse, such as those going back to ignorance and gharar. And like the stipulations of endowers and testators in their endowments and bequests, of intended stipulations: all are sound, so long as they do not enter into the unlawful. Likewise stipulations between the spouses, such as her stipulating her house or town, or a specified maintenance or the like, for the most deserving stipulations to be fulfilled are of this kind."
"40","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Delay by a rich person is injustice. When one of you is transferred to a solvent person, let him transfer."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith includes the command of good fulfilment and good collection, and the prohibition of what contradicts both or one of them. His saying: ""Delay by a rich person is injustice"" i.e.: stalling in discharging a due right is injustice, for it is abandoning the duty of justice, since the able person must hasten to discharge what is upon him, without making the right-holder need to ask, insist, or complain. Whoever does that while able to fulfil is unjust. ""The rich"" is one who has financial assets with which he can fulfil. The understood meaning of the hadith: the insolvent bears no blame for delay, and Allah obligated the right-holder to grant him respite until ease. And we understand from this hadith that financial injustice is not restricted to taking another's wealth without right; rather there enters every aggression against another's wealth or his right in whatever form. Whoever usurps another's wealth, steals it, denies a right he holds for another or part of it, claims against him what is not his, of the basis of the right or its attribute, stalls him in his right from time to time, or delivers to him less than is due to him in his liability — in attribute or amount — all these are unjust according to their states. And injustice is darknesses on the Day of Resurrection upon its people. Then he mentioned in the other sentence good collection: that the right-holder should pursue his companion with kindness and ease, not with harassment nor hardship, nor burden him with difficulty in his affair, nor refuse him when he directs him to a quarter with no harm or loss in it for him. So when he transfers him in his right to a solvent person — i.e.: one able to fulfil, neither stalling nor refusing — let him transfer to him, for this is of good collection and magnanimity. For this reason Allah the Exalted mentioned both matters in His saying: ""But whoever is pardoned something by his brother, then pursuit with kindness and payment to him with excellence"" (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:178). So He commanded the right-holder to pursue the one upon whom the right lies with kindness, what is approved by custom and reason, and the one upon whom the right lies to pay with excellence. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for whoever is characterized by this beautiful attribute, saying: ""May Allah have mercy on a servant magnanimous when he sells, magnanimous when he buys, magnanimous when he pays, magnanimous when he collects."" Magnanimity in conducting transactions, in paying and in collecting, its owner may hope for every good, religious and worldly, for falling under this blessed supplication, which must be accepted. And that has been witnessed with the eyes: you find no merchant with this attribute except that you see Allah pouring provision upon him and sending down blessing upon him. The opposite is the owner of stalling and hardship, burdening transactors — and recompense is of the nature of the deed, so the recompense of easing is easing. And since delay by a rich person is injustice, he must be compelled to discharge the right when his creditor complains of him; if he pays, well and good, otherwise he is disciplined until he pays or his creditor pardons. Whenever he causes his creditor to incur costs due to his complaint, he bears the cost of what was taken of his wealth, for he is the cause, and that without right. Likewise everyone who causes another to incur costs unjustly bears liability. This hadith is a foundation in the chapter of transfer of debt: that whoever is transferred in his right to a solvent person must transfer, and may not refuse. Its understood meaning: when transferred to a non-solvent person, he need not transfer, because of the harm in it to him. The right transferred is debts established in liabilities, of loan, price of a sold item, or otherwise. When he is transferred to the solvent person and transfers to him, the liability of the transferor is cleared, and the creditor's right moves to the one to whom he was transferred. And Allah knows best."
"41","[AI-translation] From Samura ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The hand is liable for what it takes until it delivers it."" Reported by the authors of the Sunan except al-Nasa'i.
This covers what one takes of people's wealth without right, like usurpation and the like, and what one takes with right, like pledge and hire. As for the first division, it is usurpation: taking another's wealth without right, without his consent, and it is among the greatest injustices and prohibitions, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: ""Whoever usurps a handspan of land will be collared with it on the Day of Resurrection from seven earths."" The usurper must return what he took, even if he incurs multiples of its value in returning it, and even if he suffers harm in returning it, for he is the one who brought harm upon himself. If it is deficient, he returns it with compensation for its deficiency. Its hire for the period it remained in his hand is upon him, and if it perishes, he guarantees it. And when the hand took another's wealth with its owner's consent, by hire, pledge, mudaraba, musaqat, muzaraa, or otherwise, the holder is a trustee, for the owner of the object entrusted him: if it perishes in his hand without transgression or negligence, there is no liability upon him; if it perishes through negligence in guarding it or transgression against it, he guarantees it; and when its purpose ends, he returns it to its owner. There enters under this hadith ""the hand is liable for what it takes until it delivers it."" Likewise the borrowed object: the borrower must return it to its owner when its purpose ends or its owner requests it, for borrowing is a permissible, not binding, contract. If the borrowed object perishes without transgression or negligence, some scholars held him liable, as is the well-known view of the school of Imam Ahmad; some did not hold him liable, like other trustees; and some distinguished: if its liability was stipulated, he is liable, otherwise not — and this is the best of the three views. But if wealth is found in the hand of an insane person, an imbecile, or a minor, and he took it to guard it, and it perished in his hand without transgression or negligence, then he is a doer of good, and there is no way against the doers of good. If one took a found object whose picking up is permissible, he must announce it for a full year. If it is not recognized, it belongs to its finder; if its owner is found after that and describes it, he delivers it to him if it exists, and guarantees it if he consumed it by use or otherwise. If it perished during the year of announcement without negligence or transgression, there is no liability upon the finder, for he is of the trustees, and it had not then entered his ownership. And Allah knows best."
"42","[AI-translation] From Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed preemption in everything undivided. When boundaries fall and roads are diverted, there is no preemption."" Reported by al-Bukhari.
Taken from this hadith are all the rulings of preemption: in what there is preemption and in what there is none. Preemption is only in jointly owned wealth, and it is two divisions: real estate and other. In this hadith he affirmed preemption in real estate, and it indicates that in other than real estate there is no preemption: partnership in animals, furniture, contracts, and all movables — there is no preemption in them when one of them sells his share. As for real estates: when they are divided, boundaries fixed, roads diverted, and each of the two partners chooses his share, there is no preemption in them, as is the text of the hadith, for he then becomes a neighbour, and a neighbour has no preemption over his neighbour. But when boundaries have not been fixed nor roads diverted, and one of them sells his share, the remaining partner or partners have preemption, taking it for the price upon which the contract was concluded, each according to his ownership. The apparent meaning of the hadith: there is no difference between real estate that can be divided. This is the correct view, for the wisdom in preemption — removing harm from the partner — exists in both kinds, and the hadith is general. What was adduced to distinguish between the two kinds is weak. The scholars differed concerning the neighbour's preemption over his neighbour when between them there is a shared right of the two properties, like a shared road, well, or the like. Some obligated preemption in this kind, saying: this partnership in this right is like partnership in the whole property, and the harm here is like the harm there — and this is what the proofs indicate. Some did not affirm preemption in it, as is the well-known view of the school of Imam Ahmad. Some affirmed preemption for the neighbour absolutely, and this case with them is with greater reason, as is the school of Imam Abu Hanifa. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmed preemption for the partner: if he wishes he takes, and if he wishes he does not take. It is of the rights that fall only by their being waived explicitly or by what indicates waiver. As for stipulating immediate hastening to take it, without his having an opportunity in this agreed right — this is a view with no proof. What they adduced of the two hadiths they cited: ""Preemption is like untying the hobble,"" ""Preemption belongs to whoever jumps to it,"" seeking it when he knows of the sale without delay in seeking it — nothing of them is authentically established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The correct view: this right, like other rights of the option of stipulation, defect, or the like, is established unless its owner waives it by word or deed. And Allah knows best."
"43","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah the Exalted says: I am the third of the two partners, so long as one of them does not betray his companion; if he betrays him, I leave from between them."" Reported by Abu Dawud.
This hadith indicates with its generality the permissibility of all kinds of partnerships: inan partnership, abdan, wujuh, mudaraba, mufawada, and other kinds of partnerships the two partners agree upon. Whoever forbids anything of them owes the proof indicating prohibition; otherwise the basis is permissibility, by this hadith and its comprehensiveness, and because the basis is permissibility in all transactions. The hadith indicates the virtue of partnerships and their blessing when built upon truthfulness and trustworthiness: whoever has Allah with him, He blesses his provision, makes easy for him the means by which he attains provision, provides for him whence he does not expect, and aids and guides him. That is because in partnerships there is cooperation between the partners in their judgment and their works. There may be works no one of them alone can do, and by combining works and wealth they can be achieved. Partnerships can also be branched and expanded in place, works, and otherwise. Also, in most cases they bring a rest not attained by a man working alone. One of them may run and manage the work while the other rests, or attends to some of his concerns, or during his illness. All this is with truthfulness and trustworthiness; but when betrayal enters them, and one of them or both intends betraying the other and hiding what he can, Allah leaves from between them, blessing departs, and means are not made easy. Experience and observation testify to this hadith. And Allah knows best."
"44","[AI-translation] From Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When the servant dies, his deed is cut off except from three: ongoing charity, knowledge benefited from, or a righteous child who supplicates for him."" Reported by Muslim.
Allah made the abode of this world an abode of deed, in which servants take provision of good or evil for the other abode, the abode of recompense. The negligent will regret when they move from this abode without having taken provision for their Hereafter to make them happy, when no remedy is possible. The servant cannot add to his good deeds an atom's weight, nor efface any of his evil deeds. The servant's deed is cut off from him except these three deeds, which are of the traces of his deed. The first: ongoing charity, i.e.: whose benefit continues. That is like endowments of real estates benefited from through their yield, vessels benefited from through use, animals benefited from through riding and their uses, books and copies of the Qur'an benefited from through use and benefit, or mosques, schools, houses, and the like benefited from. All of them: their reward flows for the servant so long as something of them is benefited from. This is among the greatest virtues of endowment, especially endowments aiding religious matters, like knowledge, jihad, devoting oneself to worship, and the like. For this reason the scholars stipulated for an endowment that its disbursement be toward a righteous, devoted purpose. The second: knowledge benefited from after him, like knowledge he taught to students ready for knowledge, knowledge he spread among people, and books he authored in kinds of beneficial sciences. Likewise everything whose benefit continues through his teaching, directly or in writing, its reward flows for him. How many guiding scholars died hundreds of years ago, while their books are in use and their students' good continues in succession — and that is Allah's bounty. The third: the righteous child — child of his loins, or child of his son, or daughter, male or female — whose father benefits by his righteousness and supplication. At all times he supplicates for his parents for forgiveness, mercy, raising of degrees, and attainment of rewards. These mentioned in this hadith are the content of: ""Indeed, We give life to the dead, and We write what they sent ahead and their traces"" (Qur'an Ya-Sin 36:12). What they sent ahead is what they directly engaged in of good or evil deeds. Their traces are what resulted from their deeds, of what others did or others benefited from. All that reaches the servant of the traces of his deed is three: first, matters others acted upon because of him, his call, and his guidance. Second, matters others benefited from, whatever benefit, according to that benefit, by following his example in good. Third, matters others did and gifted to him, or charity given on his behalf, or supplication for him, whether from his physical children or his spiritual children who graduated through his teaching, guidance, and direction, or from his relatives and loving companions, or from Muslims in general, according to his stations in religion, according to what he conveyed to servants of good or caused, and according to what Allah placed for him in servants' hearts of affection, whose many traces must follow, among them their supplication and seeking forgiveness for him. All fall under this noble hadith. Several benefits may combine for the servant in one thing, like the righteous, learned child whose father strove in his teaching, and like books one endows or grants to those who benefit from them. From this hadith is derived encouragement to marry, whose fruits include obtaining righteous children, and other interests, like the righteousness of the wife and teaching her what she benefits from and benefits others with. And Allah knows best."
"45","[AI-translation] From Asmar ibn Mudarris: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Whoever gets first to something to which no Muslim has got before him, it belongs to him.” Reported by Abu Dawud.
This hadith covers hastening first to all permissible things that are owned by no one and reserved for no one. Included in it is hastening first to revive dead land. Whoever gets to it first by extracting water, or conducting water to it, or by building, owns it. He does not own it without reviving it. But if the imam or his deputy grants it to him, or he marks out dead land without reviving it, he has more right to it but does not own it. If someone eager to revive it is found, he is told: either cultivate it or withdraw your hand from it. Included in that is hastening first to game of the land and the sea, and to minerals that are neither apparent nor flowing. And hastening first to take firewood or grass, or something discarded out of disinterest in it. And hastening first to sit in mosques, schools, markets and lodges, if that does not depend on a supervisor entrusted with ordering and assigning, in which case recourse is had to the stipulation of those who endowed and bequeathed. Whoever gets first to anything among the permissible things that have no owner has most right to it. Ownership in them is limited to the amount taken. Likewise, whoever is first to the tasks in reward contracts in which the offerer says: whoever does this task for me shall have such-and-such, is the one entitled to precedence and to the reward. Likewise, whoever is first to pick up a lost object or a foundling, and the like, all of it is included in this hadith. And Allah knows best."
"46","[AI-translation] From Ibn ‘Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Give the prescribed shares to those entitled to them, and whatever remains belongs to the nearest male relative.” Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim)."
"47","[AI-translation] From Abu Umamah al-Bahili (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: “Allah has given every right-holder his right, so there is no bequest for an heir.” Reported by Abu Dawud, al-Tirmidhi and Ibn Majah.
These two hadiths encompass most of the rulings on inheritance and the rulings on bequests. For Allah the Exalted has set out the rulings on inheritance in complete and clear detail, and has given every right-holder his right, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that the prescribed shares be given to those entitled to them, so they are given precedence over the residuaries. Whatever remains belongs to the nearest male relative. They are the residuaries among the male descendants, the male ascendants, the male descendants of the ascendants, and wala’. When two or more residuaries concur, precedence among these classes is given to the nearest class. If they are in the same class, the nearest in degree is given precedence. So the son is given precedence over the son’s son, and the paternal uncle, for example, over the son of the paternal uncle. If they are in the same degree and one of them is distinguished by strength of kinship — and that is conceivable only among the descendants of the ascendants, such as brothers and paternal uncles in general and their sons — the stronger, namely the full sibling, is given precedence over the one related through the father. This is what is meant by his saying (may Allah be pleased with him): “to the nearest male relative”, that is, the nearest of them in class, or degree, or strength, according to this order. It is understood from this that the holder of a prescribed share is given precedence over the residuary at the outset, and that if the prescribed shares exhaust the estate the residuary drops in all cases of inheritance, even in al-Himariyyah, which is when a woman leaves behind a husband, a mother, maternal siblings and full siblings: the husband receives a half, the mother a sixth, and the maternal siblings a third. These are holders of prescribed shares to whom we have assigned their shares, while the full siblings drop because they are residuaries. This is the correct view for many proofs, of which this is the clearest. From his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “Give the prescribed shares to those entitled to them”, it is inferred that when the prescribed shares become numerous and crowd together and none of them excludes another, ‘awl is applied for them and their shares are reduced in proportion to the excess, like debts when they lay claim to the assets of a debtor which do not suffice for their debts, for they are given in proportion to their debts. This is part of justice. All joint claimants to something that cannot be completed for each of them, where none of them has a ground for precedence, are reduced in proportion to their entitlement. That applies to gifts, bequests, endowments and the like, just as increase is theirs in proportion to their ownership and entitlement. The hadith indicates that if there is no holder of a prescribed share, all the property belongs to the residuaries according to the preceding order. Likewise it indicates that if there are only holders of prescribed shares and there is no residuary, the remainder is returned to them in proportion to their shares, just as deficiency was spread over them, because part of the wisdom of prescribing and fixing the shares is that the remainder stays for the residuary. If there is none, it is returned to those entitled for lack of a competitor. The hadith indicates the validity of a bequest to a non-heir. But there is a detail in that: if the testator is rich and leaves his heirs rich, it is recommended. If he is poor and his heirs need all of his inheritance, due to their poverty or their number, it is better for him not to bequeath, but to leave his property to his heirs. As for a bequest to an heir, the hadith indicates its prohibition. He explained that by his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “Allah has given every right-holder his right, so there is no bequest for an heir.” Whoever bequeaths to an heir has transgressed the bounds of Allah and favoured some heirs over others. It is the same whether that occurs in the form of a bequest or a gift to the heir, as is the agreement of the scholars, or in the form of an endowment of his third to some of his heirs. Some have diverged on this question and permitted it, which contradicts the wording of the hadith and its meaning. As for a bequest to a stranger, or to religious causes, it is permitted up to a third or less. Whatever exceeds a third depends on the approval of the heirs."
"48","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “There are three whom Allah has made it incumbent upon Himself to help: the mukatab who seeks to pay, the one who marries seeking chastity, and the one who strives in the path of Allah.” Reported by the authors of the Sunan except al-Nasa’i.
That is because Allah the Exalted has promised those who spend recompense in this world, and He mentioned spending in general. It applies to the kinds of spending that Allah loves, because His promise of recompense belongs to the realm of reward, which occurs only for what Allah loves. As for spending on matters Allah does not love, whether on acts of disobedience or on extravagance in permissible matters, Allah has not guaranteed recompense for their people; rather it is only a loss. These three mentioned in this hadith are among the best matters Allah loves. Striving in the path of Allah is the hump of the religion, its peak and its highest part. Whether it is striving with weapons or striving with knowledge and proof, spending in this cause is replaced, and the one who travels this path is helped by Allah and has his affair made easy for him. As for the mukatab, Allah has commanded contracting emancipation in His saying: “Make contracts with them if you know any good in them” (Qur’an An-Nur 24:33), that is, soundness in setting right their religion and their worldly life. The master is commanded to do that. The mukatab slave who seeks to pay and hastens to freedom and to free himself for his religion and his worldly life is helped by Allah, Who makes his affairs easy for him and provides for him from whence he does not expect. It is incumbent on the master to be gentle with his mukatab in fixing the times at which the instalments of the contract fall due, and to give him, from the contract sum when he pays it, a fourth of it. In His saying concerning mukatabs: “And give them from the wealth of Allah which He has given you” (Qur’an An-Nur 24:33) there is a command to the master and to other Muslims. For that reason Allah has made for him a share of zakat in His saying: “and in [freeing] necks” (Qur’an At-Tawbah 9:60), and this is part of His help. There is authentically established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is more general than this, for he said: “Whoever takes people’s wealth intending to repay it, Allah will repay on his behalf, and whoever takes it intending to destroy it, Allah will destroy him.” Reported by al-Bukhari. As for marriage, Allah and His Messenger have commanded it. He has attached to it many benefits: the help of Allah, compliance with the command of Allah and His Messenger, and that it is among the ways of the messengers. In it are guarding the private parts, lowering the gaze, obtaining offspring, and spending on wife and children, for when a servant spends on his family a spending by which he seeks reward, it becomes for him a wage and good deeds with Allah, whether it is food or drink or clothing or what is used for all needs. All of it is good for the servant, and ongoing good deeds. It is better than supererogatory acts of worship whose benefit is confined. In it are remembrance of Allah’s blessings upon the servant, freeing oneself for His worship, and the cooperation of the spouses in the interests of their religion and their worldly life. Allah has said: “Marry such women as please you” (Qur’an An-Nisa 4:3), and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “A woman is married for four reasons: her wealth, her beauty, her lineage and her religion, so win the one with religion, may your hand be dusty”, because of what lies in that in the soundness of circumstances, home and children, and the tranquillity of the husband’s heart and his peace of mind. If something besides religion is attained along with religion, so be it; otherwise religion is the greatest of the qualities sought. Allah has said: “Righteous women are devout and guard in absence what Allah has guarded” (Qur’an An-Nisa 4:34). It is incumbent on the wife to fulfil the right of Allah and the right of her husband, and to give the husband’s right precedence over the rights of all creatures. It is incumbent on the husband to strive to set his wife right and to do all the means by which harmony between them is completed, for harmony is the greatest aim. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) encouraged looking at the woman one wishes to propose to, so that he may act with insight in his affair. And Allah knows best."
"49","[AI-translation] From ‘A’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden through birth.” Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
That is because those forbidden through lineage, by the text of the Qur’an and consensus, are: mothers, however high, from every side; daughters, however low, from every side; sisters without restriction; brothers’ daughters and sisters’ daughters, however low; paternal aunts and maternal aunts. All [such] relatives are forbidden, except the daughters of paternal uncles, the daughters of paternal aunts, the daughters of maternal uncles and the daughters of maternal aunts. These seven are forbidden through breastfeeding on the side of the wet-nurse and the owner of the milk, when the breastfeeding is five feedings or more within the first two years. As for the side of the relatives of the nursing child, prohibition is restricted to the offspring of the nursing child. His father by lineage and his mother and their ascendants and descendants have no connection to the prohibition. Likewise forbidden is combining two sisters, or a woman with her paternal aunt or her maternal aunt, in lineage. The same applies in breastfeeding. Likewise forbidden are the mothers of one’s wife, however high, and her daughters, however low, if he has consummated marriage with his wife, and the wives of fathers, however high, and the wives of sons, however low, from every side, and the same applies in breastfeeding. There is disagreement over questions of forbidden combination and marriage ties through breastfeeding. But the view of the majority of scholars and the four imams is the prohibition of that, on account of the general texts."
"50","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “A believing man should not hate a believing woman: if he dislikes one trait in her, he will be pleased with another.” Reported by Muslim.
This guidance from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the husband in living with his wife is among the greatest causes and motives for good companionship in kindness. He forbade the believer to treat his wife badly in companionship. Forbidding a thing is a command of its opposite. He commanded him to notice what is in her of beautiful traits and matters that suit him, and to set them against what he dislikes of her traits. For when the husband reflects on what is in his wife of beautiful traits and good qualities that he loves, and looks at the cause that moved him to vexation with her and bad companionship, he sees it as one thing or two, for example, while what he loves in her is more. If he is fair, he overlooks her faults in view of their insignificance amid her good qualities. By this, companionship lasts and obligatory and recommended rights are fulfilled. Perhaps what he dislikes in her she strives to rectify or replace. As for one who overlooks good qualities and notices faults even if they are few, this is lack of fairness. He will hardly be happy with his wife. People in this are of three kinds. The highest are those who notice beautiful traits and good qualities and overlook faults entirely and forget them. The least guided, in faith and beautiful character, are those who reverse the matter: they disregard good qualities however great, and make faults the object of their eyes. Perhaps they extend them and expand them and interpret them with conjectures and interpretations that make little into much, as is the reality. The third kind are those who notice both matters and weigh between them, and treat the wife according to what each requires. This one is fair, but he has been deprived of perfection. This etiquette to which he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided should be followed and applied with all companions and those one deals with, for its religious and worldly benefit is great, and its practitioner has striven for the comfort of his heart and for the means by which he attains fulfilment of obligatory and recommended rights, because perfection in people is unattainable. It suffices the virtuous that his faults be counted. Acclimatising oneself to what comes from companions that contradicts one’s desire makes good character easy for one, and doing good and kindness to people. And Allah is the one who grants success."
"51","[AI-translation] From ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: “O ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, do not ask for leadership, for if you are given it upon asking you will be left to it, and if you are given it without asking you will be helped in it. And when you swear an oath and see something else better than it, do what is better and expiate for your oath.” Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains two momentous sentences. The first: leadership and other positions of authority over people should not be asked for by the servant, nor should he expose himself to them. Rather he should ask Allah for well-being and safety, for he does not know whether authority will be good for him or evil, nor whether he can discharge it or not. If he asks for it and is keen on it, he is left to himself. Whenever the servant is left to himself, he is not guided, nor set right in his affairs, nor helped in them, because asking for it betokens two evils. The first: keenness for worldly life and leadership, and keenness leads to suspicion of plunging into Allah’s wealth and exalting oneself over Allah’s servants. The second: in it is a kind of reliance on oneself and severance from seeking help from Allah. For this reason he said: “you will be left to it.” As for one who is not keen on it and does not aspire to it, but it comes to him without asking and he sees in himself lack of ability for it, Allah helps him in it and does not leave him to himself, because he did not expose himself to trial. Whoever is visited by trial without his choice is borne through it and guided to discharge his duty. In this situation his reliance on Allah the Exalted grows strong, and whenever the servant undertakes the means while relying on Allah, he succeeds. In his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “you will be helped in it” is proof that leadership and other worldly positions of authority combine two matters: religion and worldly life, for the aim of all positions of authority is setting right people’s religion and worldly life. For this reason they involve commanding and forbidding, compelling to obligations, deterring from prohibitions, and compelling to render rights. Likewise matters of governance and striving, so for one who is sincere in them to Allah and discharges the duty they are among the best acts of worship, and for one who is not so they are among the greatest dangers. For this reason they are among the communal obligations, since many obligations depend on them. If it is said: how did Yusuf seek authority over the financial treasuries in his saying: “Appoint me over the treasuries of the land” (Qur’an Yusuf 12:55), it is answered by His saying: “Indeed I am a knowing guardian” (Qur’an Yusuf 12:55), for he sought it only for this interest which none but he could discharge: complete guarding, and knowledge of all aspects connected with these treasuries, of good extraction, good dispensing and establishing complete justice. When he saw that the king had chosen him for himself and placed him foremost with him in high rank, sincere counsel to the king and the subjects also became incumbent on him. It is tied to his authority. For this reason, when he took charge of the treasuries of the land he strove to strengthen agriculture greatly. No place in the Egyptian lands from end to end fit for cultivation was left uncultivated during seven years. Then he fortified it and guarded it with that wondrous guarding. Then when the years of drought came and people were compelled to seek provisions, he strove to measure out to people in justice, and prevented merchants from buying food for fear of hardship to the needy, and there resulted from that benefits and advantages beyond count, as is known. The second sentence: his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “And when you swear an oath and see something else better than it, do what is better and expiate for your oath”, includes one who swears to abandon an obligation or to abandon a recommended act, for he expiates for his oath and does that obligation or recommended act he swore to abandon. It includes one who swears to do something forbidden or to do something disliked, for he is commanded to abandon that forbidden or disliked thing and to expiate for his oath. The four categories are included in his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “do what is better”, because doing what is commanded without restriction and abandoning what is forbidden without restriction are part of what is better. This is the meaning of His saying: “Do not make Allah an obstacle, through your oaths, to doing good, being God-fearing and making peace among people” (Qur’an Al-Baqarah 2:224), that is, do not make the oath an excuse and obstacle preventing you from doing kindness, God-fearing and peace among people when you have sworn to abandon these matters; rather expiate your oaths and do kindness, God-fearing and peace among people. It is taken from this hadith that guarding the oath in other matters is preferable, but if the oath is to do what is commanded or to abandon what is forbidden, he may not break it. If it concerns what is permissible, he is given a choice between the two matters, and keeping it is preferable. Know that expiation is obligatory only for a binding oath concerning the future, when one swears and breaks it. It is a choice between freeing a slave, feeding ten poor persons, or clothing them. Whoever does not find the means fasts three days. As for an oath concerning past matters or idle oaths, such as a person’s saying: no by Allah, and yes by Allah in the course of his speech, there is no expiation in them. And Allah knows best."
"52","[AI-translation] From ‘A’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him, and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him.” Reported by al-Bukhari.
A vow is the servant binding himself to an act of obedience to Allah, whether without a cause, such as his saying: I owe to Allah, or I have vowed, the freeing of a slave, or fasting such-and-such, or giving such-and-such in charity, or due to a cause, such as suspending that on the arrival of an absent one, or the recovery of a sick one, or the attainment of something loved, or the removal of something hated. Whenever his request is fulfilled, fulfilment becomes obligatory on him. This hadith covers all acts of obedience. Whoever vows an obligatory or recommended act of obedience must fulfil the vow. There is no expiation for him; rather fulfilment is required, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded in this hadith. As Allah has praised those who fulfil their vows in His saying: “They fulfil vows” (Qur’an Al-Insan 76:7), although contracting a vow is disliked, as he forbade vowing. He said: “It brings no good; it is only extracted from the miser.” As for a vow of disobedience, the servant must abandon disobedience to Allah even if he vowed it. The remaining categories of vows, like a vow of disobedience, a permissible vow, and a vow of obstinacy and anger, have the ruling of an oath in breaching it: in them is expiation for an oath, due to their sharing the meaning of an oath. And Allah knows best."
"53","[AI-translation] From ‘Ali (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “The Muslims, their blood is equal, the least of them may grant protection on their behalf, the farthest of them returns [the spoil] upon them, and they are a hand against others. No Muslim shall be killed for a disbeliever, nor one under a covenant during his covenant.” Reported by Abu Dawud and al-Nasa’i.
And Ibn Majah reported it from Ibn ‘Abbas. This hadith is like a detailing of His saying: “The believers are but brothers” (Qur’an Al-Hujurat 49:10) and his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “And be, O servants of Allah, brothers.” The believers must be loving one another, pure-hearted to one another, neither hating nor hostile to one another, all striving for their collective interests by which their religion and their worldly life stand, with no noble one exalting himself over a lowly one, and none of them despising anyone. Their blood is equal, for nothing is stipulated in retaliation except equivalence in religion, so a Muslim is not killed for a disbeliever, as in this hadith, and equivalence in freedom, so a free man is not killed for a slave. As for the remaining attributes, all Muslims are on the same level. Whoever kills or severs a limb deliberately and wrongfully, they may retaliate against him on condition of likeness in the limb, with no distinction between the small and the large and vice versa, the male and the female and vice versa, the learned and the ignorant, the noble and the lowly, and the perfect and the defective as the reverse in these matters. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “the least of them may grant protection on their behalf” means that the protection of the Muslims is one. Whenever a disbeliever seeks protection from one of the Muslims, the rest must grant him safety, as Allah has said: “If any of the polytheists seeks your protection, grant him protection until he hears the word of Allah, then convey him to his place of safety” (Qur’an At-Tawbah 9:6). There is no difference in this between the protection of a noble leader and that of ordinary people. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “the farthest of them returns upon them”, that is, in granting safety, and likewise the sharing of armies with their detachments that go out to raid or to guard: whenever the army gains spoils, or one of the detachments belonging to the army gains spoils, all share in the spoils. The one who directly took them is not singled out, because they are all cooperating in their task. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “and they are a hand against others”, that is, all Muslims in all parts of the earth must be a hand against their enemies among the disbelievers, in word and deed, and with assistance and cooperation in military matters, economic matters and repelling by every means. The Muslims must discharge these duties according to their ability, so that Allah may grant them victory and honour them, and repel by their fulfilment of the duties of faith the aggression of enemies. We ask Him to grant them success in that. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): “nor one under a covenant during his covenant”, that is, it is not lawful to kill a disbeliever who has a covenant through protection, safety or truce, for when he said: “No Muslim shall be killed for a disbeliever”, he guarded that statement against [being taken to permit] the prohibition of killing one under covenant, lest one who supposes think it lawful. And Allah knows best."
"54","[AI-translation] From ‘Amr ibn Shu‘ayb, from his father, from his grandfather: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Whoever practises medicine without being known for it is liable.” Reported by Abu Dawud and al-Nasa’i.
This hadith indicates by its wording and import that it is not lawful for anyone to undertake a craft among the crafts while not proficient in it, whether medicine or otherwise, and that whoever ventures on that is sinful. Whatever results from his work in destruction of a life or a limb or the like, he is liable for it. Whatever wealth he took in return for that craft in which he is not proficient is to be returned to the giver, because he gave it only due to his deception and his giving the impression that he is proficient, while he is not proficient, so it enters into fraud, and “whoever deceives us is not of us.” The like of this is the builder, the carpenter, the blacksmith, the cobbler, the weaver and the like among those who set themselves up for that, giving the impression that they are proficient in the craft, while they lie. The implied meaning of the hadith is that the skilful physician and the like, when he proceeds without his hand transgressing and destruction results from that, is not liable, because he is permitted to do so by the accountable person or his guardian. All that results from what is permitted is not guaranteed, while what results from what is not permitted is guaranteed. It is inferred from this that the craft of medicine is among the beneficial sciences sought by revelation and reason. And Allah knows best."
"55","[AI-translation] From ‘A’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: “Avert the prescribed punishments from the Muslims as far as you can. If he has a way out, leave his way free, for the imam erring in pardon is better than his erring in punishment.” Reported by al-Tirmidhi, raised to the Prophet and stopping at a Companion.
This hadith indicates that prescribed punishments are averted by doubts. When a person’s affair is ambiguous and his state is problematic to us, and possibilities arise — whether he did what entails the prescribed punishment or not, whether he is knowing or ignorant, whether he acts on an interpretation deeming it lawful or not, and whether he has an excuse of contract or belief — the punishment is averted from him, because we have not verified its ground with certainty. Even if the matter wavers between the two, error in averting punishment from the doer of its cause is lighter than error in inflicting punishment on one who did not do its cause, for Allah’s mercy precedes His wrath, and His law is built on ease and facility. The starting point concerning the blood, bodies and property of those whose inviolability is protected is prohibition, until we verify what permits us something of that. The scholars have mentioned, on this basis, many examples in the chapters on prescribed punishments, most of them in agreement with this hadith. Among them are examples open to consideration, for a possibility resembling fancy and imagination carries no weight. The measure is the wording of this hadith: if you find for him, or if he has a way out, leave his way free. In this hadith is proof of a principle, namely that when two harms conflict, in reality or in possibility, we observe the greater harm and repel it to lighten evil. And Allah knows best."
"56","[AI-translation] On the authority of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no obedience in disobedience; obedience is only in what is right."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith is a restriction upon everyone whose obedience is obligatory, such as rulers, parents, the husband, and others. For the Lawgiver commanded obedience to these, and the obedience due to each of them is in what suits his position, and all of it is in what is right. For the Lawgiver referred people back, in much of what He commanded them, to custom and usage, such as dutifulness and maintaining ties, justice and general beneficence. Likewise is the obedience of everyone whose obedience is obligatory. All of it is restricted by this restriction: whoever among them commands disobedience to Allah, whether by committing something forbidden or by omitting something obligatory, then there is no obedience to any creature in disobedience to Allah. So if one of them commands the killing of an inviolable person, or striking him, or taking his wealth, or omitting an obligatory Hajj, or an obligatory act of worship, or severing one with whom maintaining ties is obligatory, there is no obedience to them, and obedience to Allah is given precedence over obedience to creatures. It is understood from this hadith that if the obligatory obedience due to these persons conflicts with a supererogatory act, then obedience to them is given precedence, because omitting a supererogatory act is not disobedience. So if a husband forbids his wife from supererogatory fasting or supererogatory Hajj, or a ruler issues an administrative order that necessitates omitting something recommended, the obligatory duty must be given precedence. And his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ""Obedience is only in what is right,"" just as it covers what we have mentioned, also covers making that conditional upon ability and capacity, just as obligations are conditional at the root of the Law. And in a hadith: ""Hearing and obedience are upon you in what you are able."" And Allah knows best."
"57","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr and Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them both), they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When the judge judges, and strives and is correct, he has two rewards. And when he judges, and strives and errs, he has one reward."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
What is meant by the judge is one who possesses the knowledge that qualifies him to judge. The people of knowledge have mentioned the conditions of the judge. Some went to extremes in them, and some limited them to the knowledge that renders one fit to give fatwa, and this is preferable. In this hadith is that if an ignorant person judges and happens to hit the correct ruling, he is still unjust and sinful, because it is not lawful for him to undertake judging while ignorant. And it indicates that the judge must strive. This is of two types: striving to bring the case in which judgment is sought under the legal rulings, and striving to apply that right to the relative and the friend and to their opposites, so that people are all equal before him in this matter, not preferring one over another, and not swayed by desire. Whenever he is like that, he is rewarded in every case: if he is correct he has two rewards, and if he errs he has one reward, and his error is pardoned, because it is beyond his capacity. And justice, like other duties, is conditional upon capacity. The difference between the striving judge and the follower of desire is that the follower of the truth has done what he was commanded of good intention and striving, and he is commanded outwardly to believe what his evidence establishes before him, unlike the follower of desire, for he speaks without knowledge and without intending the truth. This was said by Shaykh al-Islam. And in this is the virtue of the judge with this description, and that he gains reward and recompense in every case he judges. For this reason judging is among the greatest of communal obligations, because all rights between people depend upon the judge in case of dispute or ambiguity. And he must struggle against himself to realize this striving by which his liability is cleared and by which he attains good and great reward. And Allah knows best."
"58","[AI-translation] On the authority of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""If people were given on the basis of their claims, men would claim the blood and property of others, but the oath is upon the defendant."" Reported by Muslim. In a variant narration with al-Bayhaqi: ""The proof is upon the claimant, and the oath is upon the one who denies.""
This hadith is of great importance. It is a major foundation among the foundations of cases and rulings, for judging between people only occurs at dispute: one claims a right against another and the other denies it, or one claims to be free of a right that was established against him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified a foundation that resolves their dispute and by which the truthful is distinguished from the false. Whoever claims a specific object, or a debt, or a right among rights and their accessories against another, and that other denies it, the presumption is with the denier. So this claimant, if he brings a proof establishing that right, it is established for him and judgment is given for him; and if he does not bring a proof, he has nothing against the other except the oath. Likewise, whoever claims to be free of a right due from him, and the owner of the right denies that and says that it remains in his liability, then if the claimant of payment and innocence does not bring a proof, judgment is given that the right remains in his liability, because that is the presumption. But the owner of the right must swear to its continuance. Likewise claims of defects, conditions, time limits, and documents are all of this chapter. Thus it is known that judges need this hadith in all matters of judging, because proof is the name for whatever clarifies the truth. It varies according to the variation of rights, and the people of knowledge have elaborated upon it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified in this hadith the ruling, and clarified the wisdom in this universal legislation, and that it is the very rectitude of servants in their religion and worldly life, and that if people were given on the basis of their claims, evil and corruption would multiply, and men would claim the blood and property of others. Thus it is known that the legislation of Islam brings the rectitude of mankind. If you wish to know that, compare each of its universal legislations with its opposite; you will find the great difference, and you will bear witness that the One who legislated it is Wise, Knowing, Merciful to His servants, for it comprises wisdom and justice, mercy, aid to the oppressed, and restraint of the oppressor. One of the verifiers said: the Law made the oath fall upon the stronger side of the two claimants. Whoever traces that will know it. And Allah knows best."
"59","[AI-translation] On the authority of A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) - raised to the Prophet - ""The testimony of a treacherous man or a treacherous woman is not permitted, nor of one flogged with a hadd punishment, nor of one bearing rancor against his brother, nor of one suspected regarding allegiance or kinship, nor of the dependent member of a household."" Reported by al-Tirmidhi.
This hadith comprises the matters that impair testimony. That is because Allah commanded seeking upright, acceptable witnesses. And the people of knowledge stipulated concerning the witness in rights between people that he be outwardly just, and they mentioned the qualities of probity. Some defined it with a definition taken from His saying: ""from those you approve as witnesses"" (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:282). He said: everyone approved among people, whose word and testimony they trust, is accepted. This is the best definition, and people cannot act upon other than it. The things that impair testimony go back to suspicion or to its presumption. Some people have their testimony rejected absolutely in all matters in which testimony is considered, such as the treacherous man and the treacherous woman, and one who incurred a hadd punishment - that is, a major sin from which he did not repent - for due to his treachery and sinfulness probity is lost, so his testimony is not accepted. Some people are described with probity, but have a quality feared to incline with it, so that he testifies contrary to the truth, such as ascendants and descendants, the freed slave and the dependent of a household; the testimony of such persons for those mentioned is not accepted, because it is a place of suspicion. But it is accepted against them. Likewise the two spouses, and the master with his contracted slave or his freed slave. Some people are the opposite of these, such as the enemy who has rancor in his heart - that is, hatred - against his brother. If he testifies in his favor, his testimony is accepted. But if he testifies against his enemy, it is not accepted, because enmity usually carries one to harming the enemy. And Allah knows best."
"60","[AI-translation] On the authority of Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""I said: O Messenger of Allah, we will meet the enemy tomorrow, and we have no knives with us. Shall we slaughter with reeds? He said: Whatever causes blood to flow and Allah's name has been mentioned over it, then eat, except the tooth and the nail. And I shall tell you about that: as for the tooth, it is bone; and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Abyssinians. And we seized plunder of camels and sheep, and a camel among them fled, and a man shot it with an arrow and stopped it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: 'These have wildness like the wildness of wild animals, so if any of them overcomes you, do with it thus.'"" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ""Whatever causes blood to flow,"" to the end, is comprehensive speech that includes everything that causes blood to flow - that is, sheds it - whether of iron, copper, brass, reed, wood, firewood, stones, sharpened or otherwise, and whatever penetrates like lead shot, for it causes flow by its penetration, not by its weight. Included in that is what is hunted with arrows, trained dogs, and birds, when Allah's name is mentioned over all of that. As for the place of slaughter, it is the throat and the esophagus; if he cuts them, that suffices. If along with them he cuts the two jugulars - the two veins surrounding the throat - that is better. As for game, wounding it in any part of its body suffices, due to the need for that. Likewise, if a camel, cow, or sheep strays and cannot be caught, it takes the status of game, as in the hadith. In whatever part of its body it is wounded, that suffices, just as game, if caught while alive, must be slaughtered. The ruling turns with its cause: what cannot be subdued takes the status of game, even from domestic animals; and what can be subdued must be slaughtered, even from wild animals. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excepted from that the tooth, explaining that it is bone. This indicates that all bones - even if they cause blood to flow - may not be used for slaughter. It was said that the cause is the combination of the two matters: its being a tooth and its being bone, so it is restricted to the tooth. But the correct view is the first. Likewise the nail may not be used for slaughter, neither for birds nor otherwise. The conclusion is that the conditions of slaughter are: shedding blood at the place of slaughter, the slaughterer being a Muslim or one of the People of the Book, and mentioning Allah's name over it. As for hunting, it is broader than slaughter, as already stated that it may be in any part of the game's body, and that hunting with birds of prey and dogs is permitted if they are trained, with Allah's name mentioned over them when they are sent after the game. And Allah knows best."
"61","[AI-translation] On the authority of Shaddad ibn Aws (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Indeed Allah has prescribed excellence upon everything. So when you kill, do the killing well, and when you slaughter, do the slaughtering well. Let each of you sharpen his blade, and let him give rest to his slaughtered animal."" Reported by Muslim.
Excellence is of two types: excellence in worshipping the Creator, by worshipping Allah as if you see Him, for if you do not see Him, Allah sees you. It is earnestness in fulfilling the rights of Allah with sincerity and completing them. And excellence in the rights of creatures. The foundation of obligatory excellence is to fulfill their obligatory rights, such as fulfilling dutifulness to parents, maintaining kinship ties, and fairness in all transactions, by giving all the rights due from you, just as you take what is yours in full. Allah the Exalted said: ""Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and show kindness to parents, relatives, orphans, the needy, the near neighbor, the distant neighbor, the companion at your side, the traveler, and those whom your right hands possess"" (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:36). So He commanded excellence to all of these. Included in that is excellence to all humankind, and excellence to animals, even in the state in which their lives are taken. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you kill, do the killing well."" Whoever deserves to be killed for a cause requiring killing has his neck struck with the sword, without torture or mutilation. And his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ""When you slaughter, do the slaughtering well,"" means the manner and quality of slaughter. For this reason he said: ""Let each of you sharpen his blade,"" that is, his knife, ""and let him give rest to his slaughtered animal."" If the servant is commanded to show excellence to one who deserves to be killed among humans, and to perfect the slaughter of what is to be slaughtered among animals, then what of other situations? And know that the commanded excellence is of two types. One is obligatory, which is fairness and fulfilling what is obligatory upon you toward creatures according to the rights directed upon you. The second is recommended excellence, which is beyond that, consisting of giving benefit whether bodily, monetary, intellectual, or guidance to religious good or worldly benefit. Every act of kindness is charity, and everything that brings joy to creatures is charity and excellence. And everything that removes from them what they dislike and repels from them what they disapprove, little or much, is charity and excellence. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the story of the prostitute who gave water to the severely thirsty dog with her shoe from the well, and that Allah thanked her and forgave her, they said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""Do we have a reward in animals?"" He said: ""In every moist liver there is a reward."" So excellence is giving all benefits of whatever kind to whatever creature, but it varies according to the variation of those to whom excellence is shown, their right and station, and according to the excellence itself, the greatness of its place and its great benefit, and according to the faith of the doer and his sincerity, and the motive prompting him to that. Among the noblest types of excellence is excellence to one who wronged you by word or deed. Allah the Exalted said: ""Repel evil with that which is better; then the one between you and whom there is enmity will become as if he were an intimate friend. And none is granted it except those who are patient, and none is granted it except one of great fortune"" (Qur'an Fussilat 41:34-35). Whoever's way is excellence, Allah will excellently reward him: ""Is the reward of excellence anything but excellence?"" (Qur'an Ar-Rahman 55:60), ""For those who do good is the best reward and more"" (Qur'an Yunus 10:26), ""For those who do good in this world is good"" (Qur'an Az-Zumar 39:10), ""Indeed, the mercy of Allah is near to the doers of good"" (Qur'an Al-A'raf 7:56), that is, those excellent in worshipping Allah and excellent to the servants of Allah. And Allah enjoins upon His servants justice as part of excellence, and encourages them to the increase of virtue in it, and said concerning dealings: ""And do not forget graciousness between you"" (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:237), that is, give graciousness and excellence a place in your dealings. Do not exact in full all rights, but make things easy and do not make them hard, and be tolerant in buying and selling, in paying and exacting. Whoever obliges himself to this kindness attains much good and great excellence. And Allah knows best."
"62","[AI-translation] On the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, domestic donkeys, the flesh of mules, every fanged beast of prey, and every taloned bird."" Reported by al-Tirmidhi.
The foundation in all foods is permissibility, for Allah permitted to His servants what the earth produces of varied grains, fruits, and plants, and permitted to them all sea animals, alive and dead. As for land animals, He permitted all wholesome ones among them, such as the eight cattle and others, and wild game of birds and others. He only forbade of this type the impure, and set for that a limit and criterion. Sometimes He specified some of the prohibited ones, as He specified in this hadith the domestic donkeys and mules and forbade them. And He said: ""It is impure."" As for wild donkeys, they are lawful. Likewise He forbade fanged beasts of prey, such as the wolf, lion, tiger, fox, dog, and the like, and every taloned bird that hunts with its talon, such as the falcon, sparrowhawk, and the like. Whatever it is forbidden to kill, such as the shrike, or commanded to kill, such as the crow and the like, is forbidden. Whatever is impure, such as snakes, scorpions, rats, and kinds of insects, and likewise whatever dies naturally among permissible animals, or is slaughtered with an un-Islamic slaughter, is forbidden. And Allah knows best."
"63","[AI-translation] On the authority of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed the effeminate among men who imitate women, and the masculine among women who imitate men."" Reported by al-Bukhari.
The foundation in all ordinary matters is permissibility, so nothing is forbidden of them except what Allah and His Messenger forbade, either for itself, such as usurped property and what is impure in earning, in the case of men and women, or because permissibility was restricted to one of the two kinds, as the Lawgiver permitted wearing gold, silver, and silk to women and forbade it to men. As for the Lawgiver's forbidding men imitating women and women imitating men, it is general in dress, speech, and all states. Matters are of three categories: a category shared between men and women among kinds of dress and other things, which is permissible for both kinds, because the foundation is permissibility and there is no imitation in it; a category specific to men, which is not lawful for women; and a category specific to women, which is not lawful for men. Among the wisdom in forbidding imitation is that Allah has given men a degree over women, and made them maintainers over women, and distinguished them by decreed matters and legal matters. So maintaining this distinction and affirming the virtue of men over women is intended by Law and reason. Thus men imitating women lowers them from this high degree, and women imitating men nullifies the distinction. Also, men imitating women in speech, dress, and the like is among the causes of effeminacy, collapse of morals, and the desire of the imitator of women to mix with them, from which a forbidden consequence is feared, and likewise the reverse. These legal meanings, and preserving the ranks of men and the ranks of women, and placing each in the station in which Allah placed him, are commendable by reason, just as they are commendable by Law. If you wish to know the harm of complete imitation and disregard for stations, look in this age at the degraded mixing with which religious jealousy, human manliness, and praiseworthy morals have gone, replaced by the opposite of that of every vile character. Resembling this - or more severe than it - is Muslims imitating disbelievers in matters specific to them, for he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever imitates a people is one of them."" For outward imitation calls to inward imitation, and the Lawgiver intended to block means and pretexts to evils from every angle."
"64","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah did not send down a disease except that He sent down for it a cure."" Reported by al-Bukhari.
Sending down here means decreeing. In this hadith is the affirmation of decree and predestination, and the affirmation of causes. It has already been stated that this great foundation is established by the Book and the Sunnah, and is supported by reason and natural disposition. Religious and worldly benefits and harms are all by Allah's decree and predestination: He has encompassed them in knowledge, His pen has run with them, His will has been executed through them, and He has facilitated the servants to perform the causes that lead them to benefits and harms. So each is facilitated to what he was created for, of religious and worldly interests and their harms. The fortunate is whom Allah facilitates to the easiest matters, nearest to Allah's pleasure and most wholesome for his religion and worldly life, and the wretched is one for whom the matter is reversed. The generality of this hadith requires that all inward and outward diseases have medicines that counter them, repelling what has not yet descended and removing what has descended entirely or lightening it. In this is encouragement to learn bodily medicine, just as one learns the medicine of hearts, and that it is among beneficial causes. All foundations of medicine and its details are an explanation of this hadith, because the Lawgiver informed us that all diseases have medicines. So we should strive to learn them, and thereafter to act upon them and apply them. Many people used to think that some diseases have no medicine, such as tuberculosis and the like, but when the science of medicine advanced and people reached what they have reached of its knowledge, people knew the truth of this hadith and that it is upon its generality. The foundations of medicine are: managing food, by not eating until the appetite is true and the previous food is fully digested, seeking the most beneficial foods according to the state of regions, persons, and circumstances, and not filling oneself with food to a fullness that harms one's activity and one's effort to digest it. Rather the measure is His saying: ""Eat and drink, but do not be excessive"" (Qur'an Al-A'raf 7:31). And one should observe precaution against all harmful things, in their amount, in themselves, or in their timing. Then, if evacuation is possible and the purpose is attained without resorting to medicines, that is preferable and more beneficial. If forced to medicine, one uses it in measure. Only a knowledgeable, skillful physician should undertake that. And know that wholesome air, cleanliness of body and clothes, and distance from foul smells are the best aid to health. Likewise moderate exercise, for it strengthens organs, nerves, and tendons, removes wastes, and digests heavy foods. The details of medicine are known to physicians, but these foundations we mentioned are needed by everyone. And it is authentically reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""Cure is in three: a cupper's incision, a drink of honey, or cauterization with fire,"" and ""In the black seed there is cure from every disease."" ""Indian oud contains seven cures; it is inhaled for throat disease and administered through the side of the mouth for pleurisy,"" ""Fever is from the heat of Hell, so cool it with water,"" ""He permitted incantation for the evil eye, venomous stings, and sores,"" ""When you are asked to wash for the evil eye, then wash,"" ""He forbade foul medicine,"" ""And he commanded dyeing the feet for their pain."""
"65","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Qatadah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The righteous dream is from Allah, and the bad dream is from Satan. So if one of you sees what he loves, let him not narrate it except to one he loves. And if he sees what he hates, let him seek refuge in Allah from its evil and from the evil of Satan, and let him spit three times, and not narrate it to anyone, for it will not harm him."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that the righteous dream is from Allah, that is, free from Satan's confusion and disturbance. That is because when man sleeps, his soul goes out and attains some detachment by which it becomes prepared for much knowledge and understanding. It is made gentle with what Allah inspires it and what the angel casts to it in its sleep. Then he awakens with matters clarified to him that were previously unknown, or he recalls matters he had forgotten, or he is alerted to conditions whose knowledge or acting upon benefits him, or he is warned of religious or worldly harms that were not on his mind, or he takes admonition and is moved to hope and fear concerning deeds he has engaged in or is about to, or he is alerted to some particular entities for inclusion in legal rulings. All these matters are a sign of the righteous dream, which is a part of forty-six parts of prophethood. And what is of prophethood does not lie. Consider the dream of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in His saying: ""[Remember] when Allah showed them to you in your dream as few; and if He had shown them to you as many, you would have lost courage and disputed in the matter, but Allah saved you. Indeed, He is Knowing of that within the breasts"" (Qur'an Al-Anfal 8:43), how many benefits resulted from it and how many harms were repelled. Likewise His saying: ""Allah has surely fulfilled His Messenger's vision in truth: you will surely enter al-Masjid al-Haram, if Allah wills, secure, with your heads shaved and hair shortened, not fearing. He knew what you did not know and granted besides that a near victory"" (Qur'an Al-Fath 48:27), how much increase of faith resulted from it, and perfect certainty was completed through it, and it was among Allah's great signs. And consider the dream of the king of Egypt and the interpretation of Yusuf, the truthful, and how, just as he undertook the interpretation, Allah entrusted him with the management it contained. Through that much good came about, abundant blessings, necessities and needs were repelled, and Allah raised Yusuf above the servants in degrees. And reflect upon the dream of Abdullah ibn Zayd and Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them both) concerning the adhan and iqamah, and how it became a cause for legislating this great rite, which is among the greatest religious rites. And the dreams of prophets, saints, and righteous people - indeed of believers in general and others - are well known and famous; what they comprise of important benefits and wholesome fruits cannot be counted. They are among Allah's favors upon His servants, glad tidings to believers, alerts to the heedless, reminders to the averse, and establishment of proof against the obstinate. As for the bad dream, which is confused dreams, it is only from Satan's confounding of man's soul, disturbing and frightening it, bringing matters that earn it anxiety and grief, or that cause it joy, exultation, and arrogance, or that agitate it to evil, corruption, and harmful greed. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded upon that the servant take the means that repel its evil: not to narrate it to anyone, for that is a cause for its nullification and dissipation; to spit on his left three times; to seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan, who is the cause and instigator of this dream; and to be confident at that that it will not harm him, believing the word of His Messenger and trusting the success of the repelling means. As for the righteous dream, one should praise Allah for it, ask Him to fulfill it, and narrate it to one he loves and knows affection from, so that he rejoices at his joy and supplicates for him concerning it. And he should not narrate it to one he does not love, lest he disturb him with an interpretation conforming to his desire, or strive - out of envy - to remove the blessing from him. For this reason, when Yusuf saw the sun, the moon, and the eleven stars prostrating to him and related it to his father, he said to him: ""O my son, do not relate your vision to your brothers lest they plot against you; indeed, Satan is to man a clear enemy"" (Qur'an Yusuf 12:5). For this reason concealing blessings from enemies - when possible - is preferable, unless there is a preponderant benefit in that. And know that the true dream is sometimes seen by the servant in its outward form, as in the dream of the adhan and others, and sometimes parables are coined for him therein of sensible things, that he may consider thereby intelligible matters, or sensible matters resembling them, like the dream of the king of Egypt and the like. It varies according to the dreamer, the time, custom, and the variety of conditions."
"66","[AI-translation] On the authority of Ali ibn al-Husayn - may Allah have mercy on him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""From the excellence of one's Islam is leaving what does not concern him."" Reported by Malik and Ahmad.
And it was reported by Ibn Majah on the authority of Abu Hurayrah, and al-Tirmidhi reported it on the authority of Ali ibn al-Husayn and on the authority of Abu Hurayrah. Islam - when used unrestrictedly - includes faith and excellence. It is the laws of the religion, outward and inward. Muslims are divided in Islam into two sections, as implied by this hadith. Among them are those excellent in their Islam, and among them are those deficient. Whoever establishes Islam outwardly and inwardly is excellent: ""And who is better in religion than one who submits himself to Allah while doing good and follows the religion of Abraham, inclining to truth, and Allah took Abraham as an intimate friend"" (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:125). So this excellent person occupies himself with what concerns him, among what he must leave of sins and evils, and what he should leave of disliked matters and excess permissible matters in which there is no benefit for him but which cause him to miss good. So his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), ""From the excellence of one's Islam is leaving what does not concern him,"" covers what we mentioned. The implied meaning of the hadith is that whoever does not leave what does not concern him is deficient in his Islam, and that includes sayings and deeds forbidden by prohibition or dislike. This hadith is counted among the general comprehensive words, because it made this exhaustive division, and clarified the causes by which excellence of Islam is completed, which is occupying oneself with what concerns and leaving what does not concern, of word and deed, and the causes by which the servant is deficient, which are the opposite of this state. And Allah knows best."
"67","[AI-translation] From Ayyub ibn Musa ibn Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-As, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «No father grants his child any gift better than good manners.» Reported by al-Tirmidhi.
The people most deserving of your kindness, and most entitled to your good treatment, are your children, for they are trusts which Allah has placed with you, and He has enjoined you to raise them with a righteous upbringing for their bodies and their hearts. Everything you do for them in these matters, small or great, is part of fulfilling the duty upon you, and among the best of what draws you near to Allah, so strive in that and seek its reward with Allah. For just as when you feed them and clothe them and undertake the nurturing of their bodies, you are upholding what is right and are rewarded, so too — and even greater than that — when you undertake the nurturing of their hearts and souls with beneficial knowledge, true understanding, guidance to praiseworthy morals, and warning against their opposites. And ""nihal"" are gifts and acts of kindness; good manners are better for children in the present and in the end than giving them gold and silver and kinds of worldly goods, for by good manners and beautiful character they rise, by them they attain happiness, by them they fulfil what is due upon them of the rights of Allah and the rights of His servants, by them they avoid kinds of harms, and by them their dutifulness to their parents is completed. As for neglecting children, its harm is great and its danger grave. Consider: if you had a garden and you nurtured it until its trees were complete, its fruits ripened, and its crops and flowers were adorned, then you neglected it and did not protect it, nor water it, nor cleanse it of pests, nor prepare it for growth at all times — is this not among the greatest ignorance and folly? Then how can you neglect your children, who are a part of your liver, the fruit of your heart, a copy of your spirit, and those who stand in your place in life and after death, by whose happiness your happiness is completed, and by whose success and prosperity you attain much good? «And none will remember except those of understanding» (Qur'an Aal Imran 3:7)."
"68","[AI-translation] From Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The example of the righteous companion and the evil companion is like the carrier of musk and the blower of the bellows. The carrier of musk will either give you some, or you will buy from him, or you will find from him a pleasant scent. And the blower of the bellows will either burn your clothes, or you will find from him a foul smell.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith includes an encouragement to choose righteous companions and a warning against their opposites. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave these two examples, explaining that with the righteous companion, in all your states with him you are in gain and good, like the carrier of musk from whose musk you benefit: either as a gift or in exchange for payment. And the least of that is that for the duration of your sitting with him, you are content in yourself at the scent of the musk. The good that the servant attains from his righteous companion is more far-reaching and better than the finest musk, for either he teaches you what benefits you in your religion and your worldly life, or he gives you sincere advice, or he warns you against persisting in what harms you. So he urges you to obey Allah, to be dutiful to parents, to maintain ties of kinship, he shows you the faults of your own self, and he calls you to noble morals and their beauties by his words, his deeds, and his state. For man is naturally disposed to imitate his companion and intimate, and natures and souls are troops assembled together, leading one another to good or to its opposite. And the least you gain from the righteous companion — and it is a benefit not to be belittled — is that you refrain because of him from misdeeds and acts of disobedience, out of regard for the companionship, competing in good, rising above evil, and that he protects you in your presence and in your absence, and that his love and his supplication for you benefit you during your life and after your death, and that he defends you by virtue of his connection to you and his love for you. These are matters which you yourself do not directly undertake to defend, just as he may connect you with persons and deeds whose connection benefits you. The benefits of righteous companions cannot be counted or enumerated. It suffices a man to consider his associate, and that he is upon the religion of his close friend. As for keeping company with evil people, it is the opposite of all that we have mentioned, and they are a harm from every angle to whoever keeps their company, and an evil to whoever mixes with them. How many peoples have perished because of them, and how often have they led their companions to ruin, whether knowingly or unknowingly. For this reason, among the greatest of Allah's blessings upon the believing servant is that He grants him success in keeping the company of the good. And among His punishment of His servant is that He afflicts him with the company of evil people. The company of the good brings the servant to the highest of the high, and the company of evil people brings him to the lowest of the low. The company of the good necessitates for him beneficial knowledge, excellent morals, and righteous deeds, while the company of evil people deprives him of all of that: «And the Day the wrongdoer will bite on his hands, saying: Would that I had taken with the Messenger a way. Oh, woe to me! Would that I had not taken so-and-so as a friend. He led me away from the remembrance after it had come to me. And Satan is ever to man a deserter» (Qur'an Al-Furqan 25:27-29)."
"69","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The believer is not stung from one hole twice.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This is an example given by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to explain the perfection of the believer's caution and vigilance, and that his faith prevents him from committing the misdeeds whose commission harms him, and that whenever he falls into something of them, he immediately hastens to regret, repentance, and turning back. And among the perfection of his repentance is that he guards himself with the utmost caution against the cause that made him fall into the sin, like the state of one who put his hand into a hole and a snake stung him, for after that he will hardly put his hand into that hole again, because of what first afflicted him there. And just as faith carries its bearer to perform acts of obedience, inclines him to them, and grieves him at missing them, so too it restrains him from committing misdeeds, and if they occur he hastens to withdraw from them and does not return to the like of what he fell into. In this hadith there is an encouragement to firmness and sagacity in all matters. Among the requirements of that is knowing the beneficial causes so as to uphold them, and the harmful causes so as to avoid them. It indicates an encouragement to avoid the causes of suspicion whose approach is feared to lead to falling into evil. And that pretexts are taken into account. Allah has warned the believers against returning to what Satan has adorned of falling into acts of disobedience, saying: «Allah warns you never to return to the like of this, if you are believers» (Qur'an An-Nur 24:17). For this reason, one who has tasted evil among the repentant has a greater hatred for it, and his warning and caution against it are more intense, because he has known by experience its ugly effects. And in the hadith: «Deliberation is from Allah, haste is from Satan; there is no forbearing one except one who has stumbled, and no wise one except one with experience.» And Allah knows best."
"70","[AI-translation] From Abu Dharr al-Ghifari (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «O Abu Dharr, there is no intellect like planning, no God-conscious restraint like refraining, and no lineage like good character.» Reported by al-Bayhaqi in ""Shu'ab al-Iman"".
This hadith comprises three statements, under each of which is a vast field of knowledge. As for the first statement, it explains intellect, its effects, and its signs, and that the praiseworthy intellect in the Book and the Sunnah is a strength and blessing with which Allah has blessed the servant, by which he grasps beneficial things, sciences, and branches of knowledge, restrains himself by it, and holds back from harmful and ugly matters. It is indispensable for man; he cannot do without it in any of his religious or worldly states, for by it he knows what is beneficial and the way to it, and he knows what is harmful and how to be safe from it. Intellect is known by its effects. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained in this hadith its good effects, saying: «There is no intellect like planning», that is, the servant's planning of the affairs of his religion and the affairs of his worldly life. His planning of the affairs of his religion is that he strives to know the straight path and what the noble Prophet was upon of morals, guidance, and conduct, then strives to follow it in an orderly state. As the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Seek help in the early morning, the late afternoon, and something of the night journey; aim for moderation, aim for moderation, and you will reach.» The explanation of this hadith has already been given, and the explanation of the path to which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) guided — that it is an easy path leading to Allah and to the abode of His honour with ease and comfort, that it does not deprive the servant of any of his rest or his worldly affairs, but rather the servant is able along with it to attain both benefits and win both happinesses and the good life. Whenever he plans his religious affairs by this lawful measure, his religion and his intellect are complete, for what is required of the intellect is to bring its bearer to praiseworthy outcomes by the nearest and easiest way. As for planning one's livelihood, the intelligent person strives to seek provision by what is clear to him to be most beneficial and most effective in attaining his aim. He does not flounder among the means blindly, with no settled course; rather, when he sees a means by which a door of provision has opened for him, let him keep to it, persevere in it, and seek gracefully, for in this there is a proven blessing. Then he plans a further planning, in spending and disposal: he does not spend in unlawful ways, or in ways that are not beneficial, nor is he extravagant in lawful spending nor stingy. The measure of that is His saying, exalted be He, in praise of the good: «And those who, when they spend, are neither extravagant nor stingy, but hold a just balance between that» (Qur'an Al-Furqan 25:67). So good planning in earning provisions, and good planning in spending, disposal, safeguarding, and their adjuncts, is proof of the perfection of a man's intellect, his gravity, and his right-mindedness. Its opposite is proof of the deficiency of his intellect and the corruption of his mind. The second statement is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «No God-conscious restraint is like refraining.» This is a comprehensive definition of restraint, by which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained that true restraint is that one holds back oneself, one's heart, one's tongue, and all one's limbs from unlawful harmful matters. Everything the people of knowledge have said in explaining restraint returns to this clear, comprehensive explanation. Whoever guards his heart against doubts and uncertainties, against unlawful desires, rancour, and envy, and against all other evils of character, and guards his tongue against backbiting, tale-bearing, lying, and insult, and against every sin, harm, and unlawful speech, and guards his private parts and his sight against the unlawful, and guards his stomach against eating the unlawful, and his limbs against earning sins — this is true restraint. Whoever neglects anything of that, his restraint is diminished to that extent. For this reason the Shaykh al-Islam said: ""Restraint is leaving what one fears will harm in the Hereafter."" The third statement is his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «And no lineage like good character.» That is because lineage is a high rank among people, and the person of lineage has standing and honour according to that. It is of two kinds. The first kind is lineage connected to a man's ancestry and the nobility of his house. This kind is only praise because it suggests that its bearer acts in accordance with his lineage, rising above base things and adorning himself with noble traits. So it is intended for the sake of something else. The second kind is the true lineage which is a description of the servant, a beauty for him and an adornment, and a good in this world and in religion, namely good character, comprising broad forbearance, patience, pardon, bestowing kindness and beneficence, bearing mistreatment and harm, and treating the classes of people with good character. Or if you wish, say: good character is of two kinds. The first is good character with Allah, which is to receive His legal and decreed rulings with contentment and submission to His decree, and compliance with His law, with tranquillity and contentment, and gratitude to Allah for what He has bestowed of command and guidance, and patience over His painful decrees and contentment with them. The second is good character with people, which is bestowing generosity, bearing harm, and refraining from harm, as He, exalted be He, said: «Hold to forgiveness, command what is right, and turn away from the ignorant» (Qur'an Al-A'raf 7:199), and «And not equal are the good deed and the bad deed. Repel by that which is better; and thereupon the one whom between you and him is enmity will become as though he was a devoted friend. But none is granted it except those who are patient, and none is granted it except one having a great portion» (Qur'an Fussilat 41:34-35). Whoever upholds good character with Allah and with people has attained good and success. And Allah knows best."
"71","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: «A man came and said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He said: Do not get angry. Then he repeated it several times, and he said: Do not get angry.» Reported by al-Bukhari.
This man thought it was advice about a minor matter, while he wanted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to advise him with comprehensive words, and for this reason he repeated it. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repeated it to him, he knew that this is comprehensive speech, and so it is; for his saying «Do not get angry» includes two great matters. The first is the command to practise the causes and to train oneself in good character, forbearance, and patience, and to accustom the soul to what befalls a man from people of verbal and physical harm. If the servant is granted success in that, and a surge of anger comes upon him, he bears it with his good character, receives it with his forbearance and patience, and his knowledge of its good outcomes, for the command to do a thing is a command to do it and to do what it cannot be completed without, and the prohibition of a thing is a command to do its opposite, and a command to practise the causes that help the servant avoid the prohibited matter, and this is of that. The second is the command — after anger — not to carry out one's anger, for anger in most cases man cannot repel and ward off, but he can refrain from carrying it out. So when he is angry, he must restrain himself from the unlawful words and deeds which anger entails. Whenever he restrains himself from acting upon the harmful effects of anger, it is as though in reality he did not get angry. By this the servant is of complete intellectual strength and strength of heart, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «The strong is not the wrestler; the strong is the one who controls himself when angry.» The perfection of the servant's strength is that he refrains from letting the force of desire and the force of anger produce in him evil effects; rather he directs these two forces to attaining what benefits in religion and worldly life, and to repelling what harms in them. The best of people are those whose desire and inclination follow what the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought, and whose anger and resistance are in support of truth against falsehood. And the worst of people are those felled by their desire and anger. There is no power nor strength except through Allah."
"72","[AI-translation] From Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «None shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a speck of arrogance.» A man said: A man loves that his garment be fine and his sandals be fine? He said: «Allah is Beautiful and loves beauty. Arrogance is rejecting the truth and despising people.» Reported by Muslim.
Allah, exalted be He, has declared that Hell is the abode of the arrogant, and in this hadith: «None shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a speck of arrogance», which shows that arrogance necessitates entering Hell and prevents entering Paradise. With this comprehensive explanation which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave, this meaning becomes clear with the utmost clarity, for he made arrogance of two kinds. Arrogance of the first kind is towards the truth, which is rejecting it and not accepting it; everyone who rejects the truth is arrogant towards it to the extent of what he rejected of the truth. That is because it is obligatory upon servants to submit to the truth with which Allah sent His messengers and sent down His books. Those who are arrogant in refusing the messengers altogether are disbelievers abiding eternally in Hell, for the truth came to them at the hands of the messengers supported by signs and proofs, but arrogance stood in their hearts as a barrier, and they rejected it. He, exalted be He, said: «Those who dispute concerning the signs of Allah without any authority having come to them — there is in their breasts nothing but pride, which they will never attain» (Qur'an Ghafir 40:56). As for those who are arrogant in refusing some of the truth which contradicts their opinion and desire — even if they are not disbelievers — with them is of what necessitates punishment according to what is with them of arrogance, and their being affected by refraining from accepting the truth which became clear to them after the Law came with it. For this reason the scholars are agreed that whoever has had the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made clear to him, it is not lawful for him to turn away from it for the saying of anyone, whoever he may be. The seeker of knowledge must resolve with firm resolve to give precedence to the saying of Allah and the saying of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over the saying of anyone, and that his origin to which he returns and his foundation upon which he builds be guidance by the guidance of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and striving to know his intent and to follow him in that, outwardly and inwardly. Whenever he is granted success in this momentous matter, he is granted success in good, and his error becomes pardoned, because his general aim is to follow the Law. Error is excused when he has done what he can of inference and striving to know the truth, and this is the one humble before the truth. As for arrogance towards people — the second kind — it is despising them and scorning them, and that arises from a man's self-admiration and his magnifying himself over them. Self-admiration carries one to arrogance over people, scorning them, mocking them, and belittling them by word and deed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Sufficient evil for a man to despise his Muslim brother.» When this man said: «A man loves that his garment be fine and his sandals be fine», fearing that this is of the arrogance concerning which the threat came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained to him that this is not of arrogance, when its bearer is submissive to the truth and humble towards people, and that it is of the beauty which Allah loves, for He, exalted be He, is Beautiful in His essence, His names, His attributes, and His acts, and He loves outward beauty and inward beauty. Outward beauty is like cleanliness in the body, clothing, dwelling, and their adjuncts. Inward beauty is adorning oneself with the meanings of morals and their beauties. For this reason, among the supplications of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: «O Allah, guide me to the best of deeds and morals; none guides to the best of them but You. And turn away from me the evil of deeds and morals; none turns away from me their evil but You.» And Allah knows best."
"73","[AI-translation] From Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «He has succeeded who becomes Muslim, is given sufficient provision, and Allah makes him content with what He has given him.» Reported by Muslim.
He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) decreed success for whoever combines these three traits. And ""success"" is a comprehensive term for attaining everything sought and loved, and safety from everything feared and dreaded. That is because these three combine the good of religion and worldly life, for when the servant is guided to Islam, which is the religion of Allah besides which no religion is accepted, and which is the pivot of winning reward and salvation from punishment, and he obtains the provision that suffices him and spares him asking people, then Allah completes the blessing upon him by making him content with what He has given him, and he attains satisfaction with the provision and sufficiency he has been given, and his soul does not aspire to what is beyond that — he has attained the good of this world and the Hereafter. For deficiency through missing these three matters or one of them is either that he is not guided to Islam, in which case, whatever his state, his outcome is eternal misery; or that he is guided to Islam but is afflicted either with a poverty that makes one forget, or a wealth that makes one transgress, both of which are great harm and deficiency; or that he obtains sufficient provision, ample or measured, but he is not content with Allah's provision, and his heart is not at rest with what Allah has given him — such a one is poor in heart and soul. For wealth is not about abundance of goods; wealth is only wealth of the heart. How many owners of fortunes have poor, grief-stricken hearts, and how many poor in means have hearts rich, satisfied, and content with Allah's provision. So the resolute one, when the world becomes straitened for him, does not combine upon himself its straitness and poverty with poverty of heart, its grief and sorrow; rather, just as he strives to obtain provision, let him strive for the rest, tranquillity, and repose of the heart. And Allah knows best."
"74","[AI-translation] From Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: «A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me briefly. He said: When you stand in your prayer, pray the prayer of one bidding farewell, do not speak any word for which you will have to excuse yourself tomorrow, and gather despair of what is in people's hands.» Reported by Ahmad.
What counsels these three are! If the servant takes them, his affairs are completed and he prospers. The first counsel includes perfecting the prayer and striving to perform it in the best of states. That is by holding himself to account over every prayer he prays, that he will complete everything in it of obligations, duties, and sunnahs, and that he verify the station of excellence, which is the highest of stations. That is by standing for it while conscious of standing before his Lord, that he communes with Him by what he says of recitation, remembrance, and supplication, and humbles himself to Him in his standing, bowing, prostration, lowering, and rising. What assists him in this momentous aim is to settle himself upon that without hesitation or laziness of heart, and to be conscious in every prayer that it is the prayer of one bidding farewell, as though he will pray no other. It is known that one bidding farewell strives with a striving in which he exerts all his capacity. He continues to keep present these beneficial meanings and strong causes until the matter becomes easy for him and he grows accustomed to it. Prayer in this form forbids its performer every base trait and urges him to every beautiful trait, because of what it effects in his soul of increase in faith, light of the heart and its joy, and his complete desire for good. As for the second counsel, it is guarding the tongue and watching over it, for upon guarding the tongue turns the pivot, and it is the master of the servant's affair. Whenever the servant masters his tongue, he masters all his limbs, and whenever his tongue masters him and he does not guard it against harmful speech, his affair falls into disorder in his religion and his worldly life. Let him not speak any word except that he has known its benefit in his religion or his worldly life. And every speech that may involve criticism or apology, let him leave it, for when he speaks it, speech masters him and he becomes its captive. It may well bring upon him a harm he cannot remedy. As for the third counsel, it is settling the soul upon attachment to Allah alone in the affairs of livelihood and of the return, so he asks none but Allah, hopes only for His bounty, and settles himself upon despair of what is in people's hands, for despair is a protection, and whoever despairs of a thing dispenses with it. Just as he asks none but Allah with his tongue, let him attach his heart to none but Allah. Then he remains a servant of Allah in truth, safe from servitude to people. He is freed from their bondage, and gains by that honour and nobility, for one attached to people gains disgrace and fall according to his attachment to them. And Allah knows best."
"75","[AI-translation] From Mus'ab ibn Sa'd: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Are you given victory and given provision except through your weak ones?» Reported by al-Bukhari.
In this hadith is that the strong and able should not belittle the weak and incapable, neither in matters of struggle and aid, nor in matters of provision and their inability to earn. The Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) explained that victory over enemies and the spreading of provision may occur by means of the weak, through their devotion, supplication, seeking victory, and seeking provision. That is because the causes by which aims are attained are of two kinds. One kind is witnessed by the senses, namely strength through verbal and physical courage, and through attaining wealth and ability to earn. This kind is what dominates the hearts of most people, and they attach to it the attainment of victory and provision, until the state of many of the people of ignorance reached that they killed their children for fear of poverty, and others reached that they were vexed by their dependents who lacked earnings and whose strength was lost. All this is short-sightedness, weak faith, little trust in Allah's promise and sufficiency, and viewing matters other than as they are. The second kind is unseen causes, namely strength of reliance upon Allah in attaining religious and worldly aims, perfection of trust in Him, and strength of devotion to Him and asking of Him. These matters are very strong in the weak and incapable, whom necessity has driven to know with true knowledge that their sufficiency, provision, and victory are from Allah, and that they are in utter incapacity. So their hearts are broken, and they turn to Allah, and He sends down for them of His aid and provision — of repelling harms and bringing benefits — what the able do not attain. And He facilitates for the able, by means of them, of provision what they had not reckoned; for Allah has appointed for everyone a decreed provision, and He has placed the provisions of these incapable ones at the hands of the able, and aided the able in that, especially those whose trust in Allah is strong and whose souls are at rest in His reward, for Allah opens for such of the causes of victory and provision what had not occurred to them nor ever crossed their imagination. How often a man whose provision was straitened, then when his household and those attached to him grew numerous, Allah widened provision for him from lawful, decreed, divine quarters and causes. And from the angle of Allah's promise, which He does not break: «And whatever you spend of anything, He will replace it» (Qur'an Saba 34:39). And from the angle of the angels' supplication every morning: «O Allah, give the spender a replacement, and give the withholder loss.» And from the angle that the provisions of these weak ones are directed to whoever maintains them, and were at his hand. And from the angle that the giving hand is the upper in every respect. And from the angle that aid from Allah comes according to the burden, and that blessing accompanies everything done for His sake and sought for His reward. For this reason we say: and from the angle of the servant's sincerity to Allah and his drawing near to Him with his heart, tongue, and hand — whenever he spends, he turns to Allah and draws near to Him. And what is for Him is blessed. And from the angle of strength of reliance, the spender's trust, and his hope in Allah's bounty and kindness. Hope and expectation are among the greatest causes for attaining what is sought. And from the angle of the supplication of the oppressed ones spent upon, for they supplicate Allah — standing and sitting, and in all their states — for whoever maintains their sufficiency. And supplication is a strong cause: «And your Lord said: Call upon Me; I will respond to you» (Qur'an Ghafir 40:60). All this is tried and witnessed, so away with the deprived, and how great is the profit of the successful. And Allah knows best."
"76","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Allah laughs at two men, one of whom kills the other, yet both enter Paradise: this one fights in the path of Allah and is killed, then Allah turns to the killer and he becomes Muslim and is martyred.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith shows the diversity of the generosity of the Generous, and that His generosity and bounty are diverse in ways that cannot be counted or enumerated, nor enter the minds and thoughts of people. These two men, one of whom killed the other, Allah decreed for each of them, of His bounty and generosity, a cause that brought him to Paradise. The first fought in His path, and Allah honoured him at the hand of the other man — who had not yet become Muslim — with martyrdom, which is the highest of ranks after the rank of the utterly truthful, his aim in his struggle being to exalt the word of Allah and to draw near to his Lord thereby. So his reward is with Allah, and he has no claim against the killer; his reward is confirmed with Allah. As for the other, Allah, exalted be He, has made the door of repentance open to everyone who desires repentance, through Islam and what is below it, and He has not made any sin a barrier to the acceptance of repentance, as He, exalted be He, said concerning the repentant: «Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins. Indeed, it is He who is the Forgiving, the Merciful» (Qur'an Az-Zumar 39:53). When he became Muslim and repented, Allah effaced from him disbelief and its traces, then favoured him with martyrdom, so he entered Paradise like his brother whom he had killed and whom Allah had honoured at his hand, and He did not disgrace him at his brother's hand through his killing him while a disbeliever. This laughter of the Creator shows the utmost of His generosity and munificence, and the diversity of His kindness. This laughter occurring in this hadith and in other texts besides it is like the rest of Allah's attributes. The believer must acknowledge it and believe in it, that it is true in its reality, and that His attributes are attributes of perfection, in which He has no like, no resemblance, and no equal. Just as Allah has an essence which no essences resemble, so He, exalted be He, has attributes which no attributes resemble, all of them attributes of praise, glory, exaltation, majesty, beauty, and perfection. So we believe in what the Book and the Sunnah have brought of the attributes of our Lord, and we know that faith and affirmation of unity are not complete except by affirming them in a manner befitting the greatness, grandeur, and glory of Allah. This hadith is among the hadiths encouraging entry into Islam and opening the doors of repentance by every means, for Islam effaces what came before it, and what a man did in his state of disbelief — he has become Muslim upon what he sent ahead — even the necks he struck in support of his falsehood, and the wealth he seized for that purpose; all that is pardoned after Islam. Our saying ""for that purpose"" is a guard against the rights entailed by dealings between Muslims and disbelievers, for when a disbeliever becomes Muslim owing rights, debts, and specific properties he took and acquired by dealing, Islam does not drop them, because they are dealings shared among people, their righteous and their wicked, their Muslim and their disbeliever — unlike the first category, for each of the two parties, Muslims and disbelievers, when war occurs and killing and seizure of property follow, nothing is returned except voluntarily, as a bounty from whoever it reached. And Allah knows best. Similar to this in some respects is the fighting of the people of rebellion against the people of justice, where the scholars did not hold them liable for what they destroyed during the war of lives and property on grounds of interpretation, as the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) unanimously agreed when the tribulation occurred: they agreed that what was lost of lives and destroyed of property involves no liability from either side. In His saying «Then Allah turns to the other and he becomes Muslim» is proof that Allah's turning to one who becomes Muslim or repents of his sins precedes the servant's turning, for He, exalted be He, permits his repentance, decrees it, and is kind to him, in that He decrees for him the causes necessitating his repentance; then the servant repents, then Allah turns to him after that by effacing what preceded of crimes — disbelief and what is below it. The servant's repentance is enfolded between two turnings with which his Lord favours him: His permitting, decreeing, and facilitating repentance for him until he repents, then His acceptance of his repentance and effacing of his slip. He is the Oft-Returning, the Merciful. Repentance is among the noblest acts of obedience and the greatest of them; this ruling holds in all acts of obedience: Allah first grants the servant success in them, facilitates their causes for him, and eases their paths for him; then when the obedient one performs them, He accepts them and decrees for him thereby His pleasure and reward. How vast is the bounty of the Generous, and how abundant His diverse, far-reaching generosity. And Allah knows best."
"77","[AI-translation] From Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «None of you should wish for death because of a harm that has befallen him; if he must do so, let him say: O Allah, keep me alive as long as life is good for me, and take me when death is good for me.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This is a prohibition of wishing for death because of the harm that descends upon the servant — illness, poverty, fear, falling into hardship and ruin, or similar things — for in wishing for death for that there are evils. Among them is that it betokens displeasure and impatience with the state by which he has been afflicted, while he is commanded to be patient and to perform his duty. It is known that wishing for death contradicts that. Among them is that it weakens the soul, produces feebleness and laziness, and leads to despair. What is required of the servant is to resist these things and to strive to weaken and lighten them according to his ability, and to have with him strength of heart and strength of hope in the removal of what has befallen him. That necessitates two things: the divine kindness for one who undertakes the commanded causes, and the beneficial striving which strength of heart and hope entail. Among them is that wishing for death is ignorance and folly, for he does not know what comes after death; perhaps he is like one seeking refuge from harm in what is more terrible than it, of the torment of the intermediate state and its horrors. Among them is that death cuts off from the servant the righteous deeds which he is engaged in performing and upholding, and the remainder of the believer's life has no value — so how can he wish for the cutting off of a deed, a speck of which is better than the world and what is in it. And more specific than this general point is his upholding patience over the harm that has befallen him, for Allah repays the patient their reward without account. For this reason he said at the end of the hadith: «If he must do so, let him say: O Allah, keep me alive when life is good for me, and take me when death is good for me», so the servant leaves the matter entrusted to his Lord, who knows what is good and right for him, who knows of His servant's interests what the servant does not know, who wants for him of good what he does not want for himself, and who is kind to him in his affliction as He is kind to him in His blessings. The difference between this and his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «None of you should say: O Allah, forgive me if You will; O Allah, have mercy on me if You will; but let him be resolute in asking, for Allah has no compeller», is that what is mentioned in the hadith involving suspension upon Allah's knowledge and will is in specified matters whose outcome and benefit the servant does not know. As for what is mentioned in the other hadith, they are matters whose benefit is known — indeed their necessity and every servant's need of them — namely Allah's forgiveness and mercy and the like. The servant asks them and seeks them from his Lord with a resolute request, not suspended upon will or otherwise, because he is commanded and required to strive in them and in everything leading to them. This is like the difference between performing the obligatory and recommended duties whose command is established — the servant is commanded to perform them as obligation or recommendation — and certain specified matters whose reality and benefit the servant does not know, where he pauses until the matter becomes clear to him. Many of the people of knowledge excepted from this the permissibility of wishing for death for fear of tribulation, and they counted of this the saying of Maryam (may Allah be pleased with her): «Would that I had died before this» (Qur'an Maryam 19:23). As some of them excepted wishing for death out of longing for Allah, and they counted of it the saying of Yusuf (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «You are my protector in this world and the Hereafter; take me as a Muslim and join me with the righteous» (Qur'an Yusuf 12:101). In this there is a point to consider, for Yusuf did not wish for death; he only asked Allah for steadfastness upon Islam until He takes him as a Muslim, as the servant asks his Lord for a good end. And Allah knows best."
"78","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «This world is sweet and green. And Allah is making you stewards in it, and He sees how you act. So beware of this world, and beware of women; for the first trial of the Children of Israel was concerning women.» Reported by Muslim.
He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith of the state of this world and what it is like, of the description that delights beholders and tasters. Then he informed that Allah made this world a trial and a test for servants, then he commanded taking up the means that guard against falling into its trials. His informing that it is sweet and green covers the descriptions it bears: it is sweet in its taste and flavor, its pleasures and desires, green in its splendor and outward beauty, as the Exalted said: «Beautified for people is the love of desires — of women and sons, heaped-up heaps of gold and silver, branded horses, cattle and tilth» (Qur'an Aal Imran 3:14), and the Exalted said: «Indeed We have made what is upon the earth an adornment for it, that We may test them as to which of them is best in deed» (Qur'an Al-Kahf 7). These varied pleasures in it, and the splendid sights, Allah made a trial and a test from Him, and He placed servants as stewards in it to see how they act. Whoever takes of it from what is lawful, places it in its due place, and seeks help through it for what he was created for — upholding servitude to Allah — it becomes provision for him and a mount toward an abode nobler and more lasting than it, and worldly and otherworldly happiness is completed for him. Whoever makes it his greatest concern and the utmost of his knowledge and aim is given of it only what was decreed for him, and his outcome after that is wretchedness, and he enjoys its pleasures and desires only for a short while; his pleasures are few and his sorrows long. Every kind of its pleasures contains this trial and test, but the most intense and severest in trial is women, for their trial is great, falling into it is dangerous and its harm is great; they are Satan's snares and traps — how many a sound man he has hunted through them, who became a prisoner of his desire, a hostage of his sin, for whom deliverance became hard, though the fault is his own, for he is the one who did not guard himself against this affliction; had he guarded himself from it, not entered places of suspicion, not exposed himself to trial, and sought help through holding fast to the Master, he would have been saved from this trial and delivered from this ordeal. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) warned in this hadith against them in particular, and informed of what befell those before us among the nations; in that is a lesson for those who take lessons and an admonition for the God-fearing. And Allah knows best."
"79","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Faith is seventy-odd — or sixty-odd — branches. The highest of them is the saying: There is no god but Allah; the lowest of them is removing harm from the road; and modesty is a branch of faith.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith is among the texts indicating that faith is a name comprising the beliefs and deeds of the heart, the deeds of the limbs, and the sayings of the tongue; everything that draws near to Allah, that He loves and approves, of obligatory and recommended acts, is part of faith. He mentioned here its highest and its lowest, and what is between them — modesty. «Modesty is a branch of faith» — modesty was perhaps mentioned because it is the strongest cause for upholding all the branches of faith. For whoever is modest before Allah due to the succession of His blessings, His abundant generosity, and His manifesting Himself to him through His beautiful names — while the servant, despite this, falls much short toward this Majestic, Great Lord, wronging himself and offending against it — this modesty necessitates for him guarding against crimes and upholding obligations and recommended acts. The highest of these branches, their root and foundation, is the saying «There is no god but Allah» truthfully from his heart, such that he knows and is certain that none deserves this mighty description — godhood — except Allah alone; for He is his Lord who nurtures him and nurtures all the worlds by His bounty and beneficence; all are poor and He is Rich; all are incapable and He is Strong. Then he upholds, in all his states, servitude to his Lord, devoting religion sincerely to Him; for all the branches of faith are offshoots and fruits of this root. It indicates that some branches of faith go back to sincerity toward the truly worshipped, and some go back to beneficence toward creatures. He drew attention, by removing harm, to all kinds of beneficence in word and deed: beneficence in which benefits reach others, and beneficence in which harms are repelled from creatures. Once we know that all the branches of faith go back to these matters, we know that every trait of good is among the branches. Scholars have spoken on specifying them: some reached this estimated number in the hadith, and some came close to it. But once the meaning is understood, a person can count every trait reported from the Lawgiver — in word or deed, outward or inward — among the branches; the servant's share of faith is according to his share of these traits, in littleness and abundance, strength and weakness, completion and its opposite. They go back to believing the report of Allah and the report of His Messenger, complying with their command, and avoiding their prohibition. Allah has described faith as a good tree, firm in its root with its branches towering in the sky, yielding its fruit at all times by permission of its Lord; and Allah sets forth parables for people that they may remember. And Allah knows best."
"80","[AI-translation] On the authority of Adi ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «There is none among you but his Lord will speak to him, with no interpreter between him and Him. He will look to his right and see nothing but what he sent forth; he will look to his left and see nothing but what he sent forth; he will look before him and see nothing but the Fire facing him. So guard against the Fire, even with half a date; whoever does not find, then with a good word.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This is a mighty hadith comprising, of the majesty of the Creator, what minds cannot encompass and tongues cannot express. He informed therein that all creatures — Allah will speak to them directly, without interpreter or intermediary, and will ask them about all their deeds, good and evil, minute and momentous, past and later, what servants know of them and what they forgot. That is because, due to His greatness and grandeur, just as He creates them and provides for them in a single hour, and resurrects them in a single hour, He will reckon them all in a single hour; blessed is He who possesses majesty and glory, immense dominion and grandeur. In this state in which He reckons them, the servant has with him no helpers nor supporters, no children nor wealth; he has come to Him alone as He created him the first time; his deeds have encompassed him, seeking recompense in good or evil, on his right and his left, and before him is the Fire which he must pass over — so is there a way to emerge from it safely? There is no way to that except by Allah's mercy, and by what his hands sent forth of deeds that save from it. For this reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged his community to guard against the Fire even with a small thing, like half a date; whoever does not find, then with a good word. In this hadith: among the greatest means of salvation from the Fire is beneficence to creatures with wealth and words, and the servant should not belittle any good, even something small. The good word includes advising creatures by teaching them what they are ignorant of and guiding them to their religious and worldly interests; it includes speech that gladdens hearts, expands breasts, accompanied by cheerfulness and brightness; it includes remembrance of Allah and praise of Him, and mention of His rulings and laws. Every speech that draws near to Allah and by which benefit to Allah's servants is attained is included in the good word. The Exalted said: «To Him ascends good speech, and righteous work raises it» (Qur'an Fatir 35:10). And the Exalted said: «The lasting righteous deeds are better with your Lord in reward and better in hope» (Qur'an Al-Kahf 46). And Allah knows best."
"81","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: «Leave me with what I have left you. Those before you were destroyed only by their frequent questioning and their disagreeing with their prophets. When I forbid you something, avoid it; and when I command you to something, do of it what you can.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
These questions which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade are those which Allah forbade in His saying: «O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would harm you» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 5:101) — questions about things among matters of the unseen, or among matters which Allah pardoned, neither forbidding nor obligating them, where the questioner asks about them at the time of the descent of revelation and legislation, so perhaps they become obligatory because of the question, or forbidden likewise, and the questioner enters into his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «The greatest of Muslims in crime is one who asked about something not forbidden, and it was forbidden because of his asking.» Likewise the servant is forbidden contentious and entangling questions; he is also forbidden to ask about trivial, unimportant matters while leaving asking about important matters. These questions and their likes are what the Lawgiver forbade. As for asking by way of seeking guidance about religious matters, of principles and branches, worship or transactions — it is among what Allah and His Messenger commanded, among what they urged; it is the means to learning sciences and grasping realities. The Exalted said: «So ask the people of remembrance if you do not know» (Qur'an Al-Anbiya 7). And He said: «And ask those We sent before you of Our messengers: did We make, besides the Merciful, gods to be worshipped?» (Qur'an Az-Zukhruf 45), and other verses. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whomever Allah wants good for, He gives him understanding in religion» — through treading the path of understanding religion by study, learning and asking. And he said: «Why did they not ask, when they did not know? For the cure of incapacity is asking.» Allah commanded gentleness with the asker, giving him what he seeks, and not being vexed with him. He said in Surat Ad-Duha: «As for the asker, do not repel him» (Qur'an Ad-Duha 10). This includes the asker about beneficial sciences and the asker for what he needs of worldly matters, wealth and otherwise. Among what enters into this hadith: asking about the modality of the Creator's attributes, for the matter concerning all attributes is as Imam Malik said to one who asked him about the modality of rising over the Throne: ""Rising is known, the modality is unknown, belief in it is obligatory, and asking about it is innovation."" Whoever asks about the modality of Allah's knowledge, or the modality of His creating and administering, is told: just as Allah's essence is unlike essences, His attributes are unlike attributes; creatures know Allah and know what He made known to them of His attributes and deeds; as for the modality of that, none knows its interpretation except Allah. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in this hadith two mighty principles. The first: his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «When I forbid you something, avoid it.» Whatever the Prophet forbade — of sayings and deeds, outward and inward — must be left and refrained from, in compliance and obedience to Allah and His Messenger. He did not say regarding prohibition ""what you can,"" because prohibition is a demand to restrain the soul, which everyone is capable of; everyone can leave all that Allah and His Messenger forbade. Servants were not compelled to any of the absolutely forbidden things; lawful things are wide, encompassing all creatures in their worship, transactions and all their dealings. As for permitting carrion, blood and pork for the compelled, in that compelling state it becomes of the kind of the lawful; necessities permit prohibitions, rendering them permissible by necessity; for He only forbade forbidden things to preserve His servants, guard them against evils and corruptions, and benefit them. When that is countered by a greater interest — the survival of the soul — the latter is given precedence, out of Allah's mercy and beneficence. Medicines are not of this chapter, for medicine does not enter the chapter of necessities; Allah heals the afflicted by various means, not restricted to medicine. Even if healing by medicine is strongly presumed, it is not lawful to seek cure with forbidden things, like wine, the milk of domestic donkeys, and kinds of forbidden things — unlike one compelled to eat carrion, who is certain that if he does not eat of it he will die. The second principle: his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «When I command you to something, do of it what you can.» This is a great principle, also indicated by His saying: «So fear Allah as much as you can» (Qur'an At-Taghabun 16). All commands of the Law are tied to the servant's ability and capacity. Whoever cannot perform an obligation at all, its obligation lapses from him. Whoever can perform part of it — that part being worship — must perform what he can of it, and what he cannot lapses from him. Included in this, of questions of jurisprudence and rulings, is what cannot be counted. The sick prays standing; if unable, sitting; if unable, on his side; if unable to gesture with his head, he gestures with his eyelid. The servant fasts as long as able; if a disease whose recovery is not hoped for incapacitates him, he feeds a poor person for each day; if a disease whose recovery is hoped for, he breaks fast and makes up days later. Of that: whoever cannot provide the obligatory covering in prayer, or facing the qiblah, or avoiding impurity — what he cannot lapses. Likewise the rest of the conditions and pillars of prayer, and the conditions of purification; whoever cannot purify with water — due to absence or harm in all or part of purification — moves to purification by tayammum. One chronically unable in Hajj must delegate one to perform Hajj for him, if able with his wealth. Enjoining right and forbidding wrong is obligatory on whoever can, by hand, then tongue, then heart. There is no blame on the blind, the lame and the sick in leaving acts of worship they cannot perform or which cause them unbearable hardship. Whoever owes obligatory maintenance and cannot provide all of it begins with his wife, then his slave, then child, then parents, then the nearest then the nearest. Likewise the fitrah charity. Thus everything the servant is commanded — obligatory or recommended — if he can do part and cannot the rest, what he can is obligatory upon him and what he cannot lapses. All are included in this hadith. Questions of drawing lots enter into this principle; when matters are uncertain — whose they are, who has more right — we return to preponderant factors; if preponderance is impossible in every respect, this duty lapses due to inability, and recourse is had to drawing lots, which is the utmost possible — many well-known questions in books of jurisprudence. All offices of authority — small and great — enter under this principle: in every office, one described with the qualities attaining the office's purpose must be appointed; if all are unattainable, the best available then the next best must be appointed. Just as this principle is inferred from that verse and hadith, it is inferred from the verses and hadiths in which Allah and His Messenger negated hardship from the community, like His saying: «Allah does not burden a soul except its capacity» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 286); «Let one of means spend from his means; and one whose provision is restricted, let him spend from what Allah gave him. Allah does not burden a soul except what He gave it» (Qur'an At-Talaq 7); «He has not placed upon you in religion any hardship» (Qur'an Al-Hajj 78); «Allah does not want to place upon you any hardship» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 6); «Allah wants ease for you and does not want hardship for you» (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 185); «Allah wants to lighten for you» (Qur'an An-Nisa 28). The legal lightenings in worship and otherwise, of all kinds, enter into this principle — along with what is inferred for this from Allah's names and attributes necessitating it, like praise, wisdom, vast mercy, gentleness, generosity and graciousness. The traces of these majestic, beautiful names, just as they are abundant, ample and vast in creatures and administrations, are likewise in laws — indeed greater, for they are the goal in creation and the greatest means to eternal happiness. Allah created the accountable to uphold servitude to Him, and made servitude to Him and upholding His law the path to attaining His pleasure and honor. As the Exalted said — after legislating purification of its kinds: «Allah does not want to place upon you hardship, but He wants to purify you and complete His blessing upon you, that you may be grateful» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 6). The traces of His mercy and blessing appeared in legal and permissible matters, as they appeared in existing things. To Him belongs the most complete praise and the highest, the most abundant thanks and eulogy and the highest, and the utmost love and magnification and its end. And success is from Allah."
"82","[AI-translation] On the authority of Jarir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whoever does not have mercy on people, Allah will not have mercy on him.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith indicates by its explicit wording that whoever does not have mercy on people, Allah will not have mercy on him; and by its implied meaning that whoever has mercy on people, Allah will have mercy on him — as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in another hadith: «The merciful will be shown mercy by the Merciful; have mercy on those on earth, and He who is in heaven will have mercy on you.» A servant's mercy toward creatures is among the greatest means by which Allah's mercy is attained — whose traces are the goods of this world and the goods of the Hereafter; its absence is among the greatest barriers and obstacles to Allah's mercy. The servant is in utmost need and poverty toward Allah's mercy; he cannot dispense with it for a blink of an eye; all he enjoys of blessings and repulsion of calamities is from Allah's mercy. Whenever he wants to preserve them and increase them, let him perform all the means by which His mercy is attained; they are all gathered in His saying: «Indeed Allah's mercy is near to the doers of good» (Qur'an Al-A'raf 56) — those excellent in worshipping Allah, excellent toward Allah's servants. Beneficence to creatures is a trace of the servant's mercy toward them. The mercy with which a servant is described is of two kinds. The first kind: instinctive mercy, with which Allah has naturally endowed some servants, placing in their hearts tenderness, mercy and affection toward creatures; they do, by its dictate, all they can of benefiting them, according to their capacity. They are praised and rewarded for what they upheld, excused for what they could not; perhaps Allah records for them, by their truthful intentions, what their strengths fell short of. The second kind: mercy the servant acquires by treading every path and means that makes his heart upon this description. The servant knows this description is among the noblest and most complete morals, so he strives with himself to be described by it; he knows what Allah attached to it of reward, and what deprivation of reward lies in missing it; he desires his Lord's bounty and strives by the means attaining it. He knows recompense is of the kind of deed. He knows religious brotherhood and faith-based love, which Allah has established and bound between believers, commanding them to be loving brothers, discarding all that contradicts it — hatred, enmities and turning away. The servant keeps recognizing the means by which he attains this noble description, striving to realize it, until his heart fills with mercy and affection toward creatures. How lovely is this excellent character and complete noble description! This mercy in hearts — its traces appear on limbs and tongue, in striving to convey righteousness, good and benefits to people, and removing harms and disliked things from them. The sign of mercy present in a servant's heart is that he loves good reaching all creatures generally, and believers particularly, and hates harm and injury befalling them; his mercy is according to this love and hate. Whoever is struck by his beloved's death or other calamities — if his grief for him is out of mercy, it is praiseworthy and does not contradict patience and contentment, for he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he wept at the death of his daughter's son, Sa'd said to him: ""What is this, O Messenger of Allah?"" He followed it with another tear and said: «This is mercy which Allah places in the hearts of His servants; Allah has mercy only on the merciful among His servants.» And he said at the death of his son Ibrahim: «The heart grieves, the eye sheds tears, and we say only what pleases our Lord; and at parting from you, O Ibrahim, we grieve.» Likewise mercy toward small children, tenderness to them and bringing them joy is of mercy; disregard for them and lack of tenderness is of harshness, coarseness and hardheartedness. As one of the coarse Bedouins said when he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions kissing their small children — the Bedouin said: I have ten children, I never kissed one of them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Can I own anything for you, if Allah has stripped mercy from your heart?» Of mercy: the mercy of the prostitute woman when she gave water to the dog nearly eating dust from thirst — Allah forgave her because of that mercy. Its opposite: the torment of the woman who tied up the cat — neither feeding nor watering it, nor leaving it to eat of the vermin of the earth — until it died. Of that is what is witnessed and tried: whoever does well to his beasts by feeding, watering and beneficial care, Allah blesses them for him; whoever does ill to them is punished in this world before the Hereafter. The Exalted said: «Because of that We decreed upon the Children of Israel that whoever kills a soul without a soul or corruption in the land, it is as if he killed all mankind; and whoever gives it life, it is as if he gave life to all mankind» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 32) — because of what is in the first's heart of harshness, coarseness and evil, and what is in the other's heart of mercy, tenderness and affection, as he is engaged in giving life to everyone he can give life to among people — just as what is in the first's heart of harshness is prepared to kill all souls."
"83","[AI-translation] On the authority of Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Whoever loves that his provision be expanded for him and his trace be deferred for him, let him maintain his kinship ties.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains urging to maintain kinship ties, and explanation that just as they necessitate Allah's pleasure and His reward in the Hereafter, they necessitate immediate reward — the attainment of the most beloved matters to the servant — and that they are a cause for expanding and enlarging provision, and a cause for long life. That is true in reality; He is the Creator of causes and their effects. Allah made for every sought thing a cause and path by which it is attained. This runs upon the great principle: of His wisdom and praiseworthiness, He made recompense of the kind of deed. Just as he joined his kin with varied righteousness and beneficence, bringing joy into their hearts, Allah joined his life, joined his provision, and opened for him doors of provision and its blessings unattained without this noble cause. Just as health, wholesome air, wholesome food, and using matters strengthening bodies and hearts are causes of long life, likewise maintaining kinship ties Allah made a Lordly cause. The causes by which desired worldly things are attained are two divisions: witnessed matters entering the perception of senses and the grasp of minds; and Lordly, divine matters decreed by Him who is powerful over all things, to whom all causes and worldly matters submit by His will, who guarantees sufficiency to the reliant and promised provision and exit from straits to the God-fearing. The Exalted said: «Whoever fears Allah, He makes for him a way out, and provides for him whence he does not expect; whoever relies upon Allah, He suffices him» (Qur'an At-Talaq 2-3). If the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says: «Charity does not decrease wealth» — indeed it increases it — then what of charity and gifts to relatives and kin? In this hadith is proof that the worker's intending what follows his deed of worldly reward does not harm him, when the deed's aim is Allah's face and the abode of the Hereafter. For Allah, by His wisdom and mercy, arranged immediate and deferred reward, promising workers that; hope and anticipation thereof energize workers and rouse their zeal for good — just as threat for crimes and mention of their punishments, by which Allah frightens His servants and rouses them to leave sins and crimes. The truthful believer is, in his doing and leaving, sincere to Allah, seeking help by what deeds contain of varied incentives toward this highest aim. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"84","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «A person is with whomever he loves.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
«A person is with whomever he loves» — this hadith contains urging to strong love of the messengers and following them according to their ranks, and warning against loving their opposites. Love is proof of strong connection of the lover to whom he loves, his suitability to his morals, and his emulating him — proof of that existence, and also a motive to it. Also, whoever loves Allah the Exalted — his very love is among the greatest of what draws him near to Allah; Allah the Exalted is Appreciative, giving the one drawing near greater — manifold multiples — than what he expended. Of His appreciation is that He joins him with whom he loves, even if his deed falls short. The Exalted said: «Whoever obeys Allah and the Messenger — they are with those Allah blessed among the prophets, the utterly truthful, the martyrs and the righteous; excellent companions are they» (Qur'an An-Nisa 69). For this reason Anas said: ""We never rejoiced at anything as we rejoiced at his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «A person is with whomever he loves.»"" He said: ""I love the Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr and Umar, so I hope to be with them."" The Exalted said: «Gardens of Eden which they enter, and whoever was righteous among their fathers, spouses and offspring» (Qur'an Ar-Ra'd 23). The Glorious said: «Those who believed, and their offspring followed them in faith — We join their offspring with them, and We diminish them nothing of their deed» (Qur'an At-Tur 21). This is witnessed and tried: when a servant loves the people of good, you see him joining them, keen to be like them; when he loves the people of evil, he joins them and does their deeds. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «A person is upon the religion of his close friend, so let each of you consider whom he befriends.» And: «The example of the righteous companion is like the carrier of musk: either he gives you some, or sells to you, or you find from him a pleasant scent; and the example of the evil companion is like the blower of the bellows: either he burns your clothes, or you find with him a foul smell.»
And if this is so in love among creatures, how then of one who loves Allah and gives His love and fear precedence over everything? He is with Allah, and has attained complete nearness to Him — the nearness of lovers — and Allah is with him. For «indeed Allah is with those who feared and those who do good» (Qur'an An-Nahl 16:128). And the highest kind of excellence is love of the All-Merciful, the Generous, the Most Merciful — love joined with knowledge of Him. So we ask Allah to grant us His love, the love of whoever loves Him, and the love of the deed that draws near to His love; indeed He is Munificent, Generous. And success is from Allah."
"85","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he mounted his camel going out on a journey, would magnify Allah three times, then say: Glory to Him who subjected this to us, and we were not capable of it; and indeed to our Lord we shall return. O Allah, we ask You in this journey of ours righteousness and God-consciousness, and of deeds what pleases You. O Allah, ease for us this journey of ours, and fold for us its distance. O Allah, You are the Companion in travel and the Successor over family. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel, the gloom of aspect, and an evil return regarding wealth, family and child. And when he returned, he said them and added therein: «Returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord.» Reported by Muslim.
This hadith contains great benefits concerning travel. These supplications comprise asking for religious interests — the most important matters — and worldly interests; attaining beloved things and repelling hated, harmful things; thanking Allah's blessings, remembering His favors and generosity; and the journey's comprising obedience to Allah and what draws near to Him. His saying: ""When he mounted his riding beast going out on a journey, he magnified three times"" — the opening of his journey with magnifying Allah and praising Him, as he would seal it therewith. His saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): «Glory to Him who subjected this to us, and we were not capable of it, and indeed to our Lord we shall return» — therein is praise of Allah for subjecting mounts carrying heavy loads and souls to distant lands and vast regions, and acknowledgment of Allah's blessing of mounts. This includes mounts: of camels, of sea and land and air ships — all enter into this. For this reason Noah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the riders with him in the ship: «Embark therein; in Allah's name is its course and its anchorage» (Qur'an Hud 41). All these conveyances, their means, and what completes and perfects them are of Allah's blessings and subjection. Servants must acknowledge Allah's blessing therein, especially when undertaking them. Therein is remembering the state which, but for the Creator, would not have occurred and been made tractable, in his saying: «and we were not capable of it» — i.e., able, had the matter been referred to our power and strength; we would be the weakest thing in knowledge, ability and will — but He subjected animals and taught man the craft of conveyances. As Allah favored in facilitating the craft of protective coats of mail, in His saying: «And We taught him the craft of garments for you, to protect you from your violence; are you then thankful?» (Qur'an Al-Anbiya 80). So upon creatures is to thank Allah, since He taught them the craft of garments covering nakedness, garments of adornment, garments of war and instruments of war; taught them the craft of ships by sea, land, and air, and the craft of all they need to benefit from; and sent down iron, wherein is mighty violence and varied benefits for people. But most creatures are heedless of thanking Allah — rather in insolence and arrogance toward Allah, tyrannizing servants by these blessings. In this hadith is reminding, by the sensory journey of this world, of the moral journey of the Hereafter, by his saying: «and indeed to our Lord we shall return.» For just as He began creation, He shall return them, to recompense those who did evil for what they did, and recompense those who did good with the best. And his saying: «O Allah, we ask You in this journey of ours righteousness and God-consciousness, and of deeds what pleases You» — he asked Allah that the journey be described by this glorious description, comprising all deeds of righteousness attached to Allah's right and to creatures' rights, and God-consciousness — guarding against Allah's wrath by abandoning all Allah dislikes of outward and inward deeds and words — just as he asked Him for deeds with which Allah is pleased. This includes all obedience and means of nearness; and whenever the journey is upon this description, it is a profitable journey, a blessed journey. And all his journeys (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) comprised these glorious meanings. Then he asked Allah for aid and easing the hardships of travel, saying: «O Allah, ease for us this journey of ours, and fold for us its distance» — for travel is a portion of torment, so he asked its easing and the folding of its distance: by lightening cares and hardships, and by blessing in the course, until he covers distant expanses while untroubled; and He provides for him of the comforting causes in travel many matters, such as rest of heart, suitable companionship, easing of the course, safety of the road from fears, and other causes. How many a journey stretched many days, yet Allah eased and facilitated it for its people; and how many a short journey became harder than all hard — so there is nothing but Allah's facilitation, kindness, and aid. For this reason he said, in realizing the easing of travel: «O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel» — its difficulty and hardness — «and the gloom of aspect» — abiding sorrow and constant care — «and an evil return regarding wealth, family and child» — i.e., O Lord, we ask You to guard for us all we left behind us and parted from by our journey, of family, child, and wealth; and that we return to them gladdened by safety and blessings successive upon us and them. By that the blessing is completed and joy perfected. Likewise he says this upon his return, coming back from his journey, adding: «Returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord» — i.e., we ask You, O Allah, to make us in our return and coming back constant in repentance to You, worship of You, and praise of You; and to seal our journey with obedience to You, as You opened it with granting success therein. For this reason He Almighty said: «And say: My Lord, admit me an entry of truthfulness and bring me out an exit of truthfulness, and grant me from Yourself a supporting authority» (Qur'an Al-Isra 80). The entry of truthfulness and its exit is that the servant's journeys, entries, and exits all comprise truthfulness and truth, occupation with what Allah loves, joined with reliance upon Allah and accompanied by His aid. In it is acknowledging His blessing at the end, as he acknowledged it at the beginning, in his saying: «praising our Lord.» For just as upon the servant is to praise Allah for granting success to do worship and begin the need, upon him is to praise Allah for its completion, perfection, and finishing — for bounty is His bounty, good is His good, and the causes are His causes. And Allah is of immense bounty."
"86","[AI-translation] On the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both): that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Take your rites from me.» Reported by Ahmad, Muslim and al-Nasa'i.
This is comprehensive speech by which people of knowledge inferred the legislation of all the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did and said in his Hajj — obligatory in obligations, recommended in recommended acts. It is like his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning prayer: «Pray as you saw me pray.» Just as that covers all details of prayer, this covers all details of rites. Shaykh al-Islam Ibn Taymiyyah has a very fine discussion in a summary of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) Hajj, which he mentioned in ""Al-Qawa'id an-Nuraniyyah."" He said — may Allah sanctify his soul and be pleased with him: It is established by continuous transmission among the specialists among hadith scholars, by many routes in the two Sahihs and others, that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he performed the Farewell Hajj, he and the Muslims entered ihram from Dhu al-Hulayfah. He said: «Whoever wishes to enter ihram for Umrah, let him do so; whoever wishes to enter ihram for Hajj, let him do so; and whoever wishes to enter ihram for Umrah and Hajj, let him do so.» When they arrived and circumambulated the House and between Safa and Marwah, he commanded all the Muslims who performed Hajj with him to exit their ihram and make it an Umrah, except whoever had driven the sacrificial animal, for he does not exit ihram until the sacrificial animal reaches its place. Some of them argued with him about that, so he became angry and said: «Look at what I have commanded you and do it.» He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) himself had driven the sacrificial animal, so he did not exit his ihram, and when he saw the dislike of some of them for exiting ihram, he said: «Had I known beforehand what I know now, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal, and I would have made it an Umrah; and were it not that the sacrificial animal is with me, I would have exited ihram.» He also said: «I have matted my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I sacrifice.» So all the Muslims exited ihram except the few who had driven the sacrificial animal; among them: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali ibn Abi Talib, and Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah. When the Day of Tarwiyah came, those who had exited ihram entered ihram for Hajj as they were going to Mina; he spent that night with them at Mina and prayed with them there the Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr. Then he journeyed with them to Namirah by the Dabb route — Namirah lies outside Arafah, to its south and west; it is neither part of the Sanctuary nor part of Arafah. A tent was pitched for him at Namirah, and there his Rightly-Guided Caliphs would alight after him; there are the markets, the relieving of needs, eating, and the like. When the sun declined, he and those who rode with him mounted, and the Muslims proceeded to the place of prayer in the valley of Uranah, where the mosque was later built; it is neither part of the Sanctuary nor part of Arafah. It is rather an isthmus between the two holy tracts — the non-sacred and the sacred — there; between it and the standing place is about a mile. He delivered to them the Hajj sermon upon his mount — it was Friday — then he descended and prayed with them the Dhuhr and Asr, shortened and combined. Then he — with the Muslims — proceeded to the standing place at Arafah, by the mountain known as the Mount of Mercy. Its name is Ilal, on the pattern of Hilal, and it is what the common people call Arafah. He and the Muslims remained in remembrance and supplication until the sun set, then he moved with them to Muzdalifah, and prayed the Maghrib and Isha after the twilight had disappeared, before the unloading of the mounts, when they alighted at Muzdalifah. He spent the night there until dawn broke, then prayed the Fajr with the Muslims at the very beginning of its time, praying it in deep darkness more than on any other day. Then he stood at Quzah — the mountain of Muzdalifah called the Sacred Monument — and remained standing with the Muslims until it was fully light. Then he moved with them until he reached Mina, beginning there with stoning the Jamrat al-Aqabah. Then he returned to his lodging at Mina, then came to the place of sacrifice and sacrificed sixty-three camels of the sacrificial animals he had driven, and he commanded Ali, who sacrificed the remainder, the total being one hundred camels, then he shaved his head. Then he went on to Makkah and performed the Tawaf al-Ifadah. He had sent ahead the weak among his family from Muzdalifah before dawn broke, and they stoned the Jamrah by night. Then he stayed with the Muslims the three days of Mina, praying with them the five prayers shortened but not combined, stoning each day the three Jamrahs after the sun's decline, beginning with the first Jamrah — the small one, the nearest to Mina and the farthest from Makkah — and ending with the Jamrat al-Aqabah, standing between the first and second Jamrahs, and between the second and third, a long standing for about the length of Surat al-Baqarah, remembering Allah and supplicating; for the standing places are three: Arafah, Muzdalifah and Mina. Then on the last of the days of Tashriq, after stoning the Jamrahs, he and the Muslims departed, and alighted at al-Muhassab, at the defile of Banu Kinanah. He and the Muslims spent the night of Wednesday there, and that night he sent Aishah with her brother Abd al-Rahman to perform Umrah from al-Tan'im — the nearest edge of the Sanctuary to Makkah, on the road of the people of Madinah. A mosque was later built there which people call the Mosque of Aishah; for none of his Companions after Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ever performed an Umrah except Aishah, because she had menstruated when she arrived while she was in ihram for Umrah. So she did not circumambulate the House before the standing, nor between Safa and Marwah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: «Do what the pilgrim does, except that you shall not circumambulate the House, nor between Safa and Marwah.» Then he and the Muslims bade farewell to the House and returned to Madinah. He did not remain after the days of Tashriq, and none ever in his time performed an Umrah in which he goes out from the Sanctuary to the non-sacred territory except Aishah alone — may Allah be pleased with her. The jurists of hadith — like Ahmad and others — adopted his Sunnah in all that, up to the end of what he said — may Allah have mercy on him and be pleased with him. And Allah knows best."
"87","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «Say: He is Allah, the One — equals a third of the Qur'an.» Reported by Muslim.
«Say: He is Allah, the One — equals a third of the Qur'an.» People of knowledge discussed the meaning of this equivalence and its explanation. The best said concerning it: its equivalence to a third of the Qur'an is due to what it contains of mighty meanings — meanings of monotheism and foundations of faith. For the majestic topics the Qur'an comprises are: 1 - either legal rulings, outward or inward, worship or transactions; 2 - or stories and reports about past and later creatures, and the states of the accountable in recompense for deeds; 3 - or monotheism and cognitions concerning Allah's names and attributes, His singling out with oneness and perfection, His transcendence above every defect and resemblance to any creature. Surat Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad comprises this, encompassing all that must be believed of this foundation — the foundation of all foundations. For this reason Allah commanded us to say it with our tongues, know it with our hearts, acknowledge it and profess to Allah belief in it and worship of Allah through it. He said: «Say: He is Allah, the One» (Qur'an Al-Ikhlas 1). Allah: the God deserving all meanings of godhood, necessitating that He alone be worshipped, alone praised, alone thanked, magnified, sanctified, possessor of majesty and honor. ""The One"" means: singled out with every perfection — glory, majesty, beauty, praise, wisdom, mercy and other attributes of perfection. He has in them no like, no equal, no counterpart in any respect. He is One in His life and self-subsistence, His knowledge and power, His greatness and majesty, His beauty and praise, His wisdom and mercy, and His other attributes — described with the utmost and end of perfection in each of these attributes. Of the realization of His oneness and singling out is that He is «the Eternal» (Qur'an Al-Ikhlas 2) — i.e., the Perfect Lord, the Mighty Master, in whom no attribute of perfection remains undescribed; described with its utmost and perfection, such that creatures cannot encompass some of those attributes with their hearts, nor express them with their tongues. He is the One repaired to, intended in all needs and calamities: «All in the heavens and earth ask of Him; every day He is upon a matter» (Qur'an Ar-Rahman 29). He is Rich in Himself, and all beings are poor to Him in themselves — in their bringing into existence, preparing, and supplying with all they need in every respect; none of them dispenses with Him by a speck's weight in any state. The Eternal: the One repaired to, intended in everything, for His perfection, generosity, munificence and beneficence. Hence: «He neither begets nor is born» (Qur'an Al-Ikhlas 3). For all creatures are generated, some from others — some begetting, some begotten; every creature is created from matter. As for the Lord, glorified be He, He is transcendent above resembling them in this description, as He is transcendent above resembling them in every attribute of deficiency. Hence He affirmed that transcendence and completed that perfection by His saying: «And there is none comparable to Him» (Qur'an Al-Ikhlas 4) — i.e., He has no like, no match, no peer: not in His names, nor His attributes, nor His deeds, nor in all His exclusive rights. His exclusive right is two matters: singling out with all perfection in every respect, and pure servitude from all creatures. A surah comprising these mighty sentences rightly equals a third of the Qur'an; for all in the Qur'an of beautiful names, mighty lofty attributes, Allah's deeds and rulings of His attributes are details of these names mentioned in this surah — indeed all in the Qur'an of outward and inward servitudes, their kinds and details, is a detailing of this surah's content. And Allah knows best."
"88","[AI-translation] On the authority of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «There is no envy except in two: a man to whom Allah gave wealth and empowered him to spend it in truth; and a man to whom Allah gave wisdom, and he judges by it and teaches it.» Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
Envy is two kinds: a kind forbidden and blameworthy in every case — wishing the removal of Allah's blessing from a servant, religious or worldly, whether he loves that with a love settled in his heart without struggling against it, or strives along with that to remove and conceal it — this is uglier, for it is repeated wrongdoing. This kind eats good deeds as fire eats wood. The second kind: not wishing the removal of Allah's blessing from another, but wishing to attain its like, or above or below it. This is two kinds: praiseworthy and unpraiseworthy. The praiseworthy: seeing Allah's religious blessing upon His servant and wishing to have its like — of the chapter of wishing good; if striving and working to attain it accompanies it, it is light upon light. The greatest to be envied in this noble sense: one with wealth attained lawfully, then empowered and granted success to spend it in truth — in obligatory and recommended dues; this is among the greatest proofs of faith and greatest kinds of beneficence. And one with knowledge and wisdom Allah taught him, granted success to expend it in teaching and judging between people — these two kinds of beneficence are unmatched. The first benefits creatures with his wealth, relieves their needs, spends on charitable projects — they stand, their benefit continues, their impact magnifies. The second benefits people with his knowledge, spreading among them the religion and knowledge by which servants are guided in all their matters — worship, transactions and otherwise. Then after these two, emulation for good is according to its state and degrees with Allah. For this reason Allah commanded joy and gladness at this good's attainment, and that none is granted success to it but people of mighty, lofty fortunes. The Exalted said: «Say: By Allah's bounty and His mercy — in that let them rejoice; it is better than what they gather» (Qur'an Yunus 58). And He said: «Good and evil are not equal. Repel by what is better; then he between whom and you is enmity becomes as an intimate ally. None attains it but the patient; none attains it but one of great fortune» (Qur'an Fussilat 34-35). One who wishes something of these good things may have the like of the doer's reward, if his intention is truthful and his resolve determined — that if able to do it, he would do its like — as established by the hadith; especially if he begins and undertakes some striving. As for unpraiseworthy emulation, it is wishing worldly aims for pleasures and indulging desires — as the Exalted said, relating of Qarun's people: «Would that we had the like of what Qarun was given; indeed he is of great fortune» (Qur'an Al-Qasas 79). Wishing the like of the state of one doing evil deeds — by his intention, their burden is equal. By this detail, the envy blameworthy in every case becomes clear, and the envy which is emulation — praised in one case, blamed in another. And Allah knows best."
"89","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): ""The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate, saying: O Allah, I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity and self-sufficiency."" Reported by Muslim.
This supplication, ""O Allah, I ask You for guidance and piety,"" is among the most comprehensive and most beneficial of supplications. It includes asking for the good of religion and the good of worldly life. For ""guidance"" is beneficial knowledge, and ""piety"" is righteous action and abandoning what Allah and His Messenger have forbidden. By that the religion is set right, for religion consists of beneficial sciences and true knowledge — that is guidance — and standing in obedience to Allah and His Messenger — that is piety. And ""chastity and self-sufficiency"" includes chastity toward people and not attaching the heart to them, and richness through Allah and His provision, contentment with what one has, and attaining what gives the heart tranquillity in the form of sufficiency. By that the happiness of worldly life and the rest of the heart are completed, and that is the good life. Whoever is granted guidance, piety, chastity and self-sufficiency attains the two happinesses, obtains everything sought, and is saved from everything feared. And Allah knows best."
"90","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever wishes to be moved away from the Fire and admitted into Paradise, let death come to him while he believes in Allah and the Last Day, and let him treat people in the way he loves to be treated by them."" Reported by Muslim.
There is no doubt that whoever is moved away from the Fire and admitted into Paradise has indeed triumphed, and that this is a goal toward which all believers strive. In this hadith the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned two means to it, to which all branches and offshoots return: faith in Allah and the Last Day — which includes faith in the foundations that Allah mentioned in His saying: ""Say: We believe in Allah"" (Qur'an Al-Baqarah 2:136) — and which includes working for the Hereafter and preparing for it, for sound faith requires and entails that — and doing good to people, conveying to them in word, deed, wealth and dealings what one loves to be treated with by them. This is the sound measure for kindness and sincere advice: in every matter of dealing with people that is unclear to you, consider whether you would love to be treated with that treatment or not. If you would love that, then you love for them what you love for yourself; and if you would not love to be treated with that treatment, then you have neglected this great duty."
"91","[AI-translation] From Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah is pleased for you with three things and dislikes for you three things: He is pleased for you that you worship Him and associate nothing with Him, that you hold fast together to the rope of Allah and do not divide, and He dislikes for you idle gossip, excessive questioning, and wasting wealth."" Reported by Muslim.
In it is affirmation of pleasure for Allah and mention of what it relates to, and affirmation of dislike from Him and mention of what it relates to. For Allah, Glorified and Exalted be He, out of His generosity toward His servants, is pleased for them with what brings their benefit and their happiness in this world and the next. That is by establishing worship of Allah alone with no partner, and sincerity of religion to Him — by people holding to the beliefs and foundations of faith, the outward and inward laws of Islam, righteous deeds and pure character, all of it sincerely for Allah and in agreement with what pleases Him, upon the Sunnah of His Prophet — and by holding fast to the rope of Allah, which is His religion, the connection between Him and His servants, fulfilling it together united and cooperating in righteousness and piety: ""The Muslim is the brother of the Muslim; he does not wrong him, nor forsake him, nor lie to him, nor despise him."" Rather, he loves him sincerely and is a supportive brother to him. By this principle and the one before it the religion is completed, the blessing upon the Muslims perfected, and Allah gives them might and victory thereby, for their upholding all the means Allah commanded them by — which guarantee whoever upholds them victory and establishment, and immediate and deferred success and prosperity. Then he mentioned what Allah dislikes for His servants: idle gossip, of what contradicts these matters He loves and annuls them. Of them is much gossip: for it is among the motives of lying, lack of verification, and believing untruth; among the causes of trials befalling, hearts estranging, and occupation with harmful matters instead of beneficial ones. Seldom is one safe from any of that, when his desire is for gossip. As for his saying: «and excessive questioning» — this is blameworthy questioning, like asking of worldly things without need or necessity, asking captiously and contentiously, asking about matters whose harm is feared, or about matters in which there is no benefit — entering into His Almighty's saying: «O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made known to you, would harm you» (Qur'an Al-Ma'idah 5:101). As for asking about beneficial sciences by way of seeking or giving guidance, it is praiseworthy, commanded. And his saying: «and wasting wealth» — that is either by leaving its guarding until it is lost, or leaving it exposed to thieves and loss; or by neglecting the cultivation of his estate, or spending on his animal; or by spending wealth in harmful matters or non-beneficial ones. All this enters into wasting wealth. Or by entrusting it to the deficient in mind, like minors, the foolish, the insane, and their like; for Allah Almighty made wealth a support for people — by it their religious and worldly interests stand. The perfection of the blessing therein is that it be spent for what it was created for: of benefits and lawful matters, and worldly benefits. What Allah dislikes for His servants, He loves of them its opposite: He loves of them to be verifying in all they say, not to convey all they hear, to pursue truthfulness, to ask only about what benefits, to guard their wealth, manage it, dispose of it in beneficial disposals, and spend it in beneficial outlets. For this reason He Almighty said: «And do not give the foolish your wealth which Allah made for you a support» (Qur'an An-Nisa 4:5). And praise be to Allah first and last. And Allah knows best."
"92","[AI-translation] From 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: ""Hind bint 'Utbah, the wife of Abu Sufyan, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a miserly man; he does not give me of maintenance what suffices me and my children, except what I take from his wealth without his knowledge. Is there any sin upon me in that? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from his wealth in a customary manner what suffices you and suffices your children."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
The scholars have drawn much understanding from this hadith, and I shall point to what comes to mind of it. That the one seeking a ruling and the one seeking redress for injustice is permitted by the ruling on backbiting to speak truthfully about the one connected to the request for a ruling and the complaint, and it is not of the forbidden backbiting; it is one of the excepted cases of backbiting, and what unites them all is the need to speak about another. For forbidden backbiting is your mentioning your brother with what he dislikes; if there is need for that — as we have mentioned, and as in specific or general sincere advice, or when one is known only by his nickname — it is permitted to the extent that the purpose is achieved. From it also: that the maintenance of children is obligatory upon the father, and that he alone is responsible for it; neither the mother nor anyone else shares it with him. Likewise in it: the obligation of the wife's maintenance, and that its measure is sufficiency, due to his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""Take from his wealth in a customary manner what suffices you and suffices your children."" And that sufficiency is determined by custom, according to the circumstances of people: their time and place, their ease and hardship. And that when the spender withholds or is miserly with maintenance, whether in its basis or its completion, then the one entitled to maintenance, or the one directly undertaking spending, may take from his wealth even without his knowledge. That is because the cause is evident, and in this case it is not attributed to treachery, so it does not enter under his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): ""Do not betray the one who betrays you."" This is the sound middle view on the question of taking from the wealth of one against whom one has a right, without his knowledge, to the extent of one's right; it is the well-known view of the school of Imam Ahmad that this is not permitted except when the cause is evident, such as maintenance for the wife, children, slaves and the like, and the right of the guest. From it also: that one entrusted with an affair requiring monetary estimation has his word accepted in the estimation, for he is trusted and has authority over that thing. From it also: that when one seeking a ruling connected to another is predominantly thought by the one asked to be truthful, there is no need to bring that other party, especially when that would cause harm, as in this case: had Abu Sufyan been brought for this complaint, it would not be safe from something improper occurring between him and his wife. There is in this no evidence for judgment against an absent party, for this is not a judgment; it is only a request for a ruling. And Allah knows best."
"93","[AI-translation] From Abu Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""Let no one judge between two persons while he is angry."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
""Let no one judge between two persons while he is angry."" This hadith points to several matters. The first: the prohibition for a judge among people against judging in any specific case between two while angry, whether in religious or worldly cases. That is because of what anger involves in the disturbance and deviation of thought. This deviation of thought harms his recall of the truth and also harms his aiming at the truth, while the fundamental duty of the judge and others is aiming at the truth in knowledge and action. The second: it indicates that one should strive to adopt the means that repel anger or lighten it, such as cultivating forbearance and patience, and accustoming the soul to what befalls it and what it hears from litigants; for this is a great aid in repelling anger or lightening it. The third: taken from this reasoning is that everything that prevents a person from knowing the truth or aiming at it takes the ruling of anger. That includes intense worry, hunger and thirst, suppressing the urge to urinate or defecate, and the like, which occupy thought as much as anger or more. The fourth: that the prohibition of judging in the state of anger and the like is intended for something beyond itself. It is that the judge should not judge until he has encompassed knowledge of the general legal ruling, the particular case in all its aspects, and how to apply it to the legal ruling. For the judge needs these three matters. The first: knowledge of the legal methods that the Lawgiver laid down for settling disputes and judging among people. The second: that he understand the dispute between the two litigants and conceive it fully, letting each of them present his proof and explain his case fully. Then, when that is verified and he has encompassed it in knowledge, he needs the third matter: the manner of applying it and bringing it under the legal rulings. When he is granted success in these three matters and aims at justice, he is granted success and guided to it; and when one of them is missed, error occurs and the judgment is flawed. And Allah knows best."
"94","[AI-translation] From 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Eat, drink, dress and give charity, without extravagance or conceit."" Reported by Ahmad and Abu Dawud, and al-Bukhari cited it in suspended form.
This hadith covers the use of wealth in matters beneficial in religion and worldly life, and avoiding harmful matters. For Allah has made wealth a support for His servants; their private and public, religious and worldly affairs stand upon it. Allah and His Messenger have guided therein — in earning and using it, managing and spending it — to the best and most beneficial ways with the best outcome, in the present and in the end. Therein He guided to striving to earn it through permissible and beneficial means, that the seeking be honourable, with no laziness or languor in it, and no engrossment in earning it that impairs the person's condition, and that one avoid forbidden and base earnings. Then, when it is earned, a person strives to preserve it and use it in a customary manner, in food, drink, clothing and needed matters, for himself and those connected to him such as wife, children and others, without stinginess or wastefulness. Likewise, when he spends it on others, he spends it in ways that benefit him and whose reward and good remain for him, such as charity to the needy among relatives, neighbours and the like, and gifts and invitations customary among people. All of that is conditioned on the absence of extravagance and the aim of boasting and pride, as restricted in this hadith, and as in His saying: ""And those who, when they spend, are neither extravagant nor stingy, but hold a just balance between the two"" (Qur'an Al-Furqan 25:67). This is justice in managing wealth: that it be moderate between the ranks of miserliness and wastefulness; by that affairs stand and are completed. Anything besides this is sin and harm, and deficiency in intellect and condition. And Allah knows best."
"95","[AI-translation] From Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: ""It was said: O Messenger of Allah, have you seen the man who does a good deed and people praise him — or love him — for it? He said: That is the hastened glad tidings of the believer."" Reported by Muslim.
He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that the hastened traces of praiseworthy deeds are part of the glad tidings; for Allah has promised His beloved servants — the believing, God-fearing — glad tidings in this life and in the Hereafter. And ""glad tidings"" is the joyful news or matter by which the servant recognizes the goodness of his outcome, and that he is among the people of happiness, and that his deed is accepted. As for in the Hereafter, it is the glad tidings of Allah's pleasure and reward, and salvation from His wrath and punishment: at death, in the grave, and at rising to resurrection — Allah sends His believing servant in those places glad tidings at the hands of angels, as the texts of the Book and the Sunnah have abounded therein, and they are known. As for the glad tidings in this world which Allah makes for the believers — a sample and hastening of His bounty, making Himself known to them thereby, and quickening them to deeds — the most general of them is His granting them success for good and preserving them from evil, as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: «As for the people of happiness, they are eased toward the deeds of the people of happiness.» When the servant finds deeds of good eased for him, facilitated to him, and finds himself guarded by Allah's guarding from deeds that harm him, this is of the glad tidings by which the believer infers the outcome of his affair; for Allah is the Most Generous of the generous, the Most Bountiful of the bountiful. When He begins His servant with beneficence, He completes it. So the greatest favor and beneficence He favors him with is His religious beneficence. The believer rejoices therein with the most complete joy: joy at Allah's favor upon him of deeds of good and their facilitation — for among the greatest signs of faith is love of good, desire for it, and joy at doing it. And a second joy at his intense hope of Allah's completing His blessing upon him and the continuance of His bounty. Of that is what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in this hadith: when the servant does a deed of the deeds of good — especially lasting traces and charitable projects of general benefit — and there follows upon that people's love for him, their praise of him, and their supplication for him, this is of the glad tidings that this deed is of the accepted deeds, in which Allah placed good and blessing. Of the glad tidings in the life of this world is the believers' love for the servant, by His Almighty's saying: «Indeed those who believed and did righteous deeds — the Most Merciful shall make for them affection» (Qur'an Maryam 19:96) — i.e., love from Him for them, and endearing them in the hearts of servants. Of that is good praise: for the believers' abundant praise of a servant is testimony from them for him. And the believers are Allah's witnesses on His earth. Of that is the righteous dream a believer sees, or seen for him; for the righteous dream is of the bearers of glad tidings. Of glad tidings is that Allah decrees for a servant a decree he loves or dislikes, and makes that decree a means to reforming his religion and his safety from evil. And the kinds of the kindnesses of the Creator, glory be to Him, are countless, conceivable by no mind, nor turning in imagination. And Allah knows best."
"96","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The pleasure of Allah lies in the pleasure of the parents, and the wrath of Allah lies in the wrath of the parents."" Collected by al-Tirmidhi.
Ibn Hibban and al-Hakim graded it sahih. This hadith is evidence for the merit of dutifulness to parents and its obligation, and that it is a cause for the pleasure of Allah. And for warning against disobedience to parents and its prohibition, and that it is a cause for the wrath of Allah. There is no doubt that this is of Allah's mercy toward parents and children, given the bond between parents and their children unlike any other tie, the firm connection, the kindness of parents unmatched by the kindness of any other creature, the varied upbringing, and the religious and worldly need of children to fulfil this confirmed right — in repayment of the right, in earning reward, and in teaching their own offspring to treat them as they treated their parents. These causes and what branches from them require making their pleasure joined to Allah's pleasure, and its opposite to its opposite. If it is said: What is the dutifulness commanded by Allah and His Messenger? It is said: Allah and His Messenger have defined it with a known definition and an explanation understood by everyone. Allah stated the command to do good to them in general terms, and mentioned some examples that are models of kindness. So every kindness in word, deed or body, according to the circumstances of parents and children, time and place — that is dutifulness. In this hadith: mention of the utmost limit and end of dutifulness, which is the parents' pleasure: kindness is the cause and means, and pleasure is the effect and result. Everything that pleases the parents among all customary dealings, and following every path and means that pleases them, enters into dutifulness, just as disobedience is everything that angers them in word or deed. But that is restricted to obedience, not disobedience to Allah. When the child cannot please his parents except by angering Allah, he must give precedence to the love of Allah over the love of the parents, and the blame and fault lie with the parents; they blame none but themselves. In this hadith: affirmation of the attributes of pleasure and wrath for Allah, and that they relate to what He loves and is pleased with. Allah loves His beloved servants and His chosen ones, and loves whoever upholds obedience to Him and obedience to His Messenger; this is of His perfection, wisdom, praiseworthiness and mercy. His pleasure and wrath are of His attributes connected to His will and power. The safeguard in that is that the believer must affirm what Allah affirmed for Himself and what His Messenger affirmed for Him of perfect attributes of essence and action, in a manner befitting Allah's majesty, grandeur and glory, and know that Allah has no peer, no equal and no likeness in His essence, names, attributes and actions. And Allah knows best."
"97","[AI-translation] From Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Three things over which the heart of a Muslim never harbours treachery: sincerity of deed for Allah, sincere advice to those in authority, and keeping to the community of the Muslims, for their supplication encompasses those behind them."" Reported by al-Tirmidhi, al-Shafi'i and others.
Shaykh Shams al-Din Ibn al-Qayyim said: That is, no rancour remains in the heart, nor does it carry rancour along with these three; rather they remove its rancour, cleanse it of it and expel it from it. For the heart harbours the greatest rancour over polytheism, and likewise harbours rancour over deceit and over leaving the community of the Muslims through innovation and misguidance. These three fill it with rancour and corruption, and the remedy for this rancour and the extraction of its impurities is by purifying sincerity and sincere advice and following the Sunnah. End of his words. That is, whoever makes all his deeds sincerely for Allah, gives sincere advice in all his affairs to the servants of Allah, and keeps to the community through unity and absence of dissension, and his heart becomes clear and pure, becomes a beloved servant of Allah; and whoever is otherwise, his heart is filled with every affliction and evil. And Allah knows best."
"98","[AI-translation] From 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""People are like a hundred camels; you can hardly find among them a mount fit for riding."" Agreed upon (al-Bukhari and Muslim).
This hadith contains a true report and beneficial guidance. As for the report, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that deficiency covers most people, and that the perfect — or near-perfect — among them are few, like a hundred camels: you deem them many, yet when you seek among them a mount fit for carrying and riding, for going and returning, you can hardly find it. So too people are many, yet when you seek to select from them one fit for teaching, giving rulings, leading prayer, major or minor offices, or important posts, you can hardly find one who fulfils that post well. This is the reality, for man is unjust and ignorant, and injustice and ignorance are causes of deficiencies, which prevent perfection and bringing others to perfection. As for the guidance, the content of this report is guidance from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the community as a whole should strive and exert themselves in qualifying men fit to undertake important tasks and general matters of public benefit. Allah has guided to this meaning in His saying: ""Why should not a group from each party of them go forth to gain understanding in religion and warn their people when they return to them"" (Qur'an At-Tawbah 9:122). He commanded struggle in His cause, that a sufficient group undertake it, and that another group devote itself to knowledge, so that these support those and those support these. His commanding offices and appointments is a command for them and for what they are completed by of conditions and complements. Religious and worldly posts and collective works are indispensable for people, and their welfare is completed only through them; and they are completed only when undertaken by the competent and trustworthy, which calls for striving to attain these qualities as far as possible. Allah said: ""So fear Allah as much as you can"" (Qur'an At-Taghabun 64:16). And Allah knows best."
"99","[AI-translation] From Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There will come upon people a time when one who holds fast to his religion will be like one who holds burning coal."" Reported by al-Tirmidhi.
This hadith also comprises a report and guidance. As for the report, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that in the last times good and its causes will decrease and evil and its causes will increase, and that at that time those who hold to religion among people will be very few, and this few will be in a state of great hardship and difficulty, like the state of one holding burning coal — due to the strength of opponents, the multitude of misleading trials, trials of doubts, uncertainties and atheism, trials of desires and people's turning to worldly life and engrossment in it, outwardly and inwardly, weakness of faith, and severe isolation for lack of helper and supporter. But the one who holds to his religion and stands repelling these oppositions and obstacles, which none but people of insight and certainty and people of firm faith withstand, is of the best of creation, highest with Allah in rank and greatest with Him in worth. As for the guidance, it is guidance to his community to brace themselves for this state, to know that it is inevitable, and that whoever plunges through these obstacles and is patient over his religion and faith — despite these oppositions — has with Allah the highest ranks, and his Master will aid him in what He loves and is pleased with, for aid is according to the burden. How much our time resembles this description mentioned by him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): nothing remains of Islam but its name, nor of the Qur'an but its script; weak faith, divided hearts, scattered governments, enmities and hatreds distancing Muslims from one another, apparent and hidden enemies working secretly and openly to do away with religion, atheism and materialism sweeping with its foul current and surging waves elders and youth, propaganda for corrupting morals and destroying the last breath of life, then people's devotion to worldly ornaments so that they have become the extent of their knowledge and their greatest concern — for them they are pleased and angered — and a vile propaganda to make them renounce the Hereafter and devote themselves wholly to building worldly life and destroying religion, despising it and mocking its people and all attributed to it, and pride, boasting and arrogance in material civilizations built upon atheism, whose traces, sparks and evils the servants have witnessed. With these accumulated evils, surging waves, grievous calamities, and present and future dark trials — with these matters and others — you find the fulfilment of this hadith. But with that, the believer does not despair of Allah's mercy nor lose hope in relief from Allah, nor is his gaze confined to outward means; rather his heart is ever turned to the Causer of means, the Generous, the Bestower, with relief before his eyes and His promise, which He does not break, that He will grant him after hardship ease, that relief comes with distress, and that the dispelling of griefs comes with intense griefs and the descent of terrors. The believer is one who says in these states: ""There is no power nor strength except through Allah,"" and ""Allah suffices us, and He is the best Disposer of affairs. In Allah we trust. O Allah, to You is praise, to You is complaint, You are the One whose help is sought, and through You rescue is sought. There is no power nor strength except through Allah, the Most High, the Most Great."" He upholds what he can of faith, sincere advice and invitation, contents himself with little when much is not possible, and with the removal of some evil and its lightening when other than that is unattainable: ""And whoever fears Allah, He will make for him a way out — and whoever trusts in Allah, He suffices him — and whoever fears Allah, He will make for him ease in his affair"" (Qur'an At-Talaq 65:2-4). Praise be to Allah by whose blessing righteous deeds are completed, and may Allah bless Muhammad and his family, Companions and followers until the Day of Judgment.
The Conclusion. This treatise comprising commentary on ninety-nine hadiths of the comprehensive Prophetic hadiths, on kinds of sciences, beneficial topics, sound creeds, noble morals, jurisprudence and manners, comprehensive reforms and general benefits, is complete. So said its compiler: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nasir ibn 'Abdullah Al Sa'di. May Allah forgive him, his parents and theirs, and all Muslims. He finished it on the tenth of Sha'ban in the year 1371 of the Hijrah. The copying of it was completed with Allah's aid and facilitation from the author's handwriting on 27 Ramadan 1371 Hijrah, by the pen of the one needy of his Generous Lord, 'Abdullah ibn Sulayman Al 'Abdullah Al Salman. May Allah forgive him, his parents and theirs, and all Muslims. This is a precious jewel and a lush meadow; it is the quest of seekers, the delight of beneficiaries and the joy of beholders, for the elegant guise in which it has appeared and the flowers of knowledge and verification with which it is adorned, and for the weighty benefits deposited in it, easy to gather, the near fruits pleasant in taste, and the sweet springs pleasant to drink from, as it covers the exposition of beneficial creeds, comprehensive foundations, varied rulings, lofty manners and other important topics and abundant sciences that earn a person guidance and rectitude and increase him in insight and certainty. Suffice you of it that it is a commentary on words that are the noblest of words after Allah's words, the most comprehensive of them for good and most beneficial — the words of the most knowledgeable of creation and most eloquent of them, Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and an elucidation of his noble aims and precious treasures, presented by the virtuous shaykh 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nasir al-Sa'di — may Allah reward him for Islam and its people with good — and may the suns of his verification ever shine and the seeds of his knowledge ever be radiant."
